window.ENG_READING_BASIC = [{"no": 1, "passages": [{"title": "Part-Time Jobs on Campus", "text": "Many college students today take part-time jobs while they study. Some work in libraries, cafes, or shops near the campus. Others become teaching assistants or help with research projects. For most students, the main reason is money. A part-time job can help pay for books, meals, and daily costs. It can also reduce the pressure on their families. However, money is not the only reason. Many students want to learn new skills and gain work experience before they graduate. A job teaches them how to manage time, talk with different people, and solve problems at work. These skills are useful in almost every future career, from teaching to business and technology. At the same time, working too much can create problems. If a student works more than twenty hours a week, study time may become too short. Grades can fall, and the student may feel tired all the time. Some students also find it hard to balance work, classes, and social life, and they may miss important lessons. Experts suggest that students should choose jobs with flexible hours. A good job should fit around classes, not take the place of classes. Students should also choose work that matches their interests or future plans. For example, a student who wants to become a teacher can work as a tutor. A student who likes writing can work for the school newspaper. In this way, work becomes part of learning, not just a way to earn money. When students plan carefully, a part-time job can be a valuable part of college life. It gives them money, confidence, and real experience. The key is to keep a balance between work and study, and to remember that learning is still the main task at university. In addition, a part-time job can help students build a professional network. They meet managers, customers, and colleagues who may offer advice or recommend them for future jobs. Some students even find their first full-time job through contacts made at work. However, students should remember to protect their study time first. If work begins to affect grades, they should reduce their hours or find a different job.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the main reason most students take part-time jobs according to the passage?", "options": ["To make friends on campus", "To avoid attending classes", "To earn money for daily costs", "To become famous quickly"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：交朋友不是主要原因；B 错误：文章没有说学生想逃课；C 正确：文章指出多数学生打工是为了支付书本、饮食等费用并减轻家庭压力；D 错误：文章未提及成名。"}, {"prompt": "What other benefit of part-time jobs is mentioned in the passage?", "options": ["They guarantee a high-paying job after graduation", "They allow students to skip exams", "They make study unnecessary", "They help students learn new skills and gain experience"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：文章没有保证高薪工作；B 错误：文章没有说可以跳过考试；C 错误：文章强调学习仍是主要任务；D 正确：文章说打工能教时间管理、沟通和解决问题。"}, {"prompt": "What problem may happen if students work too many hours?", "options": ["They will certainly fail all courses", "They will lose all their friends", "They will be asked to leave school", "They may have less time to study and feel tired"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：文章没有说一定挂科；B 错误：文章未提失去朋友；C 错误：文章没有说会被开除；D 正确：工作超过二十小时会使学习时间变短、成绩下降并感到疲倦。"}, {"prompt": "What advice do experts give to students?", "options": ["Work as many hours as possible", "Choose the highest-paying job regardless of time", "Choose jobs with flexible hours that fit around classes", "Give up study and focus on work"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：文章反对工作过多；B 错误：文章建议选与兴趣相关、时间灵活的工作；C 正确：专家建议选择工时灵活、能配合课程的工作；D 错误：文章强调学习是主要任务。"}, {"prompt": "What does the author think is the key to a good part-time job experience?", "options": ["Working only for money", "Keeping a balance between work and study", "Choosing any job available", "Studying less and working more"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：赚钱不是唯一目的；B 正确：作者认为平衡工作与学习是关键；C 错误：文章建议选择与兴趣相关的工；D 错误：与文章观点相反。"}]}, {"title": "Learning a New Language Online", "text": "In the past, learning a new language usually meant going to a classroom and studying with a teacher. Today, many people learn languages online. They use apps, videos, and websites to study at home. Online learning has many advantages. First, it is flexible. Learners can study at any time, in the morning, at lunch, or before bed. They do not need to travel to school, which saves both time and money. Second, online courses often cost less than traditional classes. Many apps are free, and some offer short lessons that take only ten minutes a day. Third, learners can choose what to study. If they like travel, they can learn words about hotels and airports. If they need English for work, they can study business English. However, online learning also has some problems. It is easy to lose motivation when nobody checks your progress. Some learners start with great excitement but stop after a few weeks. Speaking is another difficulty. It is hard to practise speaking if you only listen and read on a screen. A language is a tool for communication, and learners need to talk with real people. To solve these problems, many learners join online study groups or find language partners. They practise speaking once or twice a week and help each other. Some apps also use artificial intelligence to correct pronunciation and give quick feedback. Experts say that online learning works best when learners set clear goals and study regularly. Even fifteen minutes a day is better than two hours once a week. Learning a language takes time, but with patience and regular practice, online learners can make real progress and enjoy the process. In addition, online learners can use many free resources to support their study. They can watch films with subtitles, listen to podcasts, and read news in the target language. These activities make learning more interesting and help learners hear natural speech. Some learners also keep a language diary, writing a few sentences every day about their life.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is one advantage of learning a language online?", "options": ["It requires travelling to school every day", "It is always more expensive than classroom learning", "It is flexible and learners can study at any time", "It needs no practice at all"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：在线学习不需要去学校；B 错误：在线课程通常更便宜；C 正确：灵活、随时学习是在线学习的优点；D 错误：学习需要练习。"}, {"prompt": "Why do some online learners stop studying after a few weeks?", "options": ["Because online courses are too short", "Because they lose motivation without anyone checking progress", "Because they have learned everything", "Because teachers force them to stop"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：课程短不是原因；B 正确：没有人督促时容易失去动力而放弃；C 错误：文章没有说已学会全部；D 错误：文章未提老师强迫停止。"}, {"prompt": "What difficulty about speaking is mentioned?", "options": ["Speaking is not important for language learning", "Online learners speak too much", "It is hard to practise speaking by only listening and reading", "All apps can replace real conversation"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：文章强调语言是交流工具；B 错误：文章没有说口语太多；C 正确：只听读难以练习口语，需要与真人交流；D 错误：文章没有说应用能完全代替真人对话。"}, {"prompt": "How can learners improve their speaking online?", "options": ["By never speaking at all", "By studying only once a month", "By avoiding all feedback", "By joining study groups or finding language partners"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：不说话无法提高；B 错误：每月一次太少；C 错误：反馈有助于学习；D 正确：加入小组或找语伴并定期练习可以提高口语。"}, {"prompt": "What do experts suggest for online language learning?", "options": ["Study two hours once a week only", "Learn without any goal", "Give up after one week", "Set clear goals and study regularly, even 15 minutes a day"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：文章说每天15分钟比一周一次两小时更好；B 错误：需要明确目标；C 错误：文章强调耐心和坚持；D 正确：专家建议定明确目标、每天坚持，哪怕15分钟。"}]}]}, {"no": 2, "passages": [{"title": "Smartphones and Sleep", "text": "Many people check their smartphones before going to bed. They read news, watch videos, or reply to messages. It feels relaxing, but scientists say this habit can harm sleep. The main problem is light. Smartphones give off blue light, which tells the brain that it is still daytime. As a result, the brain produces less melatonin, a chemical that helps people feel sleepy. When melatonin is low, it becomes harder to fall asleep. People may lie in bed for a long time, thinking and turning over. Another problem is excitement. A funny video or an interesting message can make the brain active. Even after the phone is put away, the mind may still be busy. This can reduce the quality of sleep. People may wake up in the night or feel tired the next morning. Poor sleep affects more than energy. It can hurt memory, attention, and mood. Students who sleep badly may find it hard to concentrate in class. Workers may make more mistakes. Over a long time, poor sleep can also increase the risk of health problems, including heart disease. To sleep better, experts give simple advice. First, stop using the phone at least thirty minutes before bed. Second, keep the phone away from the bed, or turn it to silent mode. Third, do something calm before sleep, such as reading a paper book or listening to soft music. Some people also use night mode, which reduces blue light, but experts say this is not enough. The best choice is to put the phone away. A good night of sleep helps the body and brain recover. Protecting sleep is a simple way to protect health. In addition, poor sleep can affect relationships and daily life. People who are tired may become impatient with family and friends, and they may find it hard to enjoy activities they normally like. Some people try to solve the problem with coffee or energy drinks, but these only hide tiredness for a short time. A better solution is to build a regular bedtime routine.", "questions": [{"prompt": "Why can smartphones make it hard to fall asleep?", "options": ["Their screens are too small", "They make the room too cold", "Their blue light reduces melatonin", "They are too heavy to hold"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：屏幕大小不是原因；B 错误：手机不会让房间变冷；C 正确：蓝光让大脑以为还是白天，减少褪黑素，难以入睡；D 错误：重量与睡眠无关。"}, {"prompt": "What else can harm sleep besides light?", "options": ["Phones make people too calm", "Exciting content makes the brain active", "Reading news helps people sleep at once", "Messages always make people sleepy"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：手机内容使人兴奋而非平静；B 正确：有趣视频或消息使大脑兴奋，影响睡眠质量；C 错误：文章没有说读新闻立即助眠；D 错误：消息不会让人犯困。"}, {"prompt": "What can poor sleep affect?", "options": ["Only the colour of the phone", "Only the price of the phone", "Nothing at all", "Memory, attention, mood and health"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：与手机颜色无关；B 错误：与价格无关；C 错误：文章列举了多方面影响；D 正确：睡眠差影响记忆、注意、情绪和健康。"}, {"prompt": "What advice is given for better sleep?", "options": ["Use the phone until falling asleep", "Keep the phone under the pillow", "Watch exciting videos in bed", "Stop using the phone at least 30 minutes before bed"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：用到睡着会伤害睡眠；B 错误：放枕头下不是建议；C 错误：兴奋视频会使大脑活跃；D 正确：睡前至少30分钟不用手机是专家的建议。"}, {"prompt": "What does the author think of night mode?", "options": ["It completely solves the problem", "It makes sleep worse than normal mode", "It helps a little but is not enough; putting the phone away is best", "It is never mentioned"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：文章说这还不够；B 错误：夜间模式能减少蓝光；C 正确：夜间模式能减少蓝光但不够，最好放下手机；D 错误：文章提到了夜间模式。"}]}, {"title": "Artificial Intelligence in the Classroom", "text": "Artificial intelligence, or AI, is now entering many classrooms. Teachers use AI tools to prepare lessons, check homework, and give feedback to students. Students use AI to look for information, practise languages, and understand difficult ideas. Supporters say AI can make learning more personal. For example, if a student is weak in maths, an AI program can give more practice in that area. If another student learns quickly, the program can offer harder questions. In this way, each student can learn at their own speed. AI can also save teachers time. Checking simple homework takes a long time, but AI can do it in seconds. Teachers can then spend more time talking with students and helping those who need extra support. However, there are worries too. Some teachers are afraid that students will use AI to cheat. If a student asks AI to write a whole essay, they may not learn anything. Others worry about privacy. AI programs need a lot of student data to work well. Who keeps this data, and how is it used? These questions need clear answers. Another worry is that AI cannot replace human care. A good teacher notices when a student is sad or stressed and offers encouragement. A machine cannot truly understand feelings. Experts believe the best future is not AI alone, but AI plus teachers. AI can handle repeated tasks and give quick feedback. Teachers can guide, inspire, and support students as people. Schools should teach students how to use AI wisely: to ask good questions, check answers, and think for themselves. When used in the right way, AI can be a helpful assistant in the classroom, not a replacement for teachers. In addition, AI can help students who learn in different ways. Some students learn best by reading, others by listening or by doing exercises. AI programs can offer videos, quizzes, and games to match these styles. Teachers can also use AI to find out which topics students find difficult and then spend more time on those topics in class. This makes teaching more effective. However, schools must also teach students about the limits of AI.", "questions": [{"prompt": "How can AI make learning more personal?", "options": ["By giving every student the same questions", "By stopping teachers from teaching", "By making all lessons shorter", "By giving different practice based on each student's level"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：相同题目不是个人化；B 错误：AI不是要阻止教师；C 错误：课时缩短不是个人化；D 正确：AI能根据学生水平提供不同练习，让每人按自己的速度学习。"}, {"prompt": "How can AI help teachers?", "options": ["By replacing all teachers", "By making teachers work longer hours", "By checking simple homework quickly so teachers have more time for students", "By stopping teachers from talking to students"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：文章说AI不能代替教师；B 错误：AI是节省时间而非增加工时；C 正确：AI快速批改简单作业，教师可多花时间与学生交流；D 错误：与文章观点相反。"}, {"prompt": "What worry about cheating is mentioned?", "options": ["AI always refuses to help students", "Students may ask AI to write a whole essay and learn nothing", "Cheating has become impossible", "Teachers want students to cheat"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：AI并不会拒绝帮助；B 正确：学生可能让AI代写作文而不学习；C 错误：文章没有说作弊不可能；D 错误：教师不希望学生作弊。"}, {"prompt": "What other worry is discussed?", "options": ["AI is too cheap to use", "Classrooms are too large", "Privacy of student data used by AI programs", "Students dislike all technology"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：价格不是担忧；B 错误：教室大小不是担忧；C 正确：AI需要大量学生数据，谁保管、如何使用需要明确；D 错误：文章没有说学生讨厌所有技术。"}, {"prompt": "What future do experts expect?", "options": ["AI alone without any teachers", "No AI in schools at all", "Teachers doing everything by hand forever", "AI plus teachers, with AI as an assistant not a replacement"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：文章反对只有AI；B 错误：文章支持合理使用AI；C 错误：文章认为AI可以处理重复任务；D 正确：专家认为最好是AI加教师，AI做助手而非代替。"}]}]}, {"no": 3, "passages": [{"title": "The Power of Walking", "text": "Walking is one of the simplest forms of exercise, but many people do not walk enough. They drive to work, take lifts, and sit for most of the day. Doctors say this lifestyle is bad for health. Walking regularly can bring many benefits. First, it is good for the heart. A brisk walk for thirty minutes a day can help lower blood pressure and improve blood flow. Second, walking helps control weight. It burns calories without putting too much pressure on the knees. Third, walking is good for the mind. Many people find that a walk helps them relax and think clearly. Some even get new ideas while walking, and writers often say walking helps them solve problems. Walking in nature is especially helpful. Trees, fresh air, and birdsong can reduce stress and improve mood. Studies show that people who walk in parks feel happier than those who walk on busy streets. Walking is also a social activity. Friends can walk together and talk. Older people can walk with family members and stay connected. Unlike running or swimming, walking needs no special equipment or training. Almost everyone can do it, and it costs nothing. The key is to make walking a habit. People can start with small changes: get off the bus one stop early, take the stairs instead of the lift, or walk to a nearby shop. A step counter on a phone can help people see their progress and stay motivated. Experts suggest ten thousand steps a day, but even five thousand is a good start. Walking is not just exercise. It is a simple way to take care of body and mind in daily life. In addition, walking can be a good way to explore a new city or a new campus. When students walk instead of taking a bus, they notice shops, parks, and interesting places they might otherwise miss. Walking with a friend can also turn exercise into a pleasant social time. Some people listen to music or podcasts while walking, which makes the time pass quickly.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?", "options": ["Walking is too difficult for most people", "Only running can improve health", "Walking regularly brings many health benefits", "Walking needs expensive equipment"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：文章说几乎人人能走；B 错误：文章没有说只有跑步有效；C 正确：文章主旨是经常步行带来许多健康益处；D 错误：步行不需要特殊设备。"}, {"prompt": "How does walking help the heart?", "options": ["It makes the heart stop working", "It has no effect on the heart", "It can lower blood pressure and improve blood flow", "It increases blood pressure greatly"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：步行不会让心脏停跳；B 错误：步行对心脏有益；C 正确：快走30分钟有助于降低血压、改善血液循环；D 错误：与文章相反。"}, {"prompt": "Why is walking in nature especially helpful?", "options": ["Nature makes people feel more tired", "Trees, fresh air and birdsong can reduce stress", "Parks are always crowded and noisy", "Walking in nature costs a lot of money"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：自然步行让人放松而非更累；B 正确：在自然中步行，树木、新鲜空气和鸟鸣能减轻压力、改善情绪；C 错误：文章说公园步行让人更快乐；D 错误：步行不花钱。"}, {"prompt": "What small change is suggested to walk more?", "options": ["Drive to every nearby shop", "Always take the lift", "Sit for the whole day", "Get off the bus one stop early or take the stairs"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：开车去附近商店减少步行；B 错误：乘电梯减少运动；C 错误：久坐有害健康；D 正确：提前一站下车或走楼梯是文中建议的小改变。"}, {"prompt": "What do experts suggest about daily steps?", "options": ["No steps are needed at all", "Only one hundred steps a day", "Walking should be avoided", "Ten thousand steps a day, but five thousand is a good start"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：文章鼓励多走；B 错误：100步太少；C 错误：文章提倡步行；D 正确：专家建议每天一万步，五千步也是好的开始。"}]}, {"title": "Why Breakfast Matters", "text": "Many students skip breakfast because they are busy or want to sleep longer. Some think skipping breakfast can help them lose weight. However, doctors and teachers say breakfast is the most important meal of the day. After a night of sleep, the body has used up much of its energy. Breakfast gives the body new fuel to start the day. Without it, people may feel tired, hungry, and unable to concentrate. Studies show that students who eat breakfast do better at school. They remember more, solve problems faster, and pay more attention in class. Students who skip breakfast often feel sleepy in the morning and find it hard to focus. They may also eat too much at lunch because they are very hungry. A good breakfast does not need to be large or expensive. It should include different kinds of food. For example, bread or rice gives energy. Milk, eggs, or beans give protein, which helps the body grow and repair itself. Fruit gives vitamins and fibre. A glass of milk, an egg, and a piece of fruit can make a simple and healthy breakfast. Some people eat only sweet food for breakfast, such as cakes or sweet drinks. This gives quick energy, but the energy disappears fast, and people feel hungry again soon. It is better to choose food that gives lasting energy, such as whole grains and protein. Eating breakfast is also a good habit for family life. When families eat together in the morning, they can talk and plan the day. Even ten minutes together can make the morning calmer and happier. Breakfast is a small choice, but it can affect the whole day. Starting the day with good food helps the body, the brain, and the mood. In addition, a good breakfast can help people control their weight. People who eat breakfast are less likely to feel very hungry later and eat too much at lunch or dinner. Skipping breakfast, by contrast, often leads to snacking on unhealthy food during the morning. Teachers also notice that students who eat breakfast are more active in class discussions and group work.", "questions": [{"prompt": "Why do many students skip breakfast?", "options": ["They dislike all food", "They are busy or want to sleep longer", "They have too much time", "They are told to by doctors"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：文章没有说讨厌所有食物；B 正确：许多学生因忙或想多睡而跳过早餐；C 错误：文章没有说时间太多；D 错误：医生提倡吃早餐。"}, {"prompt": "What happens to students who eat breakfast according to studies?", "options": ["They always feel sleepy", "They forget everything", "They do better at school and concentrate more", "They cannot solve problems"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：与研究相反；B 错误：吃早餐的学生记得更多；C 正确：吃早餐的学生记得更多、解题更快、注意力更集中；D 错误：与研究相反。"}, {"prompt": "What should a good breakfast include?", "options": ["Only sweet cakes and drinks", "Only water", "Different kinds of food such as bread, protein and fruit", "Nothing at all"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：只吃甜食能量消失快；B 错误：只喝水不够；C 正确：好早餐应包含面包、蛋白质、水果等多种食物；D 错误：不吃早餐不好。"}, {"prompt": "Why is only sweet food not a good choice?", "options": ["It gives lasting energy all day", "It is too expensive for everyone", "It has no taste at all", "It gives quick energy that disappears fast"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：与事实相反；B 错误：价格不是原因；C 错误：甜食有味道；D 正确：只吃甜食能量短暂，很快又会饿。"}, {"prompt": "What is the author's main advice?", "options": ["Skip breakfast to lose weight", "Eat a large lunch instead", "Never eat in the morning", "Start the day with a good breakfast"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：跳过早餐不能健康减肥；B 错误：午餐吃太多不是建议；C 错误：与作者观点相反；D 正确：作者建议以好的早餐开始一天。"}]}]}, {"no": 4, "passages": [{"title": "Recycling on Campus", "text": "Every day, a university produces a large amount of waste. Paper, plastic bottles, food boxes, and old clothes are thrown away. Much of this waste can be recycled, but many students do not know how to do it correctly. Recycling is important because it saves resources and protects the environment. When paper is recycled, fewer trees need to be cut down. When plastic bottles are recycled, less oil is used to make new plastic. Recycling also reduces waste in landfills, where rubbish can pollute soil and water. Many universities have started recycling programs. They place different bins for paper, plastic, and food waste. Some schools give prizes to dormitories that recycle the most. Others organise activities to teach students about recycling. However, these programs face problems. The biggest problem is confusion. Many students are not sure which bin to use. A coffee cup may look like paper, but it often has a plastic cover inside and cannot be recycled as paper. Food waste in a paper bin can also make the whole bin useless. To solve this problem, schools need clear signs and simple instructions. Pictures on bins can help students decide quickly. Short videos and posters can explain common mistakes. Students can also help each other. Senior students can teach new students how to sort waste in the first week of school. Recycling is not only the school's job. Each student can take small actions. Bringing a reusable bottle, using both sides of paper, and sorting waste correctly all make a difference. If everyone on campus recycles a little more each day, the campus will become cleaner and greener, and students will learn a habit they can keep for life. In addition, recycling can save money for the university. When less waste goes to landfills, the school pays less for waste disposal. Some recycled materials can even be sold, and the money can be used for student activities. Recycling also teaches students about responsibility. When they sort waste every day, they begin to think about what they buy and throw away.", "questions": [{"prompt": "Why is recycling important?", "options": ["It makes waste increase quickly", "It uses more trees and oil", "It saves resources and protects the environment", "It pollutes soil and water"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：回收减少垃圾；B 错误：回收节约树木和石油；C 正确：回收节约资源、保护环境，减少填埋污染；D 错误：回收是减少污染。"}, {"prompt": "What do many universities do to promote recycling?", "options": ["They remove all bins from campus", "They place different bins and organise teaching activities", "They forbid students to recycle", "They throw all waste together"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：学校是增加分类桶；B 正确：大学设置分类垃圾桶并组织教学活动推广回收；C 错误：学校鼓励回收；D 错误：学校提倡分类。"}, {"prompt": "What is the biggest problem for recycling programs?", "options": ["There is too little waste on campus", "Students recycle too much", "Students are confused about which bin to use", "Bins are too easy to understand"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：校园垃圾很多；B 错误：回收太多不是问题；C 正确：最大问题是学生不清楚该用哪个桶；D 错误：标识不清才是问题。"}, {"prompt": "Why can a coffee cup be a problem?", "options": ["It is always made of pure paper", "It can be recycled in any bin", "It never causes any problem", "It looks like paper but often has a plastic cover inside"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：咖啡杯常含塑料层；B 错误：不能随便放任何桶；C 错误：咖啡杯会造成分类困难；D 正确：咖啡杯看似纸质，内层常有塑料，不能按纸回收。"}, {"prompt": "What can individual students do?", "options": ["Throw all waste in one bin", "Use only one side of paper", "Never recycle at all", "Bring a reusable bottle and sort waste correctly"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：应分类投放；B 错误：应双面用纸；C 错误：应积极回收；D 正确：带可重复使用的瓶子、正确分类都是个人可做的事。"}]}, {"title": "The Problem of Plastic Waste", "text": "Plastic is everywhere in our lives. We use plastic bags, bottles, cups, and boxes every day. Plastic is cheap, light, and easy to make. However, it has become a serious environmental problem. Most plastic does not break down quickly. A plastic bottle can stay in nature for hundreds of years. When plastic waste is thrown into rivers and seas, it harms animals. Sea turtles may eat plastic bags because they look like jellyfish. Birds may feed plastic pieces to their babies. Even small pieces of plastic, called microplastics, have been found in fish and in drinking water. People are now trying to reduce plastic use. Many countries have banned single-use plastic bags or charge money for them. Shops encourage customers to bring their own bags. Some cities have stopped using plastic straws. Companies are also looking for better materials, such as paper, bamboo, or biodegradable plastic that breaks down faster. However, changing habits is not easy. Plastic is convenient, and many people are used to it. When people shop online, goods often arrive with a lot of plastic packaging. Takeaway food also uses many plastic boxes and bags. To reduce plastic waste, everyone needs to act. We can carry a reusable bag and bottle, say no to plastic straws, and choose products with less packaging. We can also sort plastic waste correctly so it can be recycled. Small actions may seem unimportant, but when millions of people do them every day, the effect is large. Protecting the environment starts with reducing the plastic we use. In addition, plastic waste affects people who live near the sea. Fishermen find plastic in their nets, and tourists are less likely to visit beaches covered with rubbish. This can hurt local jobs in fishing and tourism. Governments, companies, and citizens must work together to solve the problem. Governments can make laws, companies can design better packaging, and citizens can change their shopping habits. Schools can also teach children about plastic and encourage them to bring reusable bottles. When young people learn to reduce plastic early, they will carry that habit into adult life.", "questions": [{"prompt": "Why has plastic become a serious problem?", "options": ["Plastic is too expensive to make", "Most plastic does not break down quickly and harms nature", "Plastic disappears in one day", "Plastic is never used by people"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：塑料便宜易制作；B 正确：多数塑料难以快速分解，会在自然中存留数百年；C 错误：塑料不会一天消失；D 错误：塑料被广泛使用。"}, {"prompt": "How does plastic harm sea animals?", "options": ["Sea animals like eating plastic", "Plastic helps animals grow", "Turtles may eat bags that look like jellyfish", "Plastic cleans the sea"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：动物不是喜欢吃塑料；B 错误：塑料危害动物；C 正确：海龟会把塑料袋当作水母吃掉，鸟会喂幼鸟塑料碎片；D 错误：塑料污染海洋。"}, {"prompt": "What are many countries doing about plastic bags?", "options": ["They give free plastic bags to everyone", "They encourage more plastic use", "They have banned single-use bags or charge for them", "They do nothing at all"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：与政策相反；B 错误：国家在减少塑料使用；C 正确：许多国家禁用一次性塑料袋或对其收费；D 错误：文章列举了多项措施。"}, {"prompt": "Why is changing habits difficult?", "options": ["Plastic is very hard to find", "People dislike convenience", "Online shopping uses no packaging", "Plastic is convenient and people are used to it"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：塑料随处可见；B 错误：人们喜欢方便；C 错误：网购使用大量包装；D 正确：塑料方便且人们已习惯，网购和外卖也使用大量塑料包装。"}, {"prompt": "What does the author suggest we do?", "options": ["Use more single-use plastic", "Throw plastic into rivers", "Ignore the problem completely", "Carry reusable bags and choose products with less packaging"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：应减少一次性塑料；B 错误：向河里扔塑料会污染环境；C 错误：作者呼吁人人行动；D 正确：作者建议带可重复使用的袋子、选择少包装产品。"}]}]}, {"no": 5, "passages": [{"title": "Reading in the Digital Age", "text": "Today, people read more words than ever before, but they read in a different way. They read messages, posts, and short articles on phones. They often read quickly, jumping from one thing to another. Some people worry that this habit is killing deep reading. Deep reading means reading slowly, thinking about the ideas, and connecting them with what you already know. It is important for learning and for understanding complex problems. When people only read short pieces, they may find it hard to focus on a long book or article. They may also remember less. However, digital reading is not all bad. The internet gives people access to many books and articles for free. A student in a small town can read the same books as a student in a big city. E-books are easy to carry, and readers can look up new words quickly. Online reading can also be social. Readers can share ideas, leave comments, and discuss books with people around the world. The key is to find a balance. Experts suggest setting aside time for deep reading every day, even if it is only twenty minutes. Turn off notifications, find a quiet place, and read a book or a long article without interruption. Keep a notebook nearby to write down ideas or questions. It is also helpful to read different kinds of texts: news, stories, science, and history. Different reading builds different skills. Reading is still one of the best ways to learn. Whether on paper or on a screen, the habit of reading carefully and thinking deeply will always be valuable. In addition, digital tools can help readers organise what they learn. Readers can highlight important sentences, save articles to read later, and search for ideas they have read before. These tools are useful, but they should not replace thinking. A good reader pauses to ask questions: Do I agree with the writer? Is the evidence strong? What does this mean for my own life? Asking such questions turns reading into active learning.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What change in reading is described?", "options": ["People never read any words now", "People only read paper books", "People read quickly on phones, jumping from one thing to another", "People have stopped using phones"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：人们读的字比以前更多；B 错误：人们多在手机上阅读；C 正确：如今人们多在手机上快速阅读，常从一处跳到另一处；D 错误：手机阅读很普遍。"}, {"prompt": "What is deep reading?", "options": ["Reading as fast as possible", "Reading slowly, thinking about ideas and connecting them", "Skipping all difficult parts", "Reading only headlines"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：快读不是深读；B 正确：深读指慢慢读、思考观点并与已知知识联系；C 错误：跳过难点不是深读；D 错误：只读标题不是深读。"}, {"prompt": "What is an advantage of digital reading?", "options": ["It makes books more expensive", "It stops people from sharing ideas", "It gives free access to many books and articles", "It makes reading impossible"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：数字阅读让书更易获得；B 错误：在线阅读可以分享讨论；C 正确：互联网让人们免费获得许多书籍文章；D 错误：数字阅读方便阅读。"}, {"prompt": "What do experts suggest for deep reading?", "options": ["Read only while chatting with friends", "Always read with notifications on", "Never read long articles", "Set aside 20 minutes daily, turn off notifications, read without interruption"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：聊天会打断阅读；B 错误：应关闭通知；C 错误：应读长文章；D 正确：专家建议每天留出时间深读，关掉通知、找安静处不间断阅读。"}, {"prompt": "What is the author's main message?", "options": ["Stop reading completely", "Only read short messages", "Reading is no longer valuable", "Find a balance and keep the habit of careful, deep reading"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：作者鼓励阅读；B 错误：只读短消息不够；C 错误：作者说阅读仍是最好的学习方式之一；D 正确：作者主张找到平衡，保持认真深读的习惯。"}]}, {"title": "Museums for Young People", "text": "Many young people think museums are boring. They imagine quiet rooms, old objects, and long texts that are hard to understand. However, modern museums are changing. They want to attract young visitors and make history, art, and science interesting for everyone. Many museums now use technology to help visitors learn. Visitors can scan a code with their phones and watch a short video about an object. Some museums use virtual reality to let visitors walk through ancient cities or see dinosaurs. Others have touch screens where visitors can play games and answer questions. These activities make learning fun and active, not just looking and reading. Museums also organise special events for young people. They hold night openings, concerts, workshops, and talks by artists and scientists. Some museums invite students to create their own exhibitions. When young people take part in making an exhibition, they learn more and feel proud of their work. Visiting a museum can also be a social activity. Friends can go together, take photos, and discuss what they see. Schools often organise museum trips to help students understand what they learn in class. Seeing a real object is different from seeing a picture in a book. A visit can make history feel real and science feel exciting. Of course, museums still need to improve. Ticket prices can be high for students, and some museums are far from city centres. Many museums now offer free days or student discounts to solve this problem. They also share exhibitions online so people can visit from home. A museum is not just a place to keep old things. It is a place to learn, to think, and to enjoy culture with others. In addition, museums can help students with their schoolwork in a lively way. A history lesson becomes more interesting when students can see real clothes, tools, and photos from the past. A science lesson becomes clearer when students can touch models and try experiments. Many museums now work with schools to design visits that match the curriculum. Teachers can prepare students before a visit and discuss what they learned afterwards.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What do many young people think about museums?", "options": ["They are the most exciting places", "They are boring with quiet rooms and hard texts", "They have no old objects", "They are only for old people"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：许多年轻人觉得博物馆无聊；B 正确：许多年轻人认为博物馆无聊、安静、文字难懂；C 错误：博物馆有许多古物；D 错误：文章没有说只适合老人。"}, {"prompt": "How do modern museums use technology?", "options": ["Museums have removed all technology", "Visitors are not allowed to use phones", "Visitors can watch videos, use VR and play games on screens", "Technology makes museums more boring"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：博物馆在增加技术；B 错误：博物馆鼓励扫码；C 正确：现代博物馆用扫码视频、虚拟现实和触屏游戏帮助参观者学习；D 错误：技术让学习更有趣。"}, {"prompt": "What special events do museums organise?", "options": ["Only silent visits", "No events at all", "Night openings, concerts, workshops and talks", "Only ticket selling"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：活动丰富多样；B 错误：文章列举了多项活动；C 正确：博物馆举办夜间开放、音乐会、工作坊和讲座；D 错误：不只是卖票。"}, {"prompt": "Why can a museum visit help students?", "options": ["Pictures in books are always better", "Museum trips stop learning", "Students learn nothing from visits", "Seeing real objects makes history and science feel real"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：实物与书中图片不同；B 错误：博物馆参观有助于学习；C 错误：参观能学到东西；D 正确：看到真实物品能让历史真实、科学令人兴奋。"}, {"prompt": "How do museums solve the problem of high ticket prices?", "options": ["They raise prices even higher", "They close on free days", "They never offer discounts", "They offer free days or student discounts"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：提高价格会加重问题；B 错误：免费日是解决办法；C 错误：文章说许多博物馆提供折扣；D 正确：博物馆提供免费日或学生折扣。"}]}]}, {"no": 6, "passages": [{"title": "Life in a University Dormitory", "text": "For many students, living in a dormitory is their first experience of living away from home. They share a small room with two or three roommates, use public bathrooms, and eat in the canteen. At first, this life can be difficult. Different people have different habits. One student likes to sleep early, while another likes to study late at night. One likes quiet, while another likes music. If roommates do not talk about these differences, small problems can become big arguments. However, dormitory life also teaches important lessons. Students learn to respect others, share space, and solve problems together. They learn to do simple things by themselves, such as washing clothes, cleaning the room, and managing money. These skills help them become independent adults. Dormitory life can also be fun. Roommates often become close friends. They study together, share food, and help each other when they are sad or stressed. On holidays, they may cook together or watch films late at night. Many graduates say their best memories of university come from dormitory life. To live well in a dormitory, students need clear rules. They should agree on bedtime, cleaning duties, and quiet hours. They should also respect each other's belongings and privacy. When problems appear, talking calmly is better than keeping silent or getting angry. A good dormitory is not just a place to sleep. It is a small community where students learn how to live with others, and those lessons stay with them long after graduation. In addition, dormitory life teaches students how to handle money and time. With a fixed amount of living expenses each month, students must plan what to spend on food, transport, and daily needs. They also learn to plan their day, balancing classes, homework, and rest. Roommates can help each other by sharing study notes, reminding each other of deadlines, and cooking simple meals together. These small acts of cooperation make dormitory life warmer. Many students say that learning to live with others was as important as what they learned in class, because it prepared them for work and adult life.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is often difficult at first in dormitory life?", "options": ["Living alone in a large house", "Different habits among roommates", "Having too much private space", "Eating at home every day"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：宿舍是合住小房间；B 正确：室友习惯不同，如作息和音乐偏好不同，易产生矛盾；C 错误：宿舍空间小而非太大；D 错误：宿舍学生在食堂吃饭。"}, {"prompt": "What can students learn from dormitory life?", "options": ["How to avoid all other people", "How to spend money without limits", "How to respect others and become independent", "How to study without any help"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：宿舍生活是与人相处；B 错误：文章说要学会管理钱；C 正确：宿舍生活教会尊重他人、共享空间、独立生活；D 错误：室友会互相帮助。"}, {"prompt": "Why do many graduates remember dormitory life fondly?", "options": ["Because dormitories are always quiet", "Because they never had any problems", "Because they lived alone", "Because roommates often become close friends and share good times"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：宿舍并不总是安静；B 错误：宿舍生活也会有问题；C 错误：宿舍是合住；D 正确：室友常成为密友，一起学习、分享食物、互相帮助，留下美好回忆。"}, {"prompt": "What should roommates agree on?", "options": ["Nothing at all", "Bedtime, cleaning duties and quiet hours", "Only the price of food", "Only exam dates"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：需要明确规则；B 正确：室友应就睡觉时间、清洁分工和安静时间达成一致；C 错误：不只是食物价格；D 错误：不只是考试日期。"}, {"prompt": "What is the author's view of a dormitory?", "options": ["It is only a place to sleep", "It is a place to avoid people", "It is a small community for learning to live with others", "It is useless for students"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：宿舍不只是睡觉的地方；B 错误：宿舍生活需要与人相处；C 正确：宿舍是一个学习与人相处的小社区；D 错误：宿舍生活有重要意义。"}]}, {"title": "Joining a Student Club", "text": "At university, joining a student club is one of the best ways to make friends and learn new skills. There are many kinds of clubs: sports clubs, music clubs, reading clubs, and volunteer clubs. Some students join a club because they love an activity. Others join because they want to try something new. Whatever the reason, clubs give students a chance to meet people who share their interests. Clubs also help students develop abilities that classes cannot teach. For example, organising an event teaches planning and teamwork. Leading a club teaches responsibility and communication. Even a small task, such as designing a poster, can teach creativity and patience. These experiences are useful when students look for jobs later. Employers often like students who have been active in clubs, because it shows they can work with others. However, students should not join too many clubs at once. If they join five clubs, they may have no time to study. It is better to choose one or two clubs and take part seriously. Students should also choose clubs that match their time and energy. A club that meets every day may be too much for a busy student. Finally, clubs are not only about skills. They are also about fun and friendship. In a club, students can relax after classes, laugh with friends, and feel part of the university. For new students, a club can make a large campus feel smaller and warmer. Choosing the right club is a small decision, but it can make university life much richer. In addition, clubs can help students discover talents they did not know they had. A student who joins a photography club may find a love for taking pictures. A student who joins a debate club may discover a talent for public speaking. These discoveries can influence future study and career choices. Clubs also give students a chance to take responsibility. Organising a charity event or leading a team teaches leadership in a real situation. Even if the event is small, the experience builds confidence. For many graduates, club memories are among the happiest parts of university life.", "questions": [{"prompt": "Why do students join clubs?", "options": ["To avoid all classes", "To stay alone all the time", "To make university life boring", "To meet people with similar interests and learn new skills"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：社团不是逃课；B 错误：社团是社交活动；C 错误：社团让大学生活更丰富；D 正确：社团让学生结识有共同兴趣的人并学习新技能。"}, {"prompt": "What can organising an event teach?", "options": ["Nothing at all", "Planning and teamwork", "How to avoid people", "How to skip study"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：组织活动能学到东西；B 正确：组织活动能教规划和团队合作；C 错误：组织活动需要与人合作；D 错误：社团不应耽误学习。"}, {"prompt": "Why do employers like students active in clubs?", "options": ["Because club members never study", "Because employers dislike teamwork", "Because it shows they can work with others", "Because clubs replace degrees"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：社团成员也需要学习；B 错误：雇主喜欢团队合作；C 正确：积极参加社团表明学生能与他人合作；D 错误：社团不能代替学位。"}, {"prompt": "What advice is given about the number of clubs?", "options": ["Join as many as possible", "Join none at all", "Join five at the same time", "Choose one or two and take part seriously"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：加入太多会没时间学习；B 错误：参加社团有益；C 错误：加入五个太多；D 正确：最好选一两个社团认真参加。"}, {"prompt": "How can a club help new students?", "options": ["It makes them feel more lonely", "It can make a large campus feel smaller and warmer", "It stops them from making friends", "It makes campus larger"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：社团帮助交友；B 正确：社团能让大校园感觉更小更温暖；C 错误：社团促进交友；D 错误：社团让校园感觉更亲切。"}]}]}, {"no": 7, "passages": [{"title": "Social Media and Friendship", "text": "Social media has changed the way young people make and keep friends. With a phone, students can talk to friends at any time, share photos, and join group chats. Even friends who live far away can stay in touch every day. For many students, social media makes friendship easier and more fun. However, some people worry that online friendship is not the same as real friendship. On social media, people often show only the best parts of their lives. They post happy photos and exciting news, but they hide sadness and problems. This can make others feel that their own lives are boring or unhappy. Some students spend hours looking at posts and feel worse about themselves. Another problem is that online talk is quick and short. People send emojis and short messages, but they may not have deep conversations. Real friendship needs time, listening, and face-to-face talk. A friend who sits with you when you are sad is more important than a hundred likes online. This does not mean social media is bad. It is a useful tool when used wisely. Students can use it to plan meetings, share useful information, and keep in touch with old friends. The key is balance. Experts suggest spending less time scrolling and more time meeting friends in person. Put the phone away during meals, talk with friends face to face, and listen carefully. True friendship grows through real time together, not only through screens. Social media can help friendship, but it cannot replace it. In addition, social media can be useful for keeping in touch with friends who move away. When classmates graduate and go to different cities, group chats help them share news and support each other. Old friends can celebrate birthdays online and plan reunions. However, online contact should be a bridge to real meetings, not a replacement for them. When friends meet in person, they can read body language, hear tone of voice, and share experiences more fully. A balanced use of social media keeps friendships strong across distance while still valuing face-to-face time together.", "questions": [{"prompt": "How has social media changed friendship?", "options": ["Friends can never talk anymore", "Social media has removed all friends", "Friends can stay in touch every day even far away", "People no longer use phones"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：社交媒体帮助人们交流；B 错误：社交媒体帮助保持联系；C 正确：有了手机，学生可随时与朋友聊天、分享照片，即使相隔很远也能每天联系；D 错误：人们广泛使用手机。"}, {"prompt": "What problem is caused by people showing only the best parts of life?", "options": ["Everyone feels happier at once", "People stop posting photos", "Friendship becomes stronger at once", "Others may feel their own lives are boring or unhappy"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：只展示美好会让人比较后更失落；B 错误：人们仍在发照片；C 错误：这会损害而非增强友谊；D 正确：人们只展示生活最好的一面，会让他人觉得自己的生活无聊或不开心。"}, {"prompt": "Why is online talk sometimes not enough?", "options": ["Online talk is always very deep", "It is quick and short and lacks deep conversation", "People never send messages online", "Emojis replace all feelings perfectly"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：网上聊天常简短；B 正确：网上聊天快速简短，缺少深入对话，而真正的友谊需要时间、倾听和面对面交流；C 错误：人们经常发消息；D 错误：表情不能完全代替真实感受。"}, {"prompt": "What does the author think is more important than likes online?", "options": ["A hundred likes", "More followers", "A friend who sits with you when you are sad", "Faster phones"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：点赞不如真人陪伴；B 错误：粉丝数不是重点；C 正确：伤心时陪伴你的朋友比网上的一百个点赞更重要；D 错误：手机速度无关。"}, {"prompt": "What advice is given?", "options": ["Spend all day scrolling", "Never meet friends face to face", "Use the phone during every meal", "Spend less time scrolling and more time meeting friends in person"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：应减少刷屏时间；B 错误：应面对面见朋友；C 错误：吃饭时应放下手机；D 正确：专家建议少刷屏、多面对面见朋友，吃饭时放下手机、认真倾听。"}]}, {"title": "Working from Home", "text": "In recent years, more and more people have started working from home. They use computers and the internet to do their jobs without going to an office. For many workers, this change brings clear benefits. First, they save time. Without a long journey to work, they can sleep longer and spend more time with family. Second, they save money on transport and food. Third, they can work in a comfortable place and choose their own working hours in some jobs. However, working from home is not perfect. Many workers find it hard to focus at home. There may be noise, children, or housework that interrupts them. Some people also feel lonely because they do not see their colleagues every day. They miss chatting, sharing ideas, and working as a team. Another problem is the line between work and life. When the office is at home, it is hard to stop working. Some people answer emails late at night and feel tired all the time. To work well from home, experts give several tips. Workers should have a fixed place to work, even if it is only a small desk. They should keep regular hours and take breaks, just as they do in an office. They should also talk with colleagues online and meet in person sometimes. Companies can help by giving clear tasks and trusting workers. Working from home is likely to stay popular in the future. If workers and companies learn to manage its problems, it can be a good way to work. In addition, working from home can help the environment. Fewer people travelling to offices means less traffic and less pollution. Companies can also save money on office space and electricity. However, companies need to support home workers with good technology and clear communication. Regular video meetings, shared documents, and clear goals help teams stay connected. Workers should also set boundaries with family members, so that work time is respected. When both workers and companies make an effort, working from home can be productive, comfortable, and good for work-life balance.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is a benefit of working from home?", "options": ["Having to travel longer", "Saving time and money on transport", "Spending more on food", "Working in a noisy office"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：在家工作不用长途通勤；B 正确：在家工作节省通勤时间和交通、饮食费用；C 错误：在家工作节省开支；D 错误：在家工作不在办公室。"}, {"prompt": "What problem do many home workers face?", "options": ["They have too many colleagues around", "They never feel lonely", "They find it hard to focus because of noise and housework", "They always stop work on time"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：在家工作见不到同事；B 错误：有人会感到孤独；C 正确：家中噪音、孩子和家务会打断工作，难以集中注意力；D 错误：有人难以停止工作。"}, {"prompt": "Why do some home workers feel lonely?", "options": ["Because they have too many meetings", "Because they dislike working alone", "Because they never use the internet", "Because they do not see colleagues every day"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：会议多不是孤独原因；B 错误：孤独源于缺少同事交流；C 错误：他们使用网络工作；D 正确：因为每天见不到同事，缺少聊天和团队合作而感到孤独。"}, {"prompt": "What problem about work and life is mentioned?", "options": ["Work and life are always clearly separate", "It is hard to stop working when the office is at home", "Home workers never work at night", "Home workers have no emails"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：在家工作时界限模糊；B 正确：当办公室就在家里时，很难停止工作，有人深夜回邮件并感到疲倦；C 错误：有人深夜回邮件；D 错误：在家工作者会收邮件。"}, {"prompt": "What tip is given for working well from home?", "options": ["Work in bed all day", "Never take breaks", "Have a fixed place to work and keep regular hours", "Avoid talking to colleagues"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：在床上工作不利于专注；B 错误：应定时休息；C 正确：应有固定工作地点、保持规律作息并休息；D 错误：应与同事保持联系。"}]}]}, {"no": 8, "passages": [{"title": "Exercise and Mental Health", "text": "When people feel stressed or sad, they often think they need to rest. However, doctors say exercise can be one of the best ways to feel better. Moving the body helps the brain produce chemicals that improve mood. After a run, a swim, or even a walk, many people feel calmer and more positive. Exercise also helps people sleep better, and good sleep improves mood the next day. Research shows that regular exercise can reduce symptoms of anxiety and depression. It does not need to be hard exercise. Thirty minutes of moderate activity, such as brisk walking, cycling, or dancing, can make a difference. The key is regularity, not intensity. Doing a little every day is better than doing a lot once a month. Exercise also gives people a sense of control. When life feels difficult, finishing a run or learning a new sport can make people feel strong and confident. Group exercise has extra benefits. Playing football or joining a dance class helps people meet friends and feel supported. For students, exercise can also help study. A short walk before studying can improve focus and memory. However, exercise is not a cure for all mental health problems. People with serious problems should still talk to a doctor or counsellor. Exercise is a helpful part of a healthy life, not a replacement for professional help. The best advice is simple: move more, sit less, and find an activity you enjoy. When exercise becomes a habit, both body and mind become stronger. In addition, exercise can help people build a daily routine that supports mental health. When people exercise at the same time each day, they create structure in their lives, which can reduce feelings of chaos and worry. Exercise outdoors has extra benefits, because fresh air and sunlight can improve mood. Even a short walk in a park after lunch can refresh the mind for afternoon study or work. Friends and family can encourage each other to stay active. A simple invitation to walk together can be the first step towards a healthier and happier life for everyone involved.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What does exercise help the brain do?", "options": ["Stop all thinking", "Make people feel more stressed", "Produce chemicals that harm health", "Produce chemicals that improve mood"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：运动不会停止思考；B 错误：运动减轻压力；C 错误：运动有益健康；D 正确：运动帮助大脑产生改善情绪的化学物质，让人更平静积极。"}, {"prompt": "What kind of exercise is enough according to the passage?", "options": ["Only very hard exercise every day", "No exercise at all", "Thirty minutes of moderate activity such as brisk walking", "One hard workout once a month"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：不需要高强度运动；B 错误：运动有益；C 正确：每天30分钟快走、骑车或跳舞等中等强度活动就能起作用；D 错误：每月一次不够。"}, {"prompt": "Why is regularity important?", "options": ["Exercise once a year is enough", "Doing a little every day is better than a lot once a month", "Intensity matters more than regularity", "Regular exercise has no effect"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：一年一次不够；B 正确：关键是规律而非强度，每天做一点比每月一次大量运动更好；C 错误：规律比强度更重要；D 错误：规律运动有效。"}, {"prompt": "What extra benefit does group exercise have?", "options": ["It makes people feel lonely", "It stops people from exercising", "It is always too expensive", "It helps people meet friends and feel supported"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：团体运动促进社交；B 错误：团体运动鼓励运动；C 错误：文章没有说太贵；D 正确：团体运动帮助人们结识朋友、获得支持。"}, {"prompt": "What is the author's advice about serious mental health problems?", "options": ["Exercise alone can cure all problems", "People should never seek help", "People should still talk to a doctor or counsellor", "Exercise should be avoided"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：运动不是万能药；B 错误：应寻求帮助；C 正确：有严重问题的人仍应找医生或咨询师，运动不能代替专业帮助；D 错误：运动是有益的习惯。"}]}, {"title": "Protecting Your Eyesight", "text": "Many students spend long hours looking at screens. They study on computers, read on phones, and watch videos at night. As a result, more and more young people have eye problems. They may have dry eyes, headaches, or blurred vision. Some cannot see distant objects clearly and need glasses. Eye doctors say these problems are often caused by bad habits, not only by genes. One bad habit is looking at a screen for too long without a break. When people focus on a screen, they blink less, and their eyes become dry. Another bad habit is reading in the dark or with very bright light. Both can make eyes tired. Holding a phone too close to the eyes is also harmful. To protect eyesight, doctors give simple advice. First, follow the 20-20-20 rule: every 20 minutes, look at something 20 feet away for 20 seconds. This gives the eyes a short rest. Second, keep the screen at a proper distance and lower its brightness at night. Third, spend more time outdoors. Sunlight and looking at distant objects help eyes relax and grow healthily. Studies show that children who spend more time outside are less likely to become short-sighted. Finally, eat food good for eyes, such as carrots, eggs, and fish, and get enough sleep. Eyesight is precious. Once it becomes worse, it is hard to make it better again. Good habits today can protect your eyes for life. In addition, regular eye checks are important, especially for students who use screens a lot. An eye doctor can find problems early and give advice on glasses or habits. Students should tell a teacher or parent if they have trouble seeing the blackboard or reading small print. Sitting too close to the television or holding a book too close are signs that eyes may need help. Schools can help by making sure classrooms have good lighting and by reminding students to take breaks. Protecting eyesight is a shared duty of students, families, and schools working together.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What eye problems are mentioned?", "options": ["Only perfect vision", "Stronger eyesight at once", "Dry eyes, headaches and blurred vision", "No problems at all"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：长时间看屏幕导致眼部问题；B 错误：视力会变差而非变强；C 正确：许多学生出现干眼、头痛或视力模糊；D 错误：文章列举了多种问题。"}, {"prompt": "Why do eyes become dry when looking at a screen?", "options": ["Because people blink too much", "Because screens give water to eyes", "Because people close their eyes", "Because people blink less"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：眨眼少而非多；B 错误：屏幕不会给眼睛补水；C 错误：人们睁眼看屏幕；D 正确：专注看屏幕时眨眼减少，眼睛变干。"}, {"prompt": "What is the 20-20-20 rule?", "options": ["Look at a screen for 20 hours", "Sleep for 20 minutes only", "Every 20 minutes, look at something 20 feet away for 20 seconds", "Read for 20 seconds only"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：看屏幕20小时有害；B 错误：睡眠不是该规则；C 正确：每20分钟看20英尺外物体20秒；D 错误：阅读20秒不是规则。"}, {"prompt": "Why is spending time outdoors helpful?", "options": ["Outdoors makes eyes worse", "Sunlight and distant views help eyes relax", "Children should never go outside", "Outdoor light harms eyes"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：户外活动有益眼睛；B 正确：阳光和看远处物体帮助眼睛放松、健康生长，户外时间多的孩子不易近视；C 错误：应多户外活动；D 错误：户外活动有益眼睛。"}, {"prompt": "What is the main message?", "options": ["Eyesight can always be fixed easily", "Bad habits do not affect eyes", "Screens never harm eyes", "Good habits today can protect your eyes for life"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：视力变差后很难恢复；B 错误：坏习惯会损害眼睛；C 错误：长时间看屏幕会伤害眼睛；D 正确：今天的好习惯能保护眼睛一生。"}]}]}, {"no": 9, "passages": [{"title": "Air Pollution in Cities", "text": "Air pollution is a serious problem in many large cities. Cars, factories, and construction produce smoke and dust that make the air dirty. On some days, the sky looks grey, and people can smell the pollution. Breathing polluted air can harm health. It can cause coughs, breathing problems, and heart disease. Children and older people are especially at risk. Air pollution also harms the environment. It can damage plants, pollute water, and make buildings dirty. Many cities are trying to solve this problem. One way is to improve public transport. If more people take buses and subways, fewer cars will be on the road. Some cities have built bike lanes to encourage cycling. Another way is to control factories. Factories must use cleaner technology and reduce smoke. Some cities have also planted more trees. Trees can absorb some harmful gases and make the air fresher. People can also help. They can walk or cycle for short trips, use public transport, and save electricity at home. On days when pollution is heavy, people should wear masks and avoid outdoor exercise. Solving air pollution takes time and needs everyone to work together. Governments must make good policies, companies must follow the rules, and citizens must change their habits. Clean air is not a luxury. It is a basic need for a healthy life, and protecting it is the duty of every city. In addition, air pollution can affect the economy of a city. When air is dirty, fewer tourists visit, and workers may take more sick days. Cleaning buildings and treating health problems also costs a lot of money. Investing in clean air, by contrast, saves money in the long run. Cities that improve air quality often become more attractive places to live and work. Young people can help by learning about pollution, using bicycles, and encouraging their families to save energy. Every small action, when multiplied by millions of people, can help make the air cleaner for everyone.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What causes air pollution in cities?", "options": ["Only trees", "Cars, factories and construction", "Only clean water", "Only bicycles"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：树木净化空气；B 正确：汽车、工厂和建筑产生烟尘使空气变脏；C 错误：清水不是污染源；D 错误：骑车减少污染。"}, {"prompt": "Who is especially at risk from polluted air?", "options": ["Only young adults", "Only factory workers", "Children and older people", "No one at all"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：儿童和老人风险更高；B 错误：不只是工人；C 正确：呼吸污染空气可致咳嗽、呼吸问题和心脏病，儿童和老人尤其危险；D 错误：污染危害健康。"}, {"prompt": "How can cities reduce pollution through transport?", "options": ["By encouraging more private cars", "By removing all buses", "By closing all subways", "By improving public transport and building bike lanes"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：应减少私家车；B 错误：应发展公交；C 错误：应发展地铁；D 正确：改善公共交通、建自行车道，鼓励更多人乘公交、地铁和骑车。"}, {"prompt": "What can trees do?", "options": ["Produce more pollution", "Damage the air", "Absorb some harmful gases and make air fresher", "Make buildings dirtier"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：树木净化空气；B 错误：树木有益环境；C 正确：树木能吸收有害气体、让空气更新鲜；D 错误：树木不会弄脏建筑。"}, {"prompt": "What does the author think is needed to solve air pollution?", "options": ["Only governments need to act", "Everyone working together: governments, companies and citizens", "Only citizens need to act", "Nothing can be done"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：仅政府不够；B 正确：解决污染需要政府、企业和市民共同努力；C 错误：仅市民不够；D 错误：文章提出多项解决办法。"}]}, {"title": "Green Energy for the Future", "text": "For many years, people have used coal, oil, and gas to produce energy. These fuels are useful, but they cause pollution and climate change. When they are burned, they produce carbon dioxide, a gas that warms the Earth. They will also run out one day. Because of this, many countries are turning to green energy. Green energy comes from nature: the sun, wind, water, and heat from the Earth. Solar power uses panels to turn sunlight into electricity. Wind power uses large turbines to catch the wind. Hydropower uses flowing water to produce electricity. These sources are clean and will not run out. Green energy is growing fast. The cost of solar panels has fallen greatly in recent years. Many houses now have panels on their roofs. Some countries already get a large part of their electricity from wind and water. However, green energy also has problems. Solar power depends on sunny weather. Wind power depends on wind. On cloudy or calm days, they produce less electricity. Storing electricity is also difficult and expensive. Scientists are working on better batteries to solve this problem. Another problem is cost. Building wind farms and solar plants needs a lot of money at first, although they save money later. Despite these problems, most experts believe green energy is the future. It can reduce pollution, slow climate change, and give countries more energy independence. The change will take time, but each solar panel and wind turbine is a step towards a cleaner world. In addition, green energy can create many new jobs. Workers are needed to make solar panels, build wind turbines, and install equipment in homes and factories. These jobs require new skills, and many young people are training for them. Green energy can also help rural areas, where sunlight and wind are plentiful. Villages can use solar power to light homes and schools, even when they are far from the main electricity network. As technology improves and costs fall, green energy will become available to more people in more places around the world.", "questions": [{"prompt": "Why are coal, oil and gas a problem?", "options": ["They are clean and endless", "They cause pollution and climate change and will run out", "They produce no carbon dioxide", "They are never used"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：化石燃料会污染且有限；B 正确：燃烧化石燃料产生二氧化碳、导致气候变暖，且终将耗尽；C 错误：它们产生二氧化碳；D 错误：它们被广泛使用。"}, {"prompt": "What is solar power?", "options": ["Using coal to make electricity", "Using water to burn fuel", "Using panels to turn sunlight into electricity", "Using wind to make coal"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：太阳能不用煤；B 错误：太阳能不用水烧燃料；C 正确：太阳能利用太阳能板把阳光转化为电；D 错误：风能与煤无关。"}, {"prompt": "What is a problem of green energy?", "options": ["It always produces the same amount of power", "It depends on weather and storing electricity is difficult", "It never needs any money", "It causes more pollution than coal"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：阴天或无风时发电减少；B 正确：太阳能依赖晴天、风能依赖风力，储电困难且昂贵；C 错误：建设需要大量资金；D 错误：绿色能源更清洁。"}, {"prompt": "Why has green energy grown fast?", "options": ["Solar panels have become more expensive", "People dislike clean energy", "Green energy is no longer available", "The cost of solar panels has fallen greatly"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：成本下降而非上升；B 错误：人们支持清洁能源；C 错误：绿色能源正在快速发展；D 正确：近年来太阳能板成本大幅下降。"}, {"prompt": "What do most experts believe?", "options": ["Green energy should be abandoned", "Coal is the best future energy", "Green energy is the future and can reduce pollution", "Climate change is not a problem"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：专家支持绿色能源；B 错误：化石燃料会造成污染；C 正确：多数专家认为绿色能源是未来，能减少污染、减缓气候变化；D 错误：气候变化是严重问题。"}]}]}, {"no": 10, "passages": [{"title": "Traditional Festivals", "text": "Every country has traditional festivals. In China, the Spring Festival is the most important one. Families come together, eat special food, and watch fireworks. Children receive red envelopes with money, and people wish each other good luck for the new year. In the West, Christmas is a similar festival. Families decorate trees, exchange gifts, and share a big meal. Although the customs are different, the meaning is similar: festivals bring people together and help them remember their culture. Festivals are also a time to rest. After a year of hard work, people can relax, visit relatives, and enjoy life. For students who study far from home, festivals are a time to return home and feel the warmth of family. However, some traditional customs are changing. In the past, people made festival food by hand and spent days preparing. Today, many people buy ready-made food from shops. Some young people prefer to travel during festivals instead of staying at home. Others spend festivals on their phones, chatting with friends online. Some older people worry that young people are forgetting traditions. But many young people are finding new ways to celebrate. They share festival photos online, learn traditional dances, and teach foreign friends about their culture. Festivals can change, but their core value stays the same: family, friendship, and hope for the future. Keeping traditions alive does not mean doing everything exactly as before. It means understanding why festivals matter and passing that feeling to the next generation. In addition, festivals help people remember their history. Stories, songs, and food from festivals pass culture from one generation to the next. Children learn about their grandparents' lives by taking part in festival customs. Even when families live in different cities, festivals give them a reason to come home and be together. In a fast-changing world, festivals provide a sense of identity and belonging. They remind people of where they come from and what matters most in life: family, community, and shared hope for the future.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the most important festival in China according to the passage?", "options": ["Christmas", "The Spring Festival", "Halloween", "Thanksgiving"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：圣诞节是西方节日；B 正确：春节是中国最重要的节日；C 错误：万圣节不是中国最重要的节日；D 错误：感恩节不是中国节日。"}, {"prompt": "What is similar about the Spring Festival and Christmas?", "options": ["Both are only about money", "Both forbid family meals", "Both bring people together and help them remember culture", "Both are celebrated in the same way"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：节日不只是关于钱；B 错误：节日重视家庭聚餐；C 正确：虽然习俗不同，但意义相似：节日使人团聚、记住文化；D 错误：习俗不同。"}, {"prompt": "How are some customs changing?", "options": ["People still make all food by hand", "No one celebrates festivals anymore", "Festivals have disappeared", "Many buy ready-made food and some travel during festivals"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：过去手工做食物，现在多买现成；B 错误：人们仍在庆祝节日；C 错误：节日依然存在；D 正确：许多人买现成食物，有人节日旅行，有人在手机上过节。"}, {"prompt": "What do some older people worry about?", "options": ["Young people celebrate too much", "Traditions never change", "Festivals are too modern", "Young people are forgetting traditions"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：担心的是忘记传统而非庆祝太多；B 错误：传统在变化；C 错误：担心的是遗忘而非太现代；D 正确：一些老人担心年轻人忘记传统。"}, {"prompt": "What is the core value of festivals according to the author?", "options": ["Only money and gifts", "Family, friendship and hope for the future", "Only fireworks", "Only travelling"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：核心不只是钱和礼物；B 正确：节日的核心价值不变：家庭、友谊和对未来的希望；C 错误：烟花只是形式；D 错误：旅行只是新方式。"}]}, {"title": "Learning English Through Movies", "text": "Many English learners enjoy watching movies in English. It is fun, and it can help improve listening, vocabulary, and pronunciation. When learners watch a film, they hear real English as native speakers use it. They learn everyday expressions, slang, and different accents. They also see body language and facial expressions, which help them understand meaning. However, watching movies is not always easy for learners. If the film is too fast or too difficult, learners may feel lost. They may only understand a few words and give up quickly. To learn well from movies, learners need a good method. First, choose a film you like and already know the story of. A comedy or a cartoon is often easier than a science fiction film with difficult words. Second, watch with English subtitles, not Chinese subtitles. English subtitles help learners connect sounds with words. Third, watch a short part again and again. Listen carefully, repeat the lines, and write down useful expressions. Fourth, do not try to understand every word. Focus on the main idea and enjoy the story. If you understand about 80 percent, that is good enough. Finally, talk about the film with friends or write a short review in English. Using new words helps you remember them. Movies cannot replace textbooks and teachers, but they are a good way to make learning enjoyable. With the right method and regular practice, learners can improve their English while having fun. In addition, movies can teach learners about culture as well as language. By watching films from different countries, learners see how people live, work, and talk in daily life. They learn about humour, politeness, and social customs. This cultural knowledge helps learners communicate more naturally and avoid misunderstandings. Learners can also keep a notebook of useful sentences from films and try to use them in real conversations. Over time, these sentences become part of their own English. Movies, used wisely, are both entertainment and a valuable classroom without walls.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What can movies help learners improve?", "options": ["Only writing", "Only maths", "Listening, vocabulary and pronunciation", "Nothing at all"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：电影主要帮助听说；B 错误：电影与数学无关；C 正确：电影能帮助提高听力、词汇和发音；D 错误：电影对学习有帮助。"}, {"prompt": "Why can watching movies be difficult?", "options": ["Movies are always too easy", "If the film is too fast or too difficult, learners may feel lost", "Learners understand everything at once", "Films have no English at all"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：电影常太难；B 正确：如果影片太快或太难，学习者可能感到迷茫；C 错误：学习者常听不懂；D 错误：电影是英文的。"}, {"prompt": "What kind of subtitles are suggested?", "options": ["Chinese subtitles only", "No subtitles at all", "English subtitles", "Subtitles in another language"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：中文 subtitles 不利于连接发音与单词；B 错误：无字幕对初学者太难；C 正确：建议用英文字幕，帮助把声音与单词联系起来；D 错误：应用英文字幕。"}, {"prompt": "How much should learners understand?", "options": ["Every single word", "Only 10 percent", "Nothing at all", "About 80 percent is good enough"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：不必理解每个词；B 错误：理解太少不够；C 错误：应理解大意；D 正确：不必理解每个词，理解约80%、抓住大意就足够。"}, {"prompt": "What should learners do after watching?", "options": ["Never use new words", "Talk about the film or write a short review in English", "Forget the film at once", "Stop learning English"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：应使用新词；B 正确：看完后与朋友讨论或用英语写短评，有助于记住新词；C 错误：应复习新表达；D 错误：电影是学习的辅助。"}]}]}, {"no": 11, "passages": [{"title": "Coping with Exam Stress", "text": "Exams can make many students feel stressed. They worry about grades, fear failing, and sometimes cannot sleep well before an important test. A little stress can be helpful because it makes people focus and work hard. However, too much stress can hurt both body and mind. Students may feel tired, lose their appetite, or find it hard to remember what they have studied. To cope with exam stress, students need a good plan. First, they should start studying early and review a little every day, instead of studying all night before the exam. A regular plan makes learning easier and reduces fear. Second, they should take breaks. A short walk, some music, or a talk with friends can help the brain rest. Third, they should keep healthy habits: sleep enough, eat well, and do some exercise. A tired body cannot think clearly. Fourth, students should change the way they think about exams. An exam is a chance to show what they have learned, not a test of their whole value as a person. Even if the result is not perfect, they can learn from mistakes and do better next time. Teachers and parents can also help by giving encouragement instead of too much pressure. Talking about worries with a friend or teacher often makes problems feel smaller. With good planning, healthy habits, and support from others, exam stress can be managed. Students can face exams with more confidence and a calmer mind. In addition, physical activity can help reduce exam stress. A short run, a game of basketball, or even a walk around the campus can clear the mind and release tension. Students who exercise regularly often sleep better and feel more positive before exams. Talking with classmates can also help, because students realise they are not alone in feeling stressed. Sharing study tips and encouraging each other makes preparation less lonely. With support, rest, and a clear plan, most students can get through exam season and learn valuable lessons about managing pressure in life.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What can a little stress do?", "options": ["It always harms the body", "It makes people stop studying", "It can help people focus and work hard", "It has no effect at all"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：只有过大压力才有害；B 错误：适度压力促进学习；C 正确：适度压力能让人集中注意力、努力学习；D 错误：压力有影响。"}, {"prompt": "What problem can too much stress cause?", "options": ["Better sleep every night", "Students may feel tired and find it hard to remember", "Students always get higher grades", "Students never worry about exams"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：压力大常导致睡眠差；B 正确：压力过大时学生会疲倦、食欲下降、难以记住所学；C 错误：压力大不利于成绩；D 错误：压力大时更担心。"}, {"prompt": "What is the first suggestion in the passage?", "options": ["Study all night before the exam", "Start early and review a little every day", "Never review at all", "Only study on the exam day"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：通宵学习不好；B 正确：应尽早开始、每天复习一点；C 错误：需要复习；D 错误：只在考试当天学习不够。"}, {"prompt": "How should students think about exams?", "options": ["As a test of their whole value as a person", "As a chance to show what they have learned", "As something to fear and avoid", "As the only thing that matters in life"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：考试不是衡量整个人价值的标准；B 正确：考试是展示所学的机会，即使结果不完美也可从错误中学习；C 错误：应以积极心态面对；D 错误：考试不是生活的全部。"}, {"prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?", "options": ["Exams should be cancelled", "Students should never feel stressed", "Exam stress can be managed with good planning, healthy habits and support", "Only teachers can solve stress"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：文章没有说取消考试；B 错误：适度压力是正常的；C 正确：文章主旨是通过好计划、健康习惯和他人支持管理考试压力；D 错误：学生自己也可采取措施。"}]}, {"title": "The University Library", "text": "The university library is one of the most important places on campus. It is not only a place to borrow books. It is also a quiet place to study, do research, and think. Many students go to the library every day, especially before exams. In the library, students can find textbooks, reference books, journals, and newspapers. They can also use computers, printers, and online databases to look for information for their essays and projects. Librarians are there to help. If a student cannot find a book, a librarian can show them how to search the system. Some libraries also offer study rooms where small groups can discuss projects without disturbing others. There are also special areas for silent study, where even whispering is not allowed. To use the library well, students should learn its rules. They should keep quiet, return books on time, and take care of books and equipment. They should also learn how to use the online catalogue and databases, which can save a lot of time. In recent years, libraries have changed. Many now have cafes, exhibition areas, and spaces for group work. Some organise reading clubs, talks, and workshops. These activities make the library more than a storehouse of books. It has become a centre for learning and交流. A good library helps students become independent learners. It teaches them how to find information, judge its quality, and use it wisely. For university students, learning to use the library is learning to learn. In addition, the library can be a place for quiet reflection and creativity. Many writers and researchers say their best ideas came while sitting in a library, surrounded by books. The calm atmosphere helps people think deeply and focus on difficult problems. Some libraries now offer workshops on writing, research skills, and using new technology. These workshops help students become better learners. By visiting the library regularly, students build a habit of independent study that will help them long after they leave university and start work.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the library besides a place to borrow books?", "options": ["A place to play loud games", "A shop for buying food only", "A quiet place to study, do research and think", "A place to sleep all day"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：图书馆要求安静；B 错误：图书馆主要是学习场所；C 正确：图书馆还是安静的学习、研究和思考之地；D 错误：图书馆不是睡觉的地方。"}, {"prompt": "What can librarians do?", "options": ["They forbid students to borrow books", "They make students study alone", "They can help students find books and search the system", "They sell textbooks to students"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：馆员帮助借书；B 错误：图书馆有小组学习空间；C 正确：馆员能帮助学生找书、教使用检索系统；D 错误：图书馆是借书而非卖书。"}, {"prompt": "What should students do to use the library well?", "options": ["Talk loudly in silent areas", "Keep books forever", "Damage books and equipment", "Keep quiet, return books on time and learn to use the catalogue"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：安静区禁止喧哗；B 错误：应按时还书；C 错误：应爱护图书设备；D 正确：应保持安静、按时还书、学会使用目录和数据库。"}, {"prompt": "How have libraries changed in recent years?", "options": ["They have removed all books", "They now have cafes, exhibitions and group work spaces", "They no longer allow study", "They only store old books"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：图书馆仍有大量图书；B 正确：许多图书馆增设咖啡厅、展览区和小组学习空间，并举办活动；C 错误：图书馆仍是学习场所；D 错误：图书馆已成为学习中心。"}, {"prompt": "What does a good library teach students?", "options": ["How to avoid reading", "How to buy expensive books", "How to ignore information", "How to find information, judge its quality and use it wisely"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：图书馆鼓励阅读；B 错误：图书馆是借书；C 错误：应重视信息；D 正确：好图书馆教学生查找信息、判断质量并明智使用。"}]}]}, {"no": 12, "passages": [{"title": "Online Shopping", "text": "Online shopping has become very popular in recent years. With a phone or computer, people can buy clothes, food, books, and many other things without leaving home. They can compare prices in different shops, read reviews from other buyers, and choose the best product. For busy people, online shopping saves a lot of time. It is also convenient for people who live far from shops. However, online shopping has some problems. First, buyers cannot touch or try products before buying them. A dress may look nice in a photo but fit badly in real life. Second, some products are of poor quality, and some sellers are dishonest. Buyers may pay for something and never receive it. Third, online shopping can make people spend too much money. It is easy to click and buy things they do not need, especially during sales. To shop safely online, buyers should choose well-known websites, read reviews carefully, and check return policies before paying. They should also be careful with personal information and avoid clicking strange links. For sellers, online shopping is a chance to reach more customers. Small shops can sell to people all over the country. At the same time, traditional shops face strong competition. Many have closed because they cannot compete with low online prices. Despite its problems, online shopping is likely to grow. The key is to use it wisely: buy what you need, compare carefully, and protect your information. In addition, online shopping has changed how people celebrate festivals and birthdays. People can order gifts online and have them delivered directly to friends and family in other cities. This is convenient, but it can also make shopping feel less personal. Some people still enjoy choosing gifts in a real shop, wrapping them by hand, and giving them in person. The best approach is to combine both: use online shopping for convenience, but keep the personal touch for special occasions. Wise shoppers think about value, quality, and need, not only about price and speed.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is an advantage of online shopping?", "options": ["Buyers must travel to every shop", "Online shopping always takes more time", "People cannot buy anything online", "Buyers can compare prices and read reviews without leaving home"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：网购不用去商店；B 错误：网购节省时间；C 错误：网购可买许多商品；D 正确：网购可比较价格、看评价、不用出门。"}, {"prompt": "What is a problem of online shopping?", "options": ["Buyers can touch all products first", "All sellers are honest", "Buyers cannot try products before buying", "Online shopping stops people from spending money"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：网购不能触摸试用；B 错误：有些卖家不诚实；C 正确：买家买前不能触摸或试用，衣服照片好看但实际可能不合身；D 错误：网购容易让人花太多钱。"}, {"prompt": "Why can online shopping make people spend too much?", "options": ["Because online shops never have sales", "Because people always think carefully", "Because online shopping is too slow", "Because it is easy to click and buy things they do not need"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：促销会刺激消费；B 错误：冲动购买是问题；C 错误：网购方便快捷；D 正确：点击购买很容易，尤其在促销时易买不需要的东西。"}, {"prompt": "What should buyers do to shop safely?", "options": ["Click every strange link", "Share all personal information freely", "Choose well-known websites and check return policies", "Never read any reviews"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：不应点击可疑链接；B 错误：应保护个人信息；C 正确：应选知名网站、仔细看评价、付款前查看退货政策；D 错误：应仔细阅读评价。"}, {"prompt": "What effect does online shopping have on traditional shops?", "options": ["It helps all traditional shops earn more", "It has no effect on them", "Many have closed because they cannot compete with low prices", "Traditional shops have become more popular"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：传统商店面临竞争；B 错误：网购对传统商店有影响；C 正确：许多传统商店因无法与低价竞争而关闭；D 错误：传统商店面临压力。"}]}, {"title": "Paper Books or E-books", "text": "Today, readers can choose between paper books and e-books. Each has its own advantages. Paper books are easy on the eyes. Readers can hold them, turn pages, and write notes in the margins. Many people enjoy the smell of a new book and the feeling of reading in a quiet room. Studies show that people often remember more when they read on paper, because they can see where information is on a page. E-books have different advantages. One small device can hold thousands of books, so readers can carry a whole library in their bag. E-books are also easy to search. Readers can find a word in seconds. They can change the font size, which helps people with poor eyesight. E-books are often cheaper than paper books, and some are free. However, e-books also have problems. Reading on a screen for a long time can make eyes tired. Some readers are easily distracted by messages and other apps on the same device. Paper books do not have this problem. Which is better? The answer depends on the reader and the situation. For deep reading at home, a paper book may be better. For travelling or looking up information quickly, an e-book may be more convenient. Many readers now use both. They read paper books at home and e-books on the bus or train. The important thing is not the form of the book, but the habit of reading. Whether on paper or on a screen, reading opens the mind and brings new ideas. In addition, libraries are adapting to both kinds of reading. Many libraries now lend e-books as well as paper books, so readers can choose what suits them. Some readers like to read a paper book at home and listen to an audiobook while travelling. Others use e-books to search for information quickly and paper books for slow, deep reading. Technology will keep changing how we read, but the pleasure of a good story and the value of learning from books will remain. The future of reading is likely to include both paper and screens, side by side.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is an advantage of paper books?", "options": ["They are heavy and cannot be carried", "They have no pages to turn", "Readers can hold them, write notes and often remember more", "They are always more expensive for everyone"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：纸质书可携带；B 错误：纸质书有页可翻；C 正确：纸质书可手持、翻页、做笔记，研究显示纸上阅读记得更多；D 错误：纸质书并非对所有人都更贵。"}, {"prompt": "What is an advantage of e-books?", "options": ["They cannot be searched", "They never change font size", "They are always heavier than paper books", "One device can hold thousands of books"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：电子书易于搜索；B 错误：电子书可调字体大小；C 错误：电子设备轻便；D 正确：一个设备可存数千本书，便于携带。"}, {"prompt": "What problem do e-books have?", "options": ["E-books never tire the eyes", "E-books have no apps at all", "Paper books cause more distraction", "Reading on a screen can make eyes tired and cause distraction"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：屏幕阅读会疲劳；B 错误：设备上有其他应用；C 错误：纸质书不易分心；D 正确：长时间看屏幕会使眼睛疲劳，且易被消息和其他应用分心。"}, {"prompt": "When might an e-book be more convenient?", "options": ["For deep reading at home", "For travelling or looking up information quickly", "For people who dislike technology", "For reading in a quiet room"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：在家深读纸质书可能更好；B 正确：旅行或快速查信息时电子书更方便；C 错误：电子书适合喜欢技术的人；D 错误：安静房间适合纸质书。"}, {"prompt": "What is the author's main point?", "options": ["Paper books are always better", "E-books are always better", "The important thing is the habit of reading, not the form", "Reading is not useful anymore"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：作者认为两者各有优点；B 错误：作者认为两者各有优点；C 正确：重要的是阅读习惯而非书的形式，无论纸质还是屏幕，阅读都能开阔思维；D 错误：作者肯定阅读的价值。"}]}]}, {"no": 13, "passages": [{"title": "A Healthy Diet", "text": "What we eat has a great effect on our health. A healthy diet gives the body energy, helps it grow, and protects it from illness. However, many young people today eat too much fast food, sweet drinks, and snacks. These foods are tasty and convenient, but they often contain too much sugar, salt, and fat. Eating them too often can lead to weight gain, tooth problems, and other health issues. A healthy diet does not mean eating only vegetables or giving up all tasty food. It means eating a variety of foods in the right amounts. Grains such as rice, bread, and noodles give energy. Meat, fish, eggs, and beans give protein. Vegetables and fruit give vitamins and fibre, which help digestion and keep the body strong. Milk and dairy products give calcium for strong bones. Water is also important. Drinking enough water helps the body work well. Sweet drinks may taste good, but water is the best choice for thirst. Eating habits also matter. Eating regular meals, not skipping breakfast, and not eating too late at night are good habits. Eating slowly and stopping when full can prevent overeating. Cooking at home is often healthier than eating out, because people can control the amount of oil and salt. A healthy diet is not a short diet for losing weight. It is a lifelong habit. Small changes, such as eating one more vegetable a day or drinking water instead of sweet drinks, can make a big difference over time. In addition, eating together as a family can help children develop healthy habits. When parents cook balanced meals and eat slowly with their children, children learn what good food looks like. They are more likely to try new vegetables and to enjoy meals without screens. Schools can also help by providing healthy lunches and teaching students about nutrition. A healthy diet is a team effort involving families, schools, and communities. When everyone works together, young people can grow up strong, energetic, and ready to learn.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What effect does food have on health?", "options": ["It has no effect at all", "It gives energy, helps growth and protects from illness", "It only affects taste", "It always makes people ill"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：饮食对健康影响很大；B 正确：健康饮食提供能量、帮助生长、预防疾病；C 错误：饮食不只影响味道；D 错误：健康饮食有益。"}, {"prompt": "Why is fast food a problem?", "options": ["It is always very healthy", "It has no taste at all", "It often contains too much sugar, salt and fat", "It is too expensive for everyone"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：快餐多不健康；B 错误：快餐味道好；C 正确：快餐常含过多糖、盐和脂肪，常吃会导致体重增加等问题；D 错误：价格不是主要问题。"}, {"prompt": "What do vegetables and fruit give?", "options": ["Only sugar", "Vitamins and fibre for digestion and strength", "Only fat", "Nothing useful"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：蔬果提供维生素和纤维；B 正确：蔬菜水果提供维生素和纤维，有助消化、强身；C 错误：蔬果脂肪少；D 错误：蔬果有益。"}, {"prompt": "What is the best choice for thirst?", "options": ["Sweet drinks", "Coffee only", "Water", "Milk only"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：甜饮料不如水；B 错误：水是最好的选择；C 正确：水是解渴的最佳选择；D 错误：水比牛奶更适合解渴。"}, {"prompt": "What does the author suggest about changing diet?", "options": ["Give up all tasty food at once", "Eat only vegetables forever", "Never cook at home", "Make small changes such as one more vegetable a day"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：健康饮食不是放弃所有美食；B 错误：应吃多种食物；C 错误：在家做饭通常更健康；D 正确：小改变如每天多吃一种蔬菜、用白水代替甜饮料，长期效果显著。"}]}, {"title": "The Benefits of Team Sports", "text": "Team sports, such as football, basketball, and volleyball, are popular among students. They are not only fun, but also good for health and personal growth. First, team sports help the body stay strong. Running, jumping, and throwing improve strength, speed, and coordination. Regular training also helps the heart and lungs work better. Second, team sports teach cooperation. No player can win a game alone. Players must pass the ball, support each other, and follow the coach's plan. They learn to trust teammates and to put the team's goal before personal glory. Third, team sports teach students how to deal with winning and losing. Winning brings joy, but losing is also a lesson. Players learn to accept defeat, find mistakes, and try again. This attitude is useful in study and work. Fourth, team sports help students make friends. Training and playing together create strong bonds. Students from different classes and backgrounds become close through a shared goal. However, team sports also need balance. Students should not train so much that they have no time to study. Injuries can also happen, so warming up and following safety rules are important. With good planning and care, team sports can be a valuable part of school life. They build a healthy body, strong teamwork, and a positive attitude towards challenges. In addition, team sports can teach students about fairness and respect. Players must follow rules, respect referees, and accept decisions even when they disagree. They learn to respect opponents as well as teammates. These lessons about fair play are important in life and work. A good player is not only skilled, but also honest and respectful. Coaches play a key role by setting a good example and by encouraging players to support each other. Through sport, students learn values that will guide them long after they stop playing.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What does team sport improve physically?", "options": ["Only reading ability", "Only memory", "Strength, speed and coordination", "Nothing at all"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：团队运动主要锻炼身体；B 错误：运动也助学习但非主要；C 正确：跑跳投掷能提高力量、速度和协调性；D 错误：团队运动有益健康。"}, {"prompt": "What do players learn about cooperation?", "options": ["They can win alone", "They must pass, support each other and follow the plan", "They should ignore teammates", "They should only seek personal glory"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：无人能独自赢球；B 正确：队员须传球、互相支持、执行教练计划，信任队友；C 错误：应与队友合作；D 错误：应把团队目标放在个人荣誉之前。"}, {"prompt": "What can losing teach players?", "options": ["To give up at once", "To accept defeat, find mistakes and try again", "To blame teammates always", "To stop playing forever"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：失败后应再尝试；B 正确：输球教会接受失败、找出错误并再试；C 错误：不应总责怪队友；D 错误：失败是学习机会。"}, {"prompt": "How do team sports help friendship?", "options": ["They make students lonely", "They stop students from talking", "Training and playing together create strong bonds", "They separate students from each other"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：团队运动促进交友；B 错误：团队运动促进交流；C 正确：一起训练比赛能建立深厚情谊；D 错误：团队运动拉近学生。"}, {"prompt": "What warning is given?", "options": ["Students should train all day", "Injuries never happen", "Safety rules are not needed", "Students should not train so much that they have no time to study"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：训练需平衡学习；B 错误：运动可能受伤；C 错误：应遵守安全规则；D 正确：不应训练过多以致无时间学习，还要注意热身和安全。"}]}]}, {"no": 14, "passages": [{"title": "Protecting Forests", "text": "Forests are often called the lungs of the Earth. They produce oxygen, absorb carbon dioxide, and provide homes for many animals and plants. Forests also protect soil, keep water clean, and help control the climate. However, forests around the world are disappearing quickly. Every year, large areas of forest are cut down for farming, building, and wood. When forests are lost, animals lose their homes, and the climate becomes warmer and drier. Soil can be washed away by rain, and floods become more common. Protecting forests is important for everyone, not only for people who live near them. Many countries have made laws to stop illegal cutting and to protect natural forests. They have also planted new trees in areas where forests were lost. Planting trees is helpful, but a new forest cannot replace an old forest quickly. Old forests have rich soil, many kinds of plants and animals, and a balanced system that has taken hundreds of years to form. People can also help protect forests in daily life. Using less paper, recycling paper, and choosing products made from recycled materials can reduce the need to cut trees. Buying wood products from responsible sources also helps. Students can learn about forests, take part in tree-planting activities, and tell others why forests matter. Forests give us clean air, fresh water, and a home for wildlife. Protecting them is protecting our own future. In addition, forests are home to medicines and foods that people depend on. Many medicines come from plants found only in forests. Fruits, nuts, and honey from forests provide food and income for local people. When forests disappear, these resources disappear too. Protecting forests therefore protects human health and livelihoods. Tourists also visit forests to enjoy nature, which brings income to local communities. By protecting forests, we protect nature, culture, and the economy at the same time, for present and future generations.", "questions": [{"prompt": "Why are forests called the lungs of the Earth?", "options": ["They have no air at all", "They only provide wood", "They produce oxygen and absorb carbon dioxide", "They make the climate warmer"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：森林净化空气；B 错误：森林不只提供木材；C 正确：森林产生氧气、吸收二氧化碳；D 错误：森林有助于调节气候。"}, {"prompt": "What happens when forests are lost?", "options": ["The climate becomes cooler", "Soil becomes stronger", "Animals lose homes and climate becomes warmer and drier", "Floods become less common"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：气候会变暖；B 错误：土壤会被雨水冲走；C 正确：森林消失后动物失去家园，气候变暖变干，土壤流失、洪水增多；D 错误：洪水更常见。"}, {"prompt": "What have many countries done?", "options": ["They encourage illegal cutting", "They have made laws to stop illegal cutting and planted new trees", "They have removed all forests", "They do nothing about forests"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：国家立法禁止非法砍伐；B 正确：多国立法制止非法砍伐、保护天然林并植树；C 错误：国家在保护森林；D 错误：文章列举了多项措施。"}, {"prompt": "Why cannot a new forest replace an old one quickly?", "options": ["New forests are always better", "Old forests have no animals", "New forests grow in one day", "Old forests have rich soil and a balanced system formed over hundreds of years"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：新林不能迅速代替老林；B 错误：老林动植物丰富；C 错误：新林生长需要时间；D 正确：古老森林有肥沃土壤、多种动植物和百年形成的平衡系统。"}, {"prompt": "What can people do in daily life?", "options": ["Cut down more trees", "Buy products from irresponsible sources", "Ignore forests completely", "Use less paper and recycle paper"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：应保护森林；B 错误：应选负责任来源的木制品；C 错误：应关注森林保护；D 正确：少用纸、回收纸、选再生材料产品可减少砍树需求。"}]}, {"title": "Saving Water", "text": "Water is essential for life. People need water to drink, cook, wash, and grow food. However, many parts of the world face water shortages. In some places, people have to walk long distances to get clean water. Even in cities with enough water, wasting water is a serious problem. Many people leave taps running while brushing teeth, take very long showers, and use too much water to wash cars and water gardens. Saving water is not difficult. Small changes can make a big difference. Turning off the tap while brushing teeth can save several litres of water each time. Taking a shorter shower, fixing leaking taps, and using a basin to wash vegetables are simple ways to save water. Farmers can also save water by using drip irrigation, which gives water directly to the roots of plants. Factories can reuse water after treating it. Schools can teach students about water and install water-saving taps. Saving water also helps the environment. When less water is used, less energy is needed to clean and transport it. This reduces pollution and saves money. In the future, water may become even more precious as the population grows and the climate changes. Learning to save water today is a way to protect tomorrow. Every drop counts, and everyone can play a part. In addition, schools and offices can save water by checking pipes regularly and by using water-saving devices. A leaking tap that drips once a second can waste thousands of litres in a year. Fixing it is cheap and easy, but many people ignore it. Public campaigns can remind people to save water and to report leaks. Children can learn to save water at school and then teach their families at home. When saving water becomes a habit for everyone, cities can use water more wisely and protect this precious resource for the future.", "questions": [{"prompt": "Why is water important?", "options": ["It is only for swimming", "It has no use in life", "People need it to drink, cook, wash and grow food", "It is only for factories"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：水用途广泛；B 错误：水对生命至关重要；C 正确：人需要水来饮用、做饭、洗漱和种粮食；D 错误：水不只用于工厂。"}, {"prompt": "What is an example of wasting water?", "options": ["Turning off the tap while brushing teeth", "Leaving taps running while brushing teeth", "Taking a short shower", "Fixing leaking taps"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：关水龙头是节水；B 正确：刷牙时让水龙头一直开着是浪费；C 错误：短时间淋浴节水；D 错误：修理漏水龙头节水。"}, {"prompt": "How can farmers save water?", "options": ["By flooding fields all day", "By using more water than needed", "By using drip irrigation to give water directly to roots", "By never watering plants"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：漫灌浪费水；B 错误：应用水适量；C 正确：滴灌直接把水送到植物根部，可节水；D 错误：植物需要水。"}, {"prompt": "How does saving water help the environment?", "options": ["It increases pollution", "It wastes more energy", "It has no environmental benefit", "Less water used means less energy to clean and transport it"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：节水减少污染；B 错误：节水节省能源；C 错误：节水有益环境；D 正确：用水少则清洁和运输所需能量少，减少污染、节省开支。"}, {"prompt": "What is the main message?", "options": ["Water will never run out", "Wasting water is good", "Only farmers need to save water", "Every drop counts and everyone can save water"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：未来水可能更珍贵；B 错误：浪费水是严重问题；C 错误：人人都应节水；D 正确：每一滴水都重要，人人都能节水。"}]}]}, {"no": 15, "passages": [{"title": "Travelling Alone", "text": "Many young people dream of travelling alone. They want to see new places, meet new people, and have adventures by themselves. Travelling alone can be exciting and rewarding. When you travel alone, you can choose where to go and what to do without asking others. You can change your plan at any time, stay longer in a place you love, or leave a place you do not like. Travelling alone also helps people grow. They learn to solve problems, such as finding a hotel, asking for directions, or dealing with lost luggage. They become more confident and independent. They also have time to think and understand themselves better. However, travelling alone has risks. It can be lonely sometimes, especially at night or during festivals when others are with family and friends. Safety is another concern. Travellers should be careful in unfamiliar places, keep their belongings safe, and tell family or friends where they are. To travel alone safely, good preparation is important. Travellers should research their destination, book safe places to stay, and keep emergency numbers. They should also keep an open mind and talk to local people, who often give helpful advice. Travelling alone does not mean being alone all the time. Many solo travellers make friends in hostels, on tours, or in cafes. In the end, travelling alone is a chance to see the world and to see yourself more clearly. In addition, travelling alone can help people learn a new language. When you travel by yourself, you must ask for directions, order food, and talk to local people. This forces you to practise the language in real situations. Many travellers say they learned more language in one week of travel than in months of classroom study. Travelling alone also builds problem-solving skills. When plans change or something goes wrong, you must find a solution by yourself. These experiences make travellers more confident and more open to new cultures and new friends.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is an advantage of travelling alone?", "options": ["You must follow others' plans", "You cannot change your plan", "You can choose where to go without asking others", "You have no freedom at all"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：独自旅行不用跟随他人；B 错误：独自旅行可随时改计划；C 正确：独自旅行可自由选择去向和活动；D 错误：独自旅行自由度高。"}, {"prompt": "How does travelling alone help people grow?", "options": ["They become more dependent", "They avoid all problems", "They learn to solve problems and become more confident", "They never meet anyone"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：独自旅行让人更独立；B 错误：旅行中会遇到问题；C 正确：独自旅行学会解决问题，如找酒店、问路、处理行李丢失，变得更自信独立；D 错误：旅行中会结识他人。"}, {"prompt": "What risk is mentioned?", "options": ["Travelling alone is always safe", "It can be lonely and safety is a concern", "Solo travellers never feel lonely", "There are no risks at all"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：独自旅行有风险；B 正确：独自旅行有时会孤独，安全也是问题；C 错误：独自旅行有时会孤独；D 错误：文章提到了风险。"}, {"prompt": "What should solo travellers do to stay safe?", "options": ["Go to unfamiliar places without any plan", "Never tell anyone where they are", "Keep belongings unsafe", "Research the destination, book safe stays and keep emergency numbers"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：应做好准备；B 错误：应告知家人朋友行踪；C 错误：应保管好财物；D 正确：应研究目的地、预订安全住宿、保存紧急电话。"}, {"prompt": "What is the author's final view?", "options": ["Travelling alone is always lonely", "Solo travel has no value", "People should never travel alone", "Travelling alone is a chance to see the world and yourself more clearly"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：独自旅行不等于一直孤独；B 错误：独自旅行有益成长；C 错误：作者肯定独自旅行；D 正确：独自旅行是看世界、更清楚认识自己的机会。"}]}, {"title": "Food Culture Around the World", "text": "Food is more than something to eat. It is a part of culture. Different countries have different food, eating habits, and table manners. In China, people often share dishes at a table. They use chopsticks and enjoy tea with meals. In Japan, people also use chopsticks, but they eat rice, fish, and soup in a quiet and careful way. In India, many people eat with their hands and use spices to make food tasty. In Italy, families enjoy pasta and pizza together, and meals can last for a long time. In the United States, fast food is popular, but many families also cook at home. Food culture also shows values. In some cultures, sharing food means friendship and respect. In others, finishing all the food on your plate shows that you enjoyed the meal, while in some places leaving a little food shows you are full and polite. Travellers should learn about local food culture before visiting a new country. Trying local food is a good way to understand people and their way of life. It is also a way to show respect. Even if the taste is strange at first, an open mind can lead to a new favourite dish. Food brings people together across languages and borders. When people cook and eat together, they share stories, laughter, and warmth. In addition, food festivals are a wonderful way to learn about culture. In many cities, food festivals bring together cooks from different countries, and visitors can taste dishes they have never tried before. These events celebrate diversity and help people understand each other better. Cooking classes for visitors are also popular. Tourists can learn to make local dishes and take the recipes home. Food is a bridge between cultures: it invites curiosity, conversation, and friendship, and it reminds us that people everywhere share the pleasure of a good meal.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What does food show besides taste?", "options": ["It has no cultural meaning", "It only shows wealth", "It is a part of culture and shows values", "It is the same everywhere"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：食物有文化含义；B 错误：食物不只显示财富；C 正确：食物是文化的一部分，也体现价值观；D 错误：各国食物不同。"}, {"prompt": "How do people eat in China according to the passage?", "options": ["They eat only with hands", "They never share food", "They often share dishes and use chopsticks", "They eat alone always"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：用手吃饭是印度习俗；B 错误：中国人常分享菜肴；C 正确：在中国，人们常共餐、用筷子、配茶；D 错误：中国人重视共餐。"}, {"prompt": "What is true about table manners in different cultures?", "options": ["Finishing all food always means the same thing", "In some places leaving a little food shows you are full and polite", "All cultures have the same manners", "Table manners do not matter"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：不同文化含义不同；B 正确：在一些地方剩一点食物表示吃饱且礼貌；C 错误：各国餐桌礼仪不同；D 错误：餐桌礼仪重要。"}, {"prompt": "Why should travellers learn about local food culture?", "options": ["To avoid all local food", "To insult local people", "To eat only fast food", "To show respect and understand people better"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：应尝试当地食物；B 错误：应尊重当地文化；C 错误：应尝试当地美食；D 正确：了解当地饮食文化可表示尊重、更好地理解当地人。"}, {"prompt": "What is the main idea?", "options": ["Food is only for filling the stomach", "All food tastes the same", "People should never try new food", "Food brings people together across languages and borders"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：食物不只是果腹；B 错误：各国食物味道不同；C 错误：应以开放心态尝试新食物；D 正确：食物跨越语言和国界把人们聚在一起。"}]}]}, {"no": 16, "passages": [{"title": "Volunteering in the Community", "text": "Volunteering means giving your time to help others without being paid. Many students volunteer in their communities. They help old people, teach children, clean parks, or work in hospitals. Volunteering is good for both the community and the volunteer. For the community, volunteers provide help that is needed but may not be available. For example, an old person who lives alone may need someone to talk to or help with shopping. A child who struggles at school may need a tutor. Volunteers can fill these gaps. For volunteers, the experience teaches important lessons. They learn to understand people from different backgrounds, to work as a team, and to take responsibility. They also gain confidence and feel a sense of purpose. Many volunteers say helping others makes them happier than receiving help. Volunteering also looks good on a resume. Employers like people who care about others and have real experience. However, volunteering should not be done only for a resume. The most important reason is to help others sincerely. Students who volunteer should choose an activity they care about and give their time regularly, not just once. Even two hours a week can make a difference. A community becomes stronger when people help each other. Volunteering is a simple way to start. In addition, volunteering can help students explore future careers. A student who volunteers in a hospital may discover an interest in medicine. A student who teaches children may decide to become a teacher. These experiences help students understand what work is really like before they choose a career. Volunteering also teaches practical skills, such as organising events, speaking in public, and working with people of different ages. Employers value these skills, but the greatest reward is the feeling of making a real difference in someone's life.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What does volunteering mean?", "options": ["Working only for money", "Helping others for a high salary", "Giving time to help others without being paid", "Doing nothing for the community"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：志愿服务无报酬；B 错误：志愿服务不是高薪工作；C 正确：志愿服务指无偿付出时间帮助他人；D 错误：志愿服务是帮助社区。"}, {"prompt": "How can volunteers help an old person?", "options": ["By ignoring them", "By talking to them or helping with shopping", "By taking their money", "By leaving them alone"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：志愿者应关心老人；B 正确：独居老人可能需要人聊天或帮忙购物；C 错误：志愿者不取钱；D 错误：志愿者应陪伴老人。"}, {"prompt": "What do volunteers learn?", "options": ["To avoid all people", "To work alone always", "To understand people from different backgrounds and work as a team", "To ignore responsibility"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：志愿服务需要与人接触；B 错误：志愿服务常需合作；C 正确：志愿者学会理解不同背景的人、团队合作和承担责任；D 错误：志愿服务培养责任感。"}, {"prompt": "Why do many volunteers feel happier?", "options": ["They receive a lot of money", "They never work hard", "They dislike helping others", "Helping others makes them happier than receiving help"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：志愿服务无报酬；B 错误：志愿服务需要付出；C 错误：志愿者乐于助人；D 正确：许多志愿者说帮助他人比接受帮助更快乐。"}, {"prompt": "What advice is given to student volunteers?", "options": ["Volunteer only once for a resume", "Choose an activity they dislike", "Never volunteer at all", "Choose an activity they care about and give time regularly"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：不应只为简历做一次；B 错误：应选择关心的活动；C 错误：志愿服务有益；D 正确：应选择关心的活动并定期付出时间。"}]}, {"title": "Public Transport in Big Cities", "text": "Public transport, such as buses, subways, and trains, is very important in big cities. Millions of people use it every day to go to work, school, and other places. Good public transport has many advantages. First, it reduces traffic. If more people take buses and subways, fewer cars are on the road, and traffic jams become less serious. Second, it reduces air pollution. One bus can carry dozens of people, using less fuel per person than many cars. Third, public transport is cheaper than driving a car. People do not need to pay for petrol, parking, or car repairs. For students and low-income families, this is very important. However, public transport also has problems. In rush hours, buses and subways are often crowded. People may have to wait a long time or stand for the whole journey. Some routes do not cover all areas, so people still need cars or taxis. Safety and cleanliness are also concerns in some cities. To improve public transport, cities need to invest more money. They can build more subway lines, add more buses, and make stations cleaner and safer. They can also use technology, such as real-time information, to help passengers plan their journeys. People can also help by using public transport more often and respecting other passengers. A good public transport system makes a city more liveable for everyone. In addition, good public transport can help older people and people with disabilities live independently. Low-floor buses, lifts in subway stations, and clear signs make travel easier for everyone. When public transport is accessible, more people can go to work, visit friends, and take part in community life. Cities should listen to passengers and improve services based on their needs. A transport system that works for the most vulnerable people works better for everyone, and it shows that a city cares about all its citizens.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is an advantage of public transport?", "options": ["It increases traffic jams", "It makes air dirtier", "It reduces traffic and air pollution", "It is more expensive than driving"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：公共交通缓解拥堵；B 错误：公交比多辆汽车更清洁；C 正确：公共交通减少交通拥堵和空气污染；D 错误：公共交通比开车便宜。"}, {"prompt": "Why is public transport important for students?", "options": ["It is always very expensive", "Students never use it", "It requires buying a car", "It is cheaper than driving a car"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：公共交通便宜；B 错误：学生常用公共交通；C 错误：乘公交不需买车；D 正确：公共交通比开车便宜，对学生和低收入家庭很重要。"}, {"prompt": "What problem happens in rush hours?", "options": ["Public transport is empty", "People never wait", "Everyone gets a seat", "Buses and subways are often crowded"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：高峰时人多；B 错误：高峰时需等待；C 错误：高峰时常需站立；D 正确：高峰时段公交地铁常拥挤。"}, {"prompt": "How can cities improve public transport?", "options": ["Remove all buses", "Close all stations", "Stop investing money", "Build more subway lines and add more buses"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：应增加公交；B 错误：应改善车站；C 错误：应增加投资；D 正确：城市应建更多地铁线、增加公交、改善车站。"}, {"prompt": "What is the main idea?", "options": ["Public transport is useless", "Cars are always better", "Cities should remove public transport", "A good public transport system makes a city more liveable"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：公共交通很重要；B 错误：公共交通有诸多优点；C 错误：应发展公共交通；D 正确：好的公共交通系统让城市更宜居。"}]}]}, {"no": 17, "passages": [{"title": "The Importance of Sleep for Students", "text": "Sleep is often the first thing students give up when they are busy. They stay up late to study, watch videos, or chat with friends. However, scientists say sleep is as important as food and exercise. During sleep, the brain stores memories, repairs the body, and prepares for the next day. Students who sleep well learn better. They can concentrate in class, remember new words, and solve problems more quickly. Students who do not sleep enough often feel tired, moody, and unable to focus. They may also get sick more easily. Research shows that teenagers and young adults need about eight hours of sleep each night. However, many students sleep only six hours or less. To sleep better, students should keep regular hours. Going to bed and waking up at the same time every day helps the body form a habit. They should avoid caffeine, such as coffee and strong tea, in the evening. They should also stop using phones and computers at least thirty minutes before bed, because the light and exciting content make sleep harder. A dark, quiet, and cool bedroom helps sleep. Some students find that reading a paper book or listening to soft music before bed helps them relax. Sleep is not a waste of time. It is an investment in learning and health. Students who protect their sleep often find they need less time to study, because a rested brain works better. In addition, napping can help students who are very tired, but it should be short. A nap of twenty minutes in the early afternoon can refresh the mind without affecting night sleep. A long nap late in the day, by contrast, can make it hard to fall asleep at night. Students should also avoid studying in bed, because the brain needs to associate the bed with sleep, not with work. A regular routine, a comfortable bed, and a calm mind are the best tools for good sleep and good learning.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What does the brain do during sleep?", "options": ["It stops working completely", "It forgets everything", "It makes people more tired", "It stores memories, repairs the body and prepares for the next day"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：睡眠时大脑仍在工作；B 错误：睡眠帮助记忆；C 错误：睡眠使人恢复精力；D 正确：睡眠时大脑储存记忆、修复身体、为次日做准备。"}, {"prompt": "How much sleep do young adults need?", "options": ["About three hours", "About twelve hours every day", "No sleep at all", "About eight hours each night"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：三小时太少；B 错误：八小时是建议量；C 错误：睡眠至关重要；D 正确：青少年和年轻人每晚约需八小时睡眠。"}, {"prompt": "What should students avoid in the evening?", "options": ["Drinking water", "Reading a paper book", "Listening to soft music", "Caffeine such as coffee and strong tea"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：喝水有益；B 错误：读纸质书有助放松；C 错误：听轻音乐有助睡眠；D 正确：晚上应避免咖啡和浓茶等咖啡因。"}, {"prompt": "Why should students stop using phones before bed?", "options": ["Because phones help sleep at once", "Because phones are too heavy", "Because phones have no light", "Because light and exciting content make sleep harder"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：手机会干扰睡眠；B 错误：重量不是原因；C 错误：手机会发光；D 正确：手机的光线和兴奋内容使入睡更难。"}, {"prompt": "What is the author's view of sleep?", "options": ["Sleep is a waste of time", "Sleep is an investment in learning and health", "Students should sleep less to study more", "Sleep has no effect on learning"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：睡眠不是浪费时间；B 正确：睡眠是对学习和健康的投资，休息好的大脑工作更高效；C 错误：睡眠不足影响学习；D 错误：睡眠影响学习。"}]}, {"title": "Robots in Our Daily Life", "text": "Robots were once only seen in science fiction films. Today, they are becoming part of daily life. In factories, robots build cars and electronic products quickly and accurately. In hospitals, robots help doctors perform operations and deliver medicine. In homes, robot vacuum cleaners clean floors, and some robots help old people by reminding them to take medicine. Robots have many advantages. They can do dangerous, boring, or repetitive work without getting tired. They can work in places that are too hot, too cold, or too dangerous for humans. They can also help people with disabilities to live more independently. However, robots also raise worries. Some people fear robots will take away jobs. If robots can do factory work, drivers' work, and even some office work, many workers may lose their jobs. Others worry about safety. What happens if a robot makes a mistake? Who is responsible? There are also questions about privacy, because some robots collect information about people. Experts say robots will change jobs, but they will also create new jobs. People will be needed to design, build, and repair robots. The key is education. Workers need to learn new skills to work with robots, not against them. In the future, robots are likely to become more common. If we use them wisely, they can make life easier, safer, and more comfortable for everyone. In addition, robots are helping in agriculture. They can plant seeds, pick fruit, and check the health of crops. This helps farmers work more efficiently and use less water and chemicals. In disaster areas, robots can search for people in dangerous buildings where humans cannot go safely. As robots become smarter, they will help in more areas of life. The challenge is to make sure robots are used to help people, not to replace human care and creativity. Humans and robots working together can achieve more than either could alone.", "questions": [{"prompt": "Where are robots used today?", "options": ["Only in films", "Nowhere at all", "Only in space", "In factories, hospitals and homes"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误：机器人已进入日常生活；B 错误：机器人应用广泛；C 错误：机器人不只在太空使用；D 正确：机器人在工厂、医院和家庭中都有应用。"}, {"prompt": "What is an advantage of robots?", "options": ["They can do dangerous or repetitive work without getting tired", "They always get tired quickly", "They cannot work in dangerous places", "They make work slower"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：机器人能做危险、枯燥或重复的工作而不疲倦；B 错误：机器人不知疲倦；C 错误：机器人可在危险环境工作；D 错误：机器人工作快速准确。"}, {"prompt": "What worry is mentioned about jobs?", "options": ["Robots will create no new jobs", "Robots may take away jobs from workers", "Robots cannot do any work", "Workers will never need new skills"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：机器人也会创造新工作；B 正确：有人担心机器人会夺走工作；C 错误：机器人能做许多工作；D 错误：工人需要学习新技能。"}, {"prompt": "What other worry is discussed?", "options": ["Safety and privacy", "Robots are too cheap", "Robots never make mistakes", "Robots collect no information"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：文章提到安全问题和隐私问题；B 错误：价格不是担忧；C 错误：机器人可能出错；D 错误：有些机器人收集个人信息。"}, {"prompt": "What do experts suggest?", "options": ["Workers need to learn new skills to work with robots", "Workers should refuse all technology", "Robots should be banned", "Education is not needed"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：专家认为工人需学习新技能与机器人合作；B 错误：应学会与机器人合作；C 错误：机器人会越来越普遍；D 错误：教育是关键。"}]}]}, {"no": 18, "passages": [{"title": "Learning to Manage Money", "text": "For many college students, managing money is a new challenge. For the first time, they receive living expenses from their families and must decide how to spend them. Some students spend too much in the first week and have little left for the rest of the month. Others save carefully and never enjoy life. Learning to manage money is an important life skill. The first step is to make a budget. Students should list their income and their fixed costs, such as food, transport, and phone bills. Then they can decide how much to spend on other things, such as clothes, entertainment, and books. A budget helps them see where money goes and avoid overspending. The second step is to save a little every month, even if it is only a small amount. Saving regularly builds a habit and provides money for emergencies, such as a broken phone or a medical bill. The third step is to think before buying. Students should ask themselves whether they really need something, or whether they only want it because of an advertisement or a friend's influence. Waiting a few days before buying something expensive can prevent regret. Students should also be careful with online payment and credit. It is easy to spend money with one click, and borrowing money can lead to debt. Managing money does not mean never spending. It means spending wisely, saving regularly, and planning for the future. Students who learn this skill early will have less stress and more freedom in life. In addition, students can learn about money by keeping a simple record of spending. Writing down every expense for one month often surprises people: small purchases, such as snacks and drinks, can add up to a large amount. Once students see where their money goes, they can decide what to cut and what to keep. Some students use apps to track spending, while others prefer a notebook. Either way, the habit of recording spending builds awareness. Over time, this awareness leads to better choices and greater financial freedom in adult life.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is a common money problem for students?", "options": ["Spending too much in the first week and having little left", "Saving too much money", "Never spending any money", "Always having enough money"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：有些学生第一周花太多，余下时间所剩无几；B 错误：存钱过多不是常见问题；C 错误：从不花钱不是问题；D 错误：许多学生面临缺钱问题。"}, {"prompt": "What is the first step in managing money?", "options": ["Borrowing money from friends", "Making a budget listing income and fixed costs", "Spending without any plan", "Ignoring all expenses"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：借钱不是第一步；B 正确：第一步是做预算，列出收入和固定支出；C 错误：应有计划；D 错误：应了解支出。"}, {"prompt": "Why should students save a little every month?", "options": ["To build a habit and have money for emergencies", "Saving is useless", "Emergencies never happen", "Students should spend all money at once"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：定期存钱养成习惯，并为突发情况提供资金；B 错误：存钱有益；C 错误：突发情况可能发生；D 错误：应定期储蓄。"}, {"prompt": "What should students ask before buying?", "options": ["Whether they really need it or only want it because of ads", "Whether it is the most expensive item", "Whether friends will laugh at them", "Whether they can borrow money to buy it"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：购买前应问自己是否真的需要，还是只因广告或朋友影响；B 错误：价格不是唯一标准；C 错误：不应在意他人嘲笑；D 错误：不应借钱购买。"}, {"prompt": "What does managing money mean?", "options": ["Never spending money", "Spending wisely, saving regularly and planning for the future", "Spending all money at once", "Borrowing as much as possible"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：理财不是从不花钱；B 正确：理财意味着明智消费、定期储蓄、规划未来；C 错误：应避免一次花光；D 错误：借钱会导致债务。"}]}, {"title": "The Value of Reading News", "text": "In the age of social media, many young people get news from short videos and posts. They see headlines, but they rarely read full articles. However, reading news carefully is still important. News helps people understand what is happening in the world, in their country, and in their community. It helps them make better decisions, such as voting, choosing a job, or protecting their health. Reading news also helps students in their studies. It gives them examples, new words, and different views on important issues. A student who reads news regularly can write better essays and join discussions with confidence. However, not all news is reliable. Some news is false or one-sided. Some headlines are made to attract clicks, not to tell the truth. Readers need to think critically. They should check the source, compare different reports, and ask whether the evidence is strong. They should also be careful about their own biases. People often believe news that matches their own opinions and ignore news that does not. Good news reading means reading widely, thinking carefully, and keeping an open mind. It also means not spending too much time on bad news. Too much negative news can make people feel anxious. The goal is to stay informed, not to be overwhelmed. Reading news is a habit that helps people become responsible citizens. In addition, reading news from different countries can broaden a person's view of the world. A story that seems simple in one country may look different from another perspective. By reading international news, people learn about other cultures, economies, and political systems. This helps them become more tolerant and better informed citizens. Students who read world news are better prepared for global work and study. In a connected world, understanding other countries is not a luxury; it is a necessary skill for the future.", "questions": [{"prompt": "How do many young people get news today?", "options": ["From short videos and posts on social media", "Only from newspapers", "Never from any source", "Only from books"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：许多年轻人从短视频和帖子获取新闻；B 错误：年轻人多从社交媒体获取新闻；C 错误：年轻人会看新闻；D 错误：新闻来源多样。"}, {"prompt": "Why is reading news important for students?", "options": ["It gives examples, new words and different views", "It makes essays worse", "It stops students from discussing", "It has no effect on study"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：新闻提供例子、新词和不同观点，有助写作和讨论；B 错误：读新闻能提高写作；C 错误：读新闻有助讨论；D 错误：读新闻对学习有益。"}, {"prompt": "What problem about news is mentioned?", "options": ["All news is always reliable", "Some news is false or one-sided", "Headlines always tell the truth", "There is no false news"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：并非所有新闻都可靠；B 正确：有些新闻是虚假或片面的；C 错误：有些标题只为吸引点击；D 错误：存在虚假新闻。"}, {"prompt": "What should readers do?", "options": ["Check the source and compare different reports", "Believe every headline at once", "Ignore all evidence", "Only read news that matches their opinions"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：读者应核查来源、比较不同报道、看证据是否充分；B 错误：不应轻信标题；C 错误：应重视证据；D 错误：应广泛阅读、保持开放心态。"}, {"prompt": "What is the goal of reading news?", "options": ["To stay informed, not to be overwhelmed", "To feel anxious all the time", "To spend all day on bad news", "To ignore the world"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：目标是了解情况而不被压垮；B 错误：过多负面新闻会让人焦虑；C 错误：不应花太多时间看坏消息；D 错误：读新闻是了解世界。"}]}]}, {"no": 19, "passages": [{"title": "Protecting Wildlife", "text": "Wildlife is an important part of nature. Animals and plants keep the natural world in balance. Bees help plants grow, birds control insects, and forests provide homes for many species. However, many wild animals are in danger. Their homes are being destroyed, they are hunted for food or money, and the climate is changing. Some species, such as pandas, elephants, and tigers, have become rare. If we lose them, the balance of nature will be broken, and human life will also be affected. Protecting wildlife is not only the job of governments. Everyone can help. First, people should not buy products made from wild animals, such as ivory or fur. When there is no market, hunting will decrease. Second, people should protect natural habitats. They should not litter in forests or parks, and they should support nature reserves. Third, people can learn about wildlife and tell others why it matters. Schools can organise trips to zoos and nature parks to teach students about animals. Many countries have made laws to protect endangered animals and punish illegal hunting. International cooperation is also important, because animals do not know borders. Protecting wildlife takes time and effort, but it is worth it. A world with rich wildlife is more beautiful, healthier, and more interesting for everyone. In addition, zoos and nature parks play a role in protecting wildlife. They care for endangered animals, help them breed, and teach visitors about nature. Some animals born in zoos are later released into the wild. However, zoos cannot replace natural habitats. The best protection is to keep wild places safe and healthy. People who visit nature parks should follow rules, stay on paths, and not disturb animals. Respect for wildlife begins with small actions in our daily lives.", "questions": [{"prompt": "Why is wildlife important?", "options": ["Animals and plants keep nature in balance", "Wildlife has no effect on nature", "Only humans matter", "Animals are useless to humans"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：动植物维持自然平衡，蜜蜂助植物生长、鸟类控制昆虫；B 错误：野生动物对自然重要；C 错误：动植物都重要；D 错误：野生动物有益人类。"}, {"prompt": "Why are many wild animals in danger?", "options": ["Their homes are destroyed, they are hunted and climate is changing", "They have too many homes", "No one hunts them", "Climate never changes"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：栖息地被毁、被猎杀、气候变化使许多野生动物濒危；B 错误：栖息地在减少；C 错误：偷猎是威胁；D 错误：气候在变化。"}, {"prompt": "What can people do about buying products?", "options": ["Buy more ivory and fur", "Not buy products made from wild animals", "Encourage hunting", "Ignore the market"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：不应买象牙皮毛；B 正确：不应购买野生动物制品，没有市场则猎杀减少；C 错误：应反对猎杀；D 错误：应关注市场影响。"}, {"prompt": "How can people protect habitats?", "options": ["Litter in forests and parks", "Not litter and support nature reserves", "Destroy nature reserves", "Pollute rivers and forests"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：不应乱扔垃圾；B 正确：不应在森林公园乱扔垃圾，应支持自然保护区；C 错误：应保护保护区；D 错误：应保护环境。"}, {"prompt": "What is the author's view?", "options": ["Protecting wildlife is worth the time and effort", "Wildlife protection is useless", "Only governments should act", "Animals do not need protection"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：保护野生动物值得付出时间和努力；B 错误：保护野生动物重要；C 错误：人人都能帮忙；D 错误：野生动物需要保护。"}]}, {"title": "The Changing World of Work", "text": "The world of work is changing fast. New technology, such as artificial intelligence and robots, is changing many jobs. Some jobs will disappear, while new jobs will appear. For example, drivers may be replaced by self-driving cars, but new jobs in data, programming, and robot repair will grow. At the same time, more people are working in flexible ways. Some work from home, some work part-time, and some work as freelancers for different companies. Lifelong learning has become more important than ever. In the past, people could learn one skill and use it for their whole career. Today, skills become old quickly. Workers need to keep learning new skills throughout their lives. Universities and companies should help workers learn and adapt. Students should also prepare for change. They should not only learn knowledge from books, but also learn how to learn, how to solve problems, and how to work with others. These abilities will help them in any job. Communication, creativity, and critical thinking are becoming more valuable. The future of work is uncertain, but it is also full of chances. People who are curious, flexible, and willing to learn will find new opportunities. The key is not to fear change, but to prepare for it. In addition, remote work is changing where people live. Some workers now live in smaller towns or villages because they do not need to go to an office every day. This can bring new life to rural areas and reduce pressure on big cities. However, remote workers still need good internet, quiet space, and regular contact with colleagues. Companies must learn to manage remote teams fairly. The future of work will be a mix of office and home, of human skills and machine help, and of old jobs and new ones.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is changing many jobs?", "options": ["New technology such as AI and robots", "Only traditional tools", "Nothing at all", "Only paper and pens"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：人工智能和机器人等新技术正在改变许多工作；B 错误：新技术是变化原因；C 错误：工作世界在快速变化；D 错误：技术是关键因素。"}, {"prompt": "What new jobs may grow?", "options": ["Jobs in data, programming and robot repair", "Jobs for drivers only", "No new jobs at all", "Jobs that need no skills"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：数据、编程和机器人维修等新工作将会增长；B 错误：司机工作可能被取代；C 错误：新工作会出现；D 错误：新工作需要技能。"}, {"prompt": "Why has lifelong learning become important?", "options": ["Skills become old quickly and workers need to keep learning", "People can use one skill forever", "Learning is no longer needed", "Skills never change"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：技能很快过时，工人需终身学习新技能；B 错误：过去可一生用一技，现在不行；C 错误：学习更重要；D 错误：技能在快速变化。"}, {"prompt": "What abilities should students learn besides book knowledge?", "options": ["How to learn, solve problems and work with others", "Only memorisation", "How to avoid communication", "How to stop thinking"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：学生应学会如何学习、解决问题和与人合作；B 错误：仅记忆不够；C 错误：沟通能力越来越重要；D 错误：批判性思维很重要。"}, {"prompt": "What is the key to the future of work?", "options": ["To fear change", "To prepare for change with curiosity and flexibility", "To stop learning", "To ignore new technology"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：不应害怕变化；B 正确：关键不是害怕变化而是做好准备，保持好奇、灵活、乐于学习；C 错误：应持续学习；D 错误：应适应新技术。"}]}]}, {"no": 20, "passages": [{"title": "Graduation and the Future", "text": "Graduation is an important moment for university students. After years of study, they leave campus and start a new life. For many students, graduation brings both excitement and worry. They are excited about freedom, new jobs, and new cities. At the same time, they worry about finding a good job, earning enough money, and living independently. Finding a job is not easy. Many graduates send out dozens of resumes and attend many interviews before they find work. Some choose to continue studying for a higher degree. Others start their own businesses. Whatever they choose, graduates need to be prepared. They should think about their strengths, interests, and goals. They should also learn practical skills, such as writing a good resume, communicating clearly, and working in a team. Experience matters too. Internships, part-time jobs, and volunteer work can help graduates stand out. Graduation is not the end of learning. In a fast-changing world, people need to keep learning throughout their lives. The knowledge from university is a foundation, but graduates must keep building on it. Friends and teachers can offer support during this time. Talking about worries and sharing advice can make the change easier. Graduation is a new beginning. With preparation, confidence, and a willingness to learn, graduates can face the future and build a life they are proud of. In addition, graduates should keep in touch with classmates and teachers. These contacts can become a valuable network for jobs, advice, and support. Alumni groups, social media, and reunions help graduates stay connected. A former classmate may recommend a job, a teacher may write a reference, and a friend may offer encouragement during a difficult time. Building and maintaining relationships is as important as building skills. A strong network, built on trust and kindness, can support a graduate throughout their career.", "questions": [{"prompt": "How do many graduates feel?", "options": ["Only excited, never worried", "Only worried, never excited", "Both excited and worried", "Neither excited nor worried"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误：毕业既兴奋又担忧；B 错误：毕业既兴奋又担忧；C 正确：许多毕业生既对自由、新工作感到兴奋，又担心找工作和独立生活；D 错误：毕业带来复杂感受。"}, {"prompt": "What do graduates need to be prepared for finding a job?", "options": ["Think about strengths and learn practical skills", "Only wait for jobs to come", "Never write a resume", "Avoid all interviews"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：毕业生应思考优势兴趣目标，学习写简历、沟通和团队合作等实用技能；B 错误：找工作需主动准备；C 错误：应写好简历；D 错误：应参加面试。"}, {"prompt": "What experience can help graduates stand out?", "options": ["Internships, part-time jobs and volunteer work", "No experience at all", "Only playing games", "Never working"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：实习、兼职和志愿工作能让毕业生脱颖而出；B 错误：经验很重要；C 错误：玩游戏不是优势；D 错误：工作经验有益。"}, {"prompt": "What is graduation according to the author?", "options": ["The end of learning", "A new beginning, not the end of learning", "A time to stop studying forever", "A useless ceremony"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：毕业不是学习的终点；B 正确：毕业是新的开始，仍需终身学习；C 错误：应继续学习；D 错误：毕业是重要时刻。"}, {"prompt": "What can help graduates during this change?", "options": ["Friends and teachers offering support and advice", "Facing everything alone", "Never talking about worries", "Ignoring all support"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：朋友和老师的支持、分享建议能让转变更容易；B 错误：他人支持有帮助；C 错误：谈论担忧有帮助；D 错误：应接受支持。"}]}, {"title": "Data Privacy in the Digital Age", "text": "Every day, people share a large amount of personal information online. They post photos, locations, and opinions on social media. They shop, bank, and study online. Companies collect this data to improve services, show advertisements, and understand customers. However, data privacy has become a serious concern. Personal information can be stolen by hackers and used for fraud. It can also be sold or shared without people's knowledge. Once information is online, it is hard to take it back. To protect privacy, people should be careful about what they share. They should not post their address, phone number, or ID information online. They should use strong passwords, change them regularly, and turn on two-step verification. They should also check privacy settings on apps and websites, and think twice before giving permission to collect data. Companies and governments also have a duty. Companies should protect user data, be clear about how they use it, and ask for permission before collecting it. Governments should make laws to protect privacy and punish those who misuse data. Data privacy is not only a technical issue. It is about trust and respect. People should be able to use the internet without fear that their personal life will be exposed. Protecting privacy requires everyone: users, companies, and governments working together. In addition, parents and teachers should help young people understand data privacy. Children often share photos and personal details online without thinking about the risks. Schools can teach students to think before they post, to use privacy settings, and to ask for help if something goes wrong. Young people who learn good digital habits early will be safer online as adults. Privacy education is as important as learning to read and write in the digital age, because personal data is a valuable part of a person's life.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What do companies do with personal data?", "options": ["Collect it to improve services and show ads", "Never collect any data", "Only delete all data", "Ignore all customers"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：公司收集数据以改进服务、展示广告、了解客户；B 错误：公司会收集数据；C 错误：公司收集并使用数据；D 错误：公司利用数据了解客户。"}, {"prompt": "What risk is mentioned?", "options": ["Personal information can be stolen and used for fraud", "Data is always safe online", "Hackers never steal data", "Information can always be taken back easily"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：个人信息可能被黑客窃取用于诈骗；B 错误：数据有被盗风险；C 错误：黑客会窃取数据；D 错误：信息上网后很难收回。"}, {"prompt": "What should people not post online?", "options": ["Their address, phone number or ID information", "Photos of landscapes", "General opinions", "Public news"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：不应在网上发布地址、电话或身份证信息；B 错误：风景照相对安全；C 错误：一般观点可分享；D 错误：公开新闻可分享。"}, {"prompt": "What should companies do?", "options": ["Protect user data and be clear about how they use it", "Sell data without permission", "Hide how they use data", "Ignore user privacy"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：公司应保护用户数据、说明用途、收集前征得同意；B 错误：不应未经许可出售数据；C 错误：应透明说明；D 错误：公司有责任保护隐私。"}, {"prompt": "What is data privacy about according to the author?", "options": ["Only technology", "Trust and respect, requiring users, companies and governments together", "Only companies", "Only governments"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：数据隐私不只是技术问题；B 正确：数据隐私关乎信任和尊重，需要用户、企业和政府共同努力；C 错误：不只企业有责任；D 错误：不只政府有责任。"}]}]}, {"no": 21, "passages": [{"title": "The Benefits of Learning a Second Language", "text": "Learning a second language is good for the brain and for life. Studies show that people who speak two languages have better memory, attention, and problem-solving skills. They can switch between tasks more easily and think more flexibly. Learning a language also opens doors to new cultures. When you learn a language, you learn about the people who speak it: their food, music, festivals, and ways of thinking. This helps you understand the world better and communicate with more people. A second language can also help your career. Many companies look for workers who can speak more than one language, especially in trade, tourism, and technology. Speaking another language can give you more job chances and a higher salary. Learning a language is not easy. It takes time, practice, and patience. Many learners feel shy about speaking and fear making mistakes. However, mistakes are a natural part of learning. The best way to learn is to use the language every day: listen, speak, read, and write, even if it is only for a short time. Talking with native speakers, watching films, and reading books in the language all help. The earlier you start, the easier it may be, but it is never too late to learn. With regular practice, anyone can make progress and enjoy the benefits of speaking another language. In addition, learning a second language can delay the effects of ageing on the brain. Research shows that bilingual people may develop memory problems later than people who speak only one language. Using two languages keeps the brain active and flexible throughout life. For older learners, language study is also a social activity: classes, conversation groups, and travel provide chances to meet people and stay engaged. Language learning is therefore not only for the young; it is a lifelong gift for the brain and for social life.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What benefit does a second language bring to the brain?", "options": ["Better memory, attention and problem-solving skills", "Worse memory", "No effect on the brain", "Less flexible thinking"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：双语者记忆、注意和解决问题能力更好；B 错误：学语言有益大脑；C 错误：学语言对大脑有益；D 错误：双语者思维更灵活。"}, {"prompt": "How does learning a language help understand culture?", "options": ["You learn about food, music, festivals and ways of thinking", "Language has no culture", "Culture is the same everywhere", "Learning a language stops cultural understanding"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：学语言能了解其使用者的食物、音乐、节日和思维方式；B 错误：语言与文化相关；C 错误：各国文化不同；D 错误：学语言增进文化理解。"}, {"prompt": "Why can a second language help a career?", "options": ["Many companies look for workers who speak more than one language", "Companies dislike bilingual workers", "Language skills reduce job chances", "Bilingual workers earn less"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：许多公司青睐会多种语言的员工，尤其在贸易、旅游和技术领域；B 错误：公司喜欢双语员工；C 错误：双语增加工作机会；D 错误：双语可能带来更高薪水。"}, {"prompt": "What do many learners fear?", "options": ["Making mistakes when speaking", "Speaking perfectly at once", "Never making any mistakes", "Learning too quickly"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：许多学习者害怕开口犯错；B 错误：学习者常怕犯错；C 错误：犯错是学习自然部分；D 错误：学习需要时间。"}, {"prompt": "What is the best way to learn according to the passage?", "options": ["Use the language every day, even for a short time", "Study once a year", "Never speak at all", "Avoid all practice"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：每天使用语言，哪怕时间短，听说读写都有帮助；B 错误：应每天练习；C 错误：应多开口；D 错误：练习至关重要。"}]}, {"title": "Healthy Eyes and Good Habits", "text": "Eyes are important for learning and daily life, but many students do not take care of them. They read in poor light, look at screens for hours, and rub their eyes when tired. Over time, these habits can damage eyesight. To keep eyes healthy, students should form good habits. First, they should read and write in good light, neither too bright nor too dark. They should keep books at a proper distance, about 30 centimetres from the eyes. Second, they should take breaks from screens. The 20-20-20 rule is helpful: every 20 minutes, look far away for 20 seconds. Third, they should spend time outdoors. Sunlight helps eyes develop healthily, and looking at distant trees and buildings relaxes the eyes. Fourth, they should not rub their eyes with dirty hands, because this can bring germs and cause infection. Fifth, they should eat food rich in vitamins, such as carrots, spinach, and fish, and drink enough water. Finally, they should have regular eye checks. If vision becomes blurred, they should see a doctor early instead of waiting until the problem becomes serious. Good eyesight helps students read, study, and enjoy life. Protecting eyes is a lifelong habit that starts with small daily choices. In addition, eye health is linked to overall health. People with diabetes or high blood pressure should have regular eye checks, because these conditions can affect vision. Eating a balanced diet, exercising, and not smoking also help protect eyes. Students who wear glasses should keep them clean and have their prescription checked regularly. If glasses are broken or lost, they should be replaced quickly. Taking care of eyes is part of taking care of the whole body, and good habits in youth protect vision in old age.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What bad habit can damage eyesight?", "options": ["Reading in good light", "Looking at screens for hours and reading in poor light", "Spending time outdoors", "Eating carrots"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：良好光线下阅读有益；B 正确：光线差时阅读、长时间看屏幕、揉眼都会损害视力；C 错误：户外活动有益眼睛；D 错误：胡萝卜有益眼睛。"}, {"prompt": "How far should books be kept from eyes?", "options": ["About 30 centimetres", "About 3 centimetres", "About 3 metres", "As close as possible"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：书本应保持约30厘米距离；B 错误：3厘米太近；C 错误：3米太远；D 错误：太近有害眼睛。"}, {"prompt": "Why is outdoor time helpful for eyes?", "options": ["Sunlight helps eyes develop and distant views relax eyes", "Outdoors harms eyes", "Children should stay indoors", "Sunlight makes eyes worse"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：阳光助眼睛健康发育，远眺使眼睛放松；B 错误：户外有益眼睛；C 错误：应多户外活动；D 错误：阳光有益眼睛发育。"}, {"prompt": "Why should students not rub eyes with dirty hands?", "options": ["It can bring germs and cause infection", "It cleans the eyes", "It improves vision", "It has no effect"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：脏手揉眼会带入细菌、引起感染；B 错误：脏手会污染眼睛；C 错误：揉眼不会改善视力；D 错误：揉眼有害。"}, {"prompt": "What should students do if vision becomes blurred?", "options": ["Wait until it becomes serious", "See a doctor early", "Ignore it completely", "Rub eyes more"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：不应等到严重；B 正确：视力模糊应尽早就医；C 错误：不应忽视；D 错误：揉眼有害。"}]}]}, {"no": 22, "passages": [{"title": "Climate Change and Our Lives", "text": "Climate change is one of the biggest challenges facing the world today. The Earth is getting warmer because people burn coal, oil, and gas, cut down forests, and produce too much waste. This warming causes many problems. Glaciers are melting, sea levels are rising, and weather is becoming more extreme. Some places have more floods, others have longer droughts. Farmers find it harder to grow food, and many animals lose their homes. Climate change affects everyone, but it affects poor people most. They have fewer resources to protect themselves from floods, heat, and food shortages. To slow climate change, countries need to reduce greenhouse gas emissions. They can use more clean energy, such as solar and wind power, and less coal and oil. They can protect forests and plant more trees. People can also help in daily life. They can save electricity, use public transport, eat less meat, and waste less food. These small actions may seem unimportant, but together they make a big difference. Education is also important. When people understand climate change, they are more likely to act. Young people are already leading the change. Many students take part in environmental activities and ask leaders to act. Climate change is a serious problem, but it is not too late to act. With cooperation and effort, we can protect the planet for future generations. In addition, young people can influence climate action by changing their own habits and by speaking up. They can choose to walk, cycle, or use public transport, reduce waste, and eat more plant-based food. They can also join school clubs, take part in clean-up activities, and share what they learn with family and friends. When young people act together, leaders listen. History shows that student movements have changed policies in many countries. The future of the planet depends in part on the choices young people make today.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What causes the Earth to get warmer?", "options": ["Burning fossil fuels, cutting forests and producing waste", "Using solar power", "Planting more trees", "Saving electricity"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：燃烧煤、石油、天然气、砍伐森林和产生过多垃圾使地球变暖；B 错误：太阳能是清洁能源；C 错误：植树有助于减缓变暖；D 错误：节电有助于减缓变暖。"}, {"prompt": "What problems does warming cause?", "options": ["Glaciers melting, sea levels rising and extreme weather", "Cooler weather everywhere", "More food for everyone", "Animals gaining more homes"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：变暖导致冰川融化、海平面上升、极端天气增多；B 错误：地球在变暖；C 错误：农民种粮更难；D 错误：动物失去家园。"}, {"prompt": "Who is affected most by climate change?", "options": ["Rich people", "Poor people with fewer resources", "No one", "Only animals"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：穷人受影响最大；B 正确：气候变化影响所有人，但穷人资源少，更难抵御洪水、高温和粮食短缺；C 错误：气候变化影响所有人；D 错误：人类也受影响。"}, {"prompt": "What can countries do?", "options": ["Use more coal and oil", "Use more clean energy and protect forests", "Cut down more forests", "Increase emissions"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：应减少化石燃料；B 正确：国家可多用太阳能、风能等清洁能源，保护森林、多种树；C 错误：应保护森林；D 错误：应减少排放。"}, {"prompt": "What can people do in daily life?", "options": ["Save electricity, use public transport, eat less meat and waste less food", "Waste more food", "Use more electricity", "Drive cars for short trips"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：日常可节电、乘公共交通、少吃肉、少浪费食物；B 错误：应减少食物浪费；C 错误：应节约用电；D 错误：短途应步行或骑车。"}]}, {"title": "The Power of Music", "text": "Music is a universal language. People from different countries may not speak the same language, but they can enjoy the same song. Music can make people happy, calm, or excited. It can help people relax after a hard day, or give them energy before exercise. Studies show that music affects the brain and the body. Listening to soft music can lower heart rate and reduce stress. Listening to fast music can make people feel more energetic. Music also helps memory. Many students find that listening to music while studying helps them focus, although loud music with words can be distracting. For some people, music is a way to express feelings that are hard to put into words. A song can tell a story of love, sadness, or hope. Learning to play an instrument has extra benefits. It improves coordination, patience, and discipline. Children who learn music often do better in maths and language. Music also brings people together. Families sing together, friends go to concerts, and communities hold music festivals. In hospitals, music is sometimes used to help patients feel better. Music is not just entertainment. It is a part of human life that heals, connects, and inspires people across cultures and generations. In addition, music education in schools is important. When schools cut music lessons to save money, students lose a valuable way to learn and express themselves. Music lessons teach discipline, teamwork, and creativity, and they give students joy. Communities can support music by organising concerts, choirs, and bands for young people. Even singing together in a classroom can build confidence and friendship. A society that values music is a society that values creativity, emotion, and human connection.", "questions": [{"prompt": "Why is music called a universal language?", "options": ["People from different countries can enjoy the same song", "Only one country likes music", "Music needs translation always", "People cannot understand music"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：不同国家的人虽语言不同，却能欣赏同一首歌；B 错误：音乐受各地喜爱；C 错误：音乐无需翻译；D 错误：音乐能被理解。"}, {"prompt": "What can soft music do?", "options": ["Lower heart rate and reduce stress", "Make people more stressed", "Increase heart rate greatly", "Make people feel angry"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：轻音乐能降低心率、减轻压力；B 错误：轻音乐使人放松；C 错误：快音乐使人更有活力；D 错误：音乐使人平静。"}, {"prompt": "What is a possible problem of music while studying?", "options": ["Loud music with words can be distracting", "All music helps focus", "Music never distracts", "Soft music always distracts"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：带歌词的大声音乐可能分散注意力；B 错误：大声音乐会分心；C 错误：音乐可能分心；D 错误：轻音乐有助放松。"}, {"prompt": "What extra benefit does learning an instrument have?", "options": ["It improves coordination, patience and discipline", "It makes children worse at maths", "It stops learning", "It has no benefit"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：学乐器能提高协调性、耐心和自律；B 错误：学音乐的孩子数学和语言常更好；C 错误：学音乐促进学习；D 错误：学乐器有益。"}, {"prompt": "What is music according to the author?", "options": ["Only entertainment", "A part of life that heals, connects and inspires", "Useless for health", "Only for professionals"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：音乐不只是娱乐；B 正确：音乐是人类生活的一部分，能治愈、连接和激励人；C 错误：音乐有益健康；D 错误：音乐属于所有人。"}]}]}, {"no": 23, "passages": [{"title": "Travelling to Learn About Culture", "text": "Travelling is not only about seeing beautiful places. It is also a good way to learn about different cultures. When you visit a new place, you can see how people live, what they eat, how they dress, and how they celebrate festivals. You can visit museums, markets, and old streets. You can talk to local people and listen to their stories. These experiences teach you more than books can. Travelling also teaches tolerance. When you meet people with different customs, you learn to respect differences instead of judging them. You realise that there is more than one way to live a good life. For students, travelling can be a valuable part of education. It broadens their minds, improves their language skills, and makes them more independent. However, travelling also has a cost to the environment. Planes, cars, and hotels use energy and produce waste. Travellers should try to travel responsibly. They should respect local customs, protect the environment, and support local businesses. They should not take photos of people without permission, and they should not damage historical sites. Travelling to learn is different from travelling only to take photos. The best travellers are curious, respectful, and open-minded. They come home not only with photos, but also with new ideas and friendships. In addition, learning a few words of the local language can make travel much richer. Even a simple greeting, thank you, or goodbye in the local language shows respect and opens doors. Local people often respond warmly when visitors try to speak their language. Travellers who learn basic phrases find it easier to ask for help, order food, and make friends. Language is a key to culture: it carries history, humour, and values. A traveller who learns the language, even a little, learns the culture more deeply.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What can travelling teach besides seeing places?", "options": ["About different cultures and how people live", "Nothing new", "Only how to take photos", "Only about hotels"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：旅行能了解不同文化、人们的生活、饮食、服饰和节日；B 错误：旅行能学到很多；C 错误：旅行不只是拍照；D 错误：旅行内容丰富。"}, {"prompt": "What does travelling teach about differences?", "options": ["To respect differences instead of judging them", "To judge others quickly", "To ignore all customs", "To believe only one way of life is right"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：旅行教人尊重差异而非评判；B 错误：应尊重差异；C 错误：应了解当地习俗；D 错误：有多种美好生活方式。"}, {"prompt": "How can travelling help students?", "options": ["It broadens minds, improves language and makes them independent", "It makes them less independent", "It stops learning", "It narrows their minds"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：旅行开阔视野、提高语言能力、增强独立性；B 错误：旅行增强独立性；C 错误：旅行是教育的一部分；D 错误：旅行开阔思维。"}, {"prompt": "What is a cost of travelling to the environment?", "options": ["Planes, cars and hotels use energy and produce waste", "Travelling has no environmental cost", "Travelling cleans the environment", "Hotels use no energy"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：飞机、汽车和酒店耗能并产生垃圾；B 错误：旅行对环境有代价；C 错误：旅行会产生废物；D 错误：酒店耗能。"}, {"prompt": "What should responsible travellers do?", "options": ["Respect customs, protect environment and support local businesses", "Take photos without permission", "Damage historical sites", "Ignore local customs"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：负责任的旅行者应尊重习俗、保护环境、支持当地商家；B 错误：拍照前应征得同意；C 错误：不应损坏历史遗迹；D 错误：应尊重当地习俗。"}]}, {"title": "Cooking at Home", "text": "Cooking at home is becoming popular again among young people. In the past, many young people relied on takeaway food or eating out. Today, more of them are learning to cook simple meals. Cooking at home has many advantages. First, it is healthier. When you cook yourself, you can choose fresh ingredients and control the amount of oil, salt, and sugar. Second, it is cheaper than eating out. A home-cooked meal often costs much less than the same meal in a restaurant. Third, cooking is a useful life skill. It helps young people become independent and take care of themselves. Fourth, cooking can be fun and relaxing. Trying a new recipe, cooking with friends, or making food for family can bring joy and a sense of achievement. However, cooking at home also takes time and practice. Beginners may burn food or make dishes that taste strange. They need to learn basic skills, such as cutting vegetables safely, controlling heat, and following recipes. With practice, they will improve. Cooking at home also helps reduce waste. People can use leftovers to make new dishes instead of throwing food away. In a busy life, cooking at home may seem difficult, but even a simple meal of rice, vegetables, and eggs is a good start. Learning to cook is learning to take care of yourself and the people you love. In addition, cooking together can strengthen family relationships. When parents and children cook together, they talk, laugh, and learn from each other. Children learn maths by measuring ingredients, science by watching food change, and culture by cooking traditional dishes. These shared moments create memories that last a lifetime. In a busy world, cooking together is a simple way to slow down and spend quality time with the people we love. A home-cooked meal is not just food; it is care, made visible on a plate.", "questions": [{"prompt": "Why is cooking at home healthier?", "options": ["You can choose fresh ingredients and control oil, salt and sugar", "Takeaway is always healthier", "Home cooking uses more oil", "Ingredients do not matter"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：自己做饭可选新鲜食材、控制油盐糖；B 错误：外卖常不健康；C 错误：在家做饭可控制用油；D 错误：食材很重要。"}, {"prompt": "Why is cooking cheaper?", "options": ["A home-cooked meal often costs much less than in a restaurant", "Restaurants are always cheaper", "Home cooking needs expensive tools", "Cooking at home wastes money"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：在家做的饭常比餐厅便宜得多；B 错误：餐厅更贵；C 错误：在家做饭成本低；D 错误：在家做饭省钱。"}, {"prompt": "What can cooking bring besides food?", "options": ["Joy and a sense of achievement", "Only stress", "No fun at all", "Only tiredness"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：尝试新菜谱、与朋友做饭、为家人做饭能带来快乐和成就感；B 错误：做饭可以放松；C 错误：做饭有趣；D 错误：做饭带来成就感。"}, {"prompt": "What problem do beginners face?", "options": ["They may burn food or make strange-tasting dishes", "They cook perfectly at once", "They never make mistakes", "Cooking needs no practice"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：初学者可能烧焦食物或做出味道奇怪的菜；B 错误：初学者需要练习；C 错误：初学者会犯错；D 错误：做饭需要练习。"}, {"prompt": "What is a simple meal suggested as a good start?", "options": ["Rice, vegetables and eggs", "Only expensive dishes", "Only takeaway food", "Only desserts"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：米饭、蔬菜和鸡蛋的简单一餐是好的开始；B 错误：简单饭菜即可；C 错误：在家做饭更好；D 错误：应吃均衡膳食。"}]}]}, {"no": 24, "passages": [{"title": "Finding a Job After Graduation", "text": "Finding a first job is a big step for graduates. Many students start preparing long before graduation. They do internships, join clubs, and learn new skills. When they apply for jobs, they need a good resume. A resume should be clear, short, and honest. It should show education, experience, and skills that match the job. Graduates should also prepare for interviews. They should learn about the company, practise answering common questions, and dress neatly. During an interview, they should be confident, polite, and honest. They should ask questions about the job to show interest. Finding a job can take time. Many graduates face rejection. They may send many applications and hear nothing back. This can be disappointing, but it is normal. Graduates should not give up. They can ask for feedback, improve their skills, and try again. They can also consider different kinds of jobs. A first job does not need to be perfect. It is a chance to learn, gain experience, and understand what they really want. Some graduates choose to work in small companies, where they can do many different tasks and learn quickly. Others choose large companies for training and stability. Whatever they choose, the first job is only the beginning of a career. With patience and hard work, graduates can build a successful future. In addition, graduates can use online platforms to find jobs and to show their skills. A well-written online profile, a portfolio of projects, and recommendations from teachers or employers can help graduates stand out. However, online applications should be personal: a generic message sent to hundreds of companies is less effective than a thoughtful application to a few well-chosen employers. Graduates should research each company, explain why they are a good fit, and follow up politely. Quality matters more than quantity in the job search.", "questions": [{"prompt": "When should students start preparing for jobs?", "options": ["Only after graduation", "Long before graduation through internships and skills", "Never prepare at all", "Only on graduation day"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：应毕业前就开始准备；B 正确：许多学生在毕业前很久就通过实习、社团和学习新技能做准备；C 错误：应提前准备；D 错误：准备需提前。"}, {"prompt": "What should a good resume be?", "options": ["Clear, short and honest", "Long, confusing and dishonest", "Empty with no information", "Only about hobbies"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：简历应清晰、简短、诚实，展示与工作匹配的教育、经验和技能；B 错误：简历应清晰诚实；C 错误：简历应有信息；D 错误：简历应包括教育和技能。"}, {"prompt": "How should graduates act in an interview?", "options": ["Be confident, polite and honest", "Be rude and dishonest", "Never ask questions", "Dress carelessly"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：面试时应自信、礼貌、诚实，并提问以示兴趣；B 错误：应礼貌诚实；C 错误：应提问；D 错误：应着装整洁。"}, {"prompt": "What should graduates do after rejection?", "options": ["Give up at once", "Ask for feedback, improve skills and try again", "Stop applying for jobs", "Blame others always"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：不应放弃；B 正确：被拒后可寻求反馈、提升技能、再尝试；C 错误：应继续申请；D 错误：应从自身改进。"}, {"prompt": "What is a first job according to the author?", "options": ["A perfect job for life", "A chance to learn, gain experience and understand what they want", "A useless step", "The end of a career"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：第一份工作不必完美；B 正确：第一份工作是学习、积累经验、了解真正想要什么的机会；C 错误：第一份工作有价值；D 错误：第一份工作只是职业生涯的开始。"}]}, {"title": "Housing for Young People", "text": "Housing is a big issue for young people in many cities. House prices are high, and rents are also rising. Many young workers spend a large part of their salary on rent. Some live far from work to save money, but then they spend a long time commuting. Others share flats with friends to reduce costs. Sharing a flat can be a good solution, but it also needs good communication and clear rules. Young people should think carefully before renting a flat. They should check the location, transport, safety, and the condition of the flat. They should also read the contract carefully and understand their rights. Some cities have built affordable housing for young workers. These flats are smaller and cheaper, but they are close to work and public transport. Governments can also help by controlling rents and giving support to first-time buyers. For young people, buying a house is a long-term goal. It needs saving, planning, and patience. They should not rush into buying a house they cannot afford. Renting first, saving money, and waiting for the right time may be wiser. Housing is not only about having a place to live. It is about security, comfort, and a sense of home. Finding a suitable home is an important step in adult life. In addition, sharing a flat can teach young people important life skills. They learn to negotiate, to share costs fairly, and to respect each other's space and privacy. A written agreement about rent, bills, and cleaning can prevent arguments. When flatmates communicate openly, sharing a home can be enjoyable and affordable. Many lifelong friendships begin as flat-sharing arrangements. With clear rules and mutual respect, a shared flat can be a happy first home for young adults starting their independent lives.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is a housing problem for young people?", "options": ["House prices and rents are high", "Housing is free for everyone", "Young people never pay rent", "Rents are falling quickly"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：房价高、租金也在上涨；B 错误：住房不免费；C 错误：年轻人需付租金；D 错误：租金在上涨。"}, {"prompt": "Why do some young people live far from work?", "options": ["To save money on rent", "To spend more time commuting", "To pay higher rent", "To avoid saving money"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：有些人为省租金住得离工作远；B 错误：长通勤是代价而非目的；C 错误：远住是为了省钱；D 错误：远住是为了省钱。"}, {"prompt": "What should renters check before renting?", "options": ["Location, transport, safety and condition of the flat", "Nothing at all", "Only the colour of walls", "Only the landlord's name"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：租房前应检查位置、交通、安全和房屋状况；B 错误：应仔细检查；C 错误：不只看墙面颜色；D 错误：不只看房东姓名。"}, {"prompt": "What have some cities done?", "options": ["Built affordable housing for young workers", "Removed all housing", "Raised rents for everyone", "Banned young people from renting"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：一些城市为年轻工人建造了经济适用房；B 错误：城市在增加住房；C 错误：政府应控制租金；D 错误：城市帮助年轻人租房。"}, {"prompt": "What advice is given about buying a house?", "options": ["Rush to buy a house you cannot afford", "Rent first, save money and wait for the right time", "Never save money", "Buy the most expensive house at once"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：不应仓促买买不起的房；B 正确：可先租房、存钱、等待合适时机；C 错误：应存钱规划；D 错误：应量力而行。"}]}]}, {"no": 25, "passages": [{"title": "Science and Everyday Life", "text": "Science is often seen as something for experts in laboratories. In fact, science is part of everyday life. When you cook, you use chemistry. When you use a phone, you use physics and electronics. When you take medicine, you use biology and chemistry. Science helps us understand the world and solve problems. For example, science has helped us make clean water, safe food, and effective medicines. It has also helped us build bridges, planes, and computers. Learning science helps students think logically and ask good questions. They learn to observe, test ideas, and find evidence. These skills are useful not only in science, but also in daily life and work. However, some students find science difficult or boring. They think it is only about memorising facts and formulas. In fact, science is about curiosity. A good science teacher can make learning fun by doing experiments and connecting science to real life. Students can also learn science outside school: by watching the stars, growing plants, or trying simple experiments at home. Science is not only for scientists. It is for everyone who wants to understand the world better. With curiosity and careful thinking, anyone can enjoy science. In addition, science helps us make better decisions about health. When people understand how germs spread, they wash their hands and get vaccinated. When they understand nutrition, they choose healthier food. Science also helps us understand the environment and the need to protect it. Citizens who understand science can judge news reports, question false claims, and vote wisely. In a world full of information, scientific thinking is a shield against misinformation and a tool for making good choices in daily life. Curiosity makes science enjoyable for everyone.", "questions": [{"prompt": "How is science part of everyday life?", "options": ["Cooking uses chemistry and phones use physics", "Science is only in laboratories", "Science has no use in daily life", "Only experts need science"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：做饭用化学、用手机用物理和电子学、吃药用生物化学；B 错误：科学是日常生活的一部分；C 错误：科学无处不在；D 错误：科学属于每个人。"}, {"prompt": "What has science helped us make?", "options": ["Clean water, safe food and effective medicines", "Only problems", "Dirty water only", "Unsafe food"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：科学帮助制造清洁水、安全食品和有效药物；B 错误：科学解决问题；C 错误：科学制造清洁水；D 错误：科学保障食品安全。"}, {"prompt": "What skills does learning science teach?", "options": ["To observe, test ideas and find evidence", "Only memorisation", "To avoid asking questions", "To stop thinking logically"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：学科学教人观察、检验想法、寻找证据；B 错误：科学不只是记忆；C 错误：科学鼓励提问；D 错误：科学培养逻辑思维。"}, {"prompt": "Why do some students find science boring?", "options": ["They think it is only about memorising facts and formulas", "Science is always fun for everyone", "Science has no facts", "Students never study science"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：一些学生认为科学只是记忆事实和公式；B 错误：一些学生觉得科学难或无聊；C 错误：科学包含事实；D 错误：学生学习科学。"}, {"prompt": "What is science about according to the author?", "options": ["Only memorising", "Curiosity and understanding the world", "Only laboratories", "Nothing useful"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：科学不只是记忆；B 正确：科学关乎好奇心和理解世界；C 错误：科学在日常生活中；D 错误：科学很有用。"}]}, {"title": "Education for the Future", "text": "Education is changing. In the past, education meant listening to teachers, memorising textbooks, and passing exams. Today, education is becoming more about learning how to learn, how to think, and how to work with others. Technology is changing classrooms. Students can learn online, watch videos, and use apps to practise. Teachers are becoming guides, not just sources of knowledge. They help students find information, ask questions, and solve real problems. In the future, education will need to prepare students for jobs that do not exist yet. This means teaching creativity, critical thinking, communication, and cooperation. These skills cannot be learned only from books. They need practice in real projects, teamwork, and community service. Education should also teach values: honesty, kindness, responsibility, and respect for others. A good education is not only about getting a good job. It is about becoming a good person and a responsible citizen. Lifelong learning will become normal. People will return to school many times in their lives to learn new skills. Education for the future is not only for children. It is for everyone, at every age, who wants to grow and adapt to a changing world. In addition, parents play a key role in education for the future. They can encourage curiosity by answering children's questions, reading with them, and letting them explore the world. They can model lifelong learning by learning new skills themselves. When families value learning, children are more likely to enjoy school and to keep learning as adults. Education is not only the job of schools; it is a shared effort of families, communities, and society. Together, they can prepare young people for a future we cannot yet fully imagine.", "questions": [{"prompt": "How was education in the past?", "options": ["Listening to teachers, memorising textbooks and passing exams", "Learning only online", "No exams at all", "Only learning how to think"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：过去教育指听老师讲课、背课本、通过考试；B 错误：过去教育以课堂为主；C 错误：过去有考试；D 错误：过去重记忆。"}, {"prompt": "How are teachers changing?", "options": ["They are becoming guides, not just sources of knowledge", "They no longer help students", "They only give exams", "They have disappeared"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：教师正成为引导者，而不只是知识来源；B 错误：教师帮助学生找信息、提问、解决问题；C 错误：教师角色在变化；D 错误：教师仍在。"}, {"prompt": "What skills will future education need to teach?", "options": ["Creativity, critical thinking, communication and cooperation", "Only memorisation", "Only exam skills", "No skills at all"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：未来教育需教创造力、批判性思维、沟通与合作；B 错误：仅记忆不够；C 错误：仅考试技能不够；D 错误：未来教育需多种技能。"}, {"prompt": "What values should education teach?", "options": ["Honesty, kindness, responsibility and respect", "Dishonesty and selfishness", "Only competition", "No values at all"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确：教育应教诚实、善良、责任和尊重他人；B 错误：应教正直善良；C 错误：不只竞争；D 错误：价值观很重要。"}, {"prompt": "What is a good education about according to the author?", "options": ["Only getting a good job", "Becoming a good person and a responsible citizen", "Only passing exams", "Only earning money"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。A 错误：好教育不只是找好工作；B 正确：好教育是成为好人、负责任的公民；C 错误：好教育不只是通过考试；D 错误：好教育不只是赚钱。"}]}]}, {"no": 26, "passages": [{"title": "The Library That Never Sleeps", "text": "When the final examination season arrives at Greenfield University, the central library becomes the busiest place on campus. Lights stay on until midnight, and hundreds of students arrive early in the morning to find a quiet seat near the windows. Some bring laptops and notebooks, while others carry only a bottle of water and a thick textbook. The library offers more than books. On the second floor, there are group study rooms where students can discuss projects without disturbing others. On the third floor, there is a silent zone where even whispering is not allowed. Librarians also provide free workshops on how to search for academic papers and how to manage references correctly. Many first-year students say they did not understand how to use the library at first. They thought it was only a place to borrow books. After attending a short introduction course, they discovered study guides, past examination papers, and online databases. These resources save time and help students prepare more effectively. However, the library also faces problems. During busy weeks, seats are hard to find, and some students leave their bags on chairs for hours while they go to lunch. The university has introduced a new rule: seats left empty for more than thirty minutes can be used by others. Most students support this rule because it is fair. Others suggest building more study spaces in empty classrooms. The library director says the goal is not simply to provide more seats but to create an environment where students feel calm and focused. A good library, she believes, should support both independent study and teamwork. In recent years, the library has also added comfortable chairs, charging points, and a small cafe on the ground floor. Students can take a short break, drink coffee, and return to their work feeling refreshed. For many of them, the library is no longer just a building. It has become a daily habit and an important part of university life.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the main purpose of the passage?", "options": ["To explain how to write an academic paper", "To compare different universities in the country", "To describe how a university library supports students during examinations", "To introduce the history of Greenfield University"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，文章只提到图书馆提供相关讲座，并非讲解写作；B 错误，文章没有比较不同大学；C 正确，文章主要介绍图书馆在考试季为学生提供的服务与环境；D 错误，文章没有介绍大学历史。"}, {"prompt": "What can students do on the second floor of the library?", "options": ["Borrow novels for fun", "Discuss projects in group study rooms", "Sleep in comfortable chairs", "Buy coffee and snacks"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，文中说二楼有小组讨论室可讨论项目；A 错误，二楼并非借小说的地方；C 错误，睡觉不是二楼的功能；D 错误，咖啡厅在一楼。"}, {"prompt": "Why do many first-year students change their view of the library?", "options": ["Because they find the library too noisy", "Because they attend an introduction course and discover useful resources", "Because they are forced to study there every day", "Because the library stops lending books"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，参加介绍课程后他们发现学习指南、往年试题和数据库；A 错误，文章未说图书馆太吵使他们改观；C 错误，没有强制每天学习；D 错误，图书馆并未停止借书。"}, {"prompt": "What problem does the library face during busy weeks?", "options": ["There are not enough books for students", "The cafe closes too early in the evening", "Seats are hard to find and some are occupied by bags", "Librarians refuse to help students"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。C 正确，文中说座位难找且有人用书包占座；A 错误，问题不是书不够；B 错误，未提咖啡厅提前关门；D 错误，馆员并未拒绝帮助。"}, {"prompt": "What does the library director believe a good library should do?", "options": ["Provide only silent rooms for study", "Support both independent study and teamwork", "Keep all students away from the cafe", "Limit the number of books each student can borrow"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，馆长认为好图书馆应同时支持独立学习与团队合作；A 错误，她并未说只提供安静房间；C 错误，她没有说要让学生远离咖啡厅；D 错误，未提限制借书数量。"}]}, {"title": "Learning English Through Short Videos", "text": "For many college students, learning English used to mean memorizing long word lists and reading thick textbooks. Today, short videos have changed the way they study. A three-minute video can explain a grammar point, introduce new expressions, or show a real conversation between native speakers. Students can watch it on the bus, during lunch, or before going to bed. Because the videos are short, it is easier to stay focused and to repeat difficult parts. Li Ming, a second-year student, watches one video every morning. He first listens without subtitles to test his understanding. Then he watches again with English subtitles and writes down useful phrases in a notebook. Finally, he tries to use two or three new phrases when he talks with his classmates. After three months, he feels more confident when speaking. His teacher also notices that his pronunciation has improved. However, short videos are not perfect. Some videos are made only for entertainment and contain incorrect English or slang that is not suitable for formal writing. If students watch them without guidance, they may learn mistakes. Teachers suggest choosing videos from reliable channels and checking new words in a dictionary. It is also important to do more than just watch. Students should speak, write, and review regularly. Watching alone will not make English improve quickly. Another advantage of short videos is that they cover many topics. Students can learn about travel, business, science, and daily life. This makes learning more interesting than reading the same textbook again and again. When learners are interested, they are more likely to continue. In the end, short videos are a useful tool, but they work best when they are part of a balanced study plan. A few minutes every day, combined with speaking practice and reading, can lead to steady progress over time.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage?", "options": ["Short videos are useless for learning English", "Short videos can help students learn English if used properly", "Students should stop reading textbooks completely", "Only native speakers can make English videos"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，文章认为短视频有用，但需正确使用并配合其他练习；A 错误，文章肯定了短视频的价值；C 错误，文章未说要完全放弃课本；D 错误，未说只有母语者能制作视频。"}, {"prompt": "How does Li Ming study with short videos?", "options": ["He watches each video once and never reviews it", "He listens first, then watches with subtitles and practices new phrases", "He only reads the comments under the videos", "He translates every video into Chinese"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，文中描述他先裸听，再看字幕记短语，最后练习使用；A 错误，他会重复观看和复习；C 错误，未提只看评论；D 错误，未提逐字翻译。"}, {"prompt": "What problem does the passage mention about some short videos?", "options": ["They are too long for students to finish", "They may contain incorrect English or unsuitable slang", "They are only available on computers", "They never cover useful topics"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，文中说有些视频含错误英语或不适合正式写作的俚语；A 错误，短视频的特点是短；C 错误，未说只能在电脑上看；D 错误，文中说视频覆盖很多话题。"}, {"prompt": "What do teachers suggest students do?", "options": ["Memorize every word in each video", "Watch as many videos as possible without stopping", "Choose reliable channels and check new words in a dictionary", "Avoid speaking English with classmates"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，未要求背下每个词；B 错误，老师未建议无节制观看；C 正确，老师建议选可靠频道并查词典；D 错误，文章鼓励与同学开口练习。"}, {"prompt": "What can be inferred from the last paragraph?", "options": ["Short videos alone are enough to master English", "Interest and regular practice help learners continue and improve", "Textbooks are more interesting than videos", "Students should study English only before bed"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，末段指出兴趣让人坚持、每天几分钟加口语阅读能稳步进步；A 错误，文章说只看视频不够；C 错误，文章说视频比反复读课本更有趣；D 错误，未说只能睡前学习。"}]}]}, {"no": 27, "passages": [{"title": "Why Sleep Matters for Students", "text": "Many students stay up late to finish homework or prepare for examinations. They believe that sleeping less gives them more time to study. However, research shows that this idea is often wrong. Sleep plays a key role in memory and learning. During sleep, the brain organizes new information and moves it into long-term memory. If a student sleeps only four or five hours, much of what was studied during the day may be forgotten. A study at a large university found that students who slept seven to eight hours performed better on tests than those who stayed up all night. The well-rested students could think more clearly and solve problems faster. They also made fewer careless mistakes. Sleep also affects mood and health. People who do not sleep enough often feel tired, anxious, and unable to concentrate. Over time, poor sleep can weaken the immune system and increase the risk of catching a cold. For students, this means missing classes and falling behind. Good sleep habits are not difficult to build. Experts suggest keeping a regular bedtime, even on weekends. They also advise avoiding phones and computers one hour before sleep because the bright screen can keep the brain awake. A dark, quiet room and a comfortable bed can help as well. Some students drink coffee late at night to stay awake. This may help for a short time, but it often makes sleep worse later. A better choice is to plan study time earlier in the day and take short breaks. A twenty-minute nap in the afternoon can also refresh the mind, but a nap longer than one hour may cause grogginess. In short, sleep is not wasted time. It is part of learning. Students who protect their sleep are more likely to remember what they learn, stay healthy, and perform well in examinations.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What does research show about staying up late to study?", "options": ["It always helps students get higher scores", "It often does not help because sleep supports memory", "It has no effect on learning at all", "It is the best way to prepare for exams"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，研究表明睡眠帮助记忆，熬夜常适得其反；A 错误，熬夜并非总能提分；C 错误，睡眠对学习有影响；D 错误，熬夜不是最好的备考方式。"}, {"prompt": "According to the passage, what happens during sleep?", "options": ["The brain stops working completely", "The brain organizes new information into long-term memory", "Students forget everything they learned", "The body produces more coffee"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，睡眠时大脑整理新信息并转入长期记忆；A 错误，大脑并未停止工作；C 错误，睡眠是巩固而非全部遗忘；D 错误，身体不会产生咖啡。"}, {"prompt": "What did the university study find?", "options": ["Sleep time had no link to test scores", "Students who stayed up all night made no mistakes", "Students who slept 7-8 hours did better on tests", "Short sleep improved problem-solving speed"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，睡眠与成绩有关联；B 错误，熬夜者反而易犯粗心错误；C 正确，研究发现睡7-8小时的学生考试表现更好；D 错误，睡眠不足并未提高解题速度。"}, {"prompt": "What advice do experts give about sleep habits?", "options": ["Take a two-hour nap every afternoon", "Drink coffee late at night every day", "Keep a regular bedtime and avoid screens before sleep", "Study only after midnight"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，午睡超过一小时会昏沉；B 错误，深夜喝咖啡会 worsen 睡眠；C 正确，专家建议固定就寝时间、睡前一小时远离屏幕；D 错误，未建议只在午夜后学习。"}, {"prompt": "What is the author's attitude toward sleep?", "options": ["Sleep is wasted time for students", "Sleep is an important part of learning and health", "Sleep is only useful for older people", "Sleep should be replaced by naps"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，作者认为睡眠是学习的一部分，有助记忆与健康；A 错误，作者明确反对睡眠是浪费时间；C 错误，未说睡眠只对老年人有用；D 错误，午睡只是补充，不能取代睡眠。"}]}, {"title": "Running Together: A Campus Club Story", "text": "Every Tuesday and Thursday morning, a group of students meets at the sports field of River College. They are members of the Morning Run Club, a student group started three years ago by a few friends who wanted to exercise regularly. At first, only five people joined. Today, more than eighty students run together each week. The club welcomes runners of all levels. Some members can run ten kilometers without stopping, while others can only run for ten minutes at a time. Coaches do not set strict rules. Instead, they divide runners into small groups based on speed. Each group has a leader who encourages members and makes sure no one is left behind. For many students, the club is not only about exercise. It is also about friendship and support. Chen Wei, a first-year student, says she was shy when she first arrived at college. She did not know many people and often ate lunch alone. After joining the club, she made friends who shared her interest in running. They now study together and help each other prepare for examinations. Running also helps students manage stress. After a long run, many feel more relaxed and ready to focus on their classes. Doctors agree that regular exercise can improve sleep and mood. It can also reduce the risk of many health problems. However, the club reminds members to run safely. Beginners should start slowly, wear proper shoes, and drink enough water. If someone feels pain, they should stop and rest instead of continuing. The club also organizes charity runs once a semester. Members collect donations and run to raise money for children in rural schools. Last year, they raised enough money to buy books and sports equipment. The club shows that a simple activity like running can bring people together, improve health, and help others at the same time.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the main topic of the passage?", "options": ["The history of River College", "How to win a long-distance race", "A campus running club and its benefits", "Why students should avoid exercise"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，未介绍学院历史；B 错误，文章不是教如何赢得比赛；C 正确，文章介绍晨跑社团及其对健康、友谊和公益的好处；D 错误，文章鼓励锻炼而非避免。"}, {"prompt": "How does the club organize runners?", "options": ["By age and major", "By speed into small groups with leaders", "By running alone without any help", "By asking everyone to run ten kilometers"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，俱乐部按速度分组，每组有组长；A 错误，未按年龄和专业分组；C 错误，跑步有组织和组长；D 错误，并非要求每人都跑十公里。"}, {"prompt": "Why did Chen Wei join the club?", "options": ["To become a professional athlete", "To make friends and overcome shyness", "To raise money for herself", "To avoid attending classes"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，陈薇加入后交到朋友，不再孤单；A 错误，她的目标不是成为职业运动员；C 错误，筹款是为乡村儿童；D 错误，未提逃课。"}, {"prompt": "What does the club do once a semester?", "options": ["It closes for a long holiday", "It organizes charity runs to raise money", "It asks members to run alone at night", "It stops all training activities"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，俱乐部每学期组织一次慈善跑筹款；A 错误，未说放长假；C 错误，未要求夜间独自跑；D 错误，未停止训练。"}, {"prompt": "What advice does the club give beginners?", "options": ["Run as fast as possible on the first day", "Start slowly, wear proper shoes, and drink water", "Ignore pain and keep running", "Run without warming up"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，俱乐部提醒初学者慢慢开始、穿合适的鞋、补充水分；A 错误，不建议第一天就跑最快；C 错误，感到疼痛应停下休息；D 错误，未建议不热身就跑。"}]}]}, {"no": 28, "passages": [{"title": "Electric Cars on City Streets", "text": "In many Chinese cities, electric cars have become a common sight. They are quiet, produce no exhaust fumes, and cost less to run than traditional petrol cars. For drivers who travel short distances every day, an electric car can save a large amount of money on fuel. The government has also encouraged people to buy electric cars by offering subsidies and building more charging stations in residential areas and shopping centers. However, electric cars still face challenges. One major concern is driving range. Many drivers worry that the battery will run out during a long trip. Although new models can travel more than five hundred kilometers on a single charge, charging still takes longer than filling a petrol tank. A fast charger may need thirty minutes, while a slow charger can take several hours. Another problem is the price. Electric cars are often more expensive to buy than similar petrol cars, even with subsidies. Some families decide to wait until prices fall further. Battery production also raises environmental questions. Making batteries requires rare materials, and old batteries must be recycled carefully to avoid pollution. Despite these problems, many experts believe electric cars are the future of transport. Technology is improving quickly. Batteries are becoming cheaper and more powerful, and charging networks are growing every year. In some cities, buses and taxis are already electric, which helps reduce air pollution. For ordinary drivers, the decision depends on personal needs. People who drive mainly in the city and can charge at home may find an electric car very convenient. Those who often take long road trips may still prefer a petrol car for now. Over time, as technology improves and prices fall, more people are likely to choose electric cars.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is one advantage of electric cars mentioned in the passage?", "options": ["They are noisy on the road", "They produce no exhaust fumes and cost less to run", "They can be charged in one minute", "They never need any maintenance"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，电动车安静、无尾气、运行成本低；A 错误，电动车是安静的；C 错误，充电仍需时间；D 错误，未说完全不需要维护。"}, {"prompt": "Why do some drivers worry about electric cars?", "options": ["They dislike the color of electric cars", "They think electric cars are too slow in cities", "They are afraid the battery will run out on long trips", "They cannot find petrol stations"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，未提颜色问题；B 错误，未说电动车在城市太慢；C 正确，司机担心长途行驶中电池耗尽；D 错误，电动车不需要加油站。"}, {"prompt": "What does the passage say about charging time?", "options": ["Charging is always faster than filling petrol", "Fast charging may take about thirty minutes", "All chargers take only five minutes", "Charging stations do not exist in cities"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，快充约需三十分钟；A 错误，充电仍比加油慢；C 错误，并非所有充电只需五分钟；D 错误，城市正在建设充电站。"}, {"prompt": "What environmental concern is related to batteries?", "options": ["Batteries are made without any materials", "Old batteries must be recycled carefully to avoid pollution", "Batteries can be thrown away anywhere", "Battery production has no effect on the environment"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，旧电池须谨慎回收以免污染；A 错误，电池生产需要稀有材料；C 错误，不能随意丢弃；D 错误，电池生产确实有环境影响。"}, {"prompt": "Who may find an electric car most convenient now?", "options": ["People who never drive at all", "People who take long road trips every week", "People who drive mainly in the city and can charge at home", "People who live far from any charging station"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，从不驾驶者不需要买车；B 错误，常跑长途者目前更适合油车；C 正确，主要在城内行驶且能在家充电的人最方便；D 错误，远离充电站者使用不便。"}]}, {"title": "Recycling Starts at Home", "text": "Every day, families throw away large amounts of rubbish. Much of it could be reused or recycled, but it often ends up in landfills. Recycling starts at home, and small actions can make a big difference. The first step is sorting waste. In many cities, bins are marked with different colors for kitchen waste, recyclable materials, harmful waste, and other rubbish. Paper, plastic bottles, glass, and metal cans belong in the recycling bin. Food waste should go in a separate bin so it can be turned into fertilizer. Batteries and old medicines are harmful waste and must not be mixed with ordinary rubbish. Many people say sorting is troublesome at first. They have to learn which bin to use and remember to wash bottles before throwing them away. However, after a few weeks, sorting becomes a habit. Children often learn faster than adults because schools teach them about recycling through games and activities. Recycling saves resources and energy. Making new paper from recycled paper uses less wood and water. Recycling aluminum cans saves a great deal of electricity compared with producing new aluminum. It also reduces pollution because less waste is buried or burned. Some communities have started reward programs. Residents who sort correctly can earn points and exchange them for small gifts or shopping coupons. This encourages more people to take part. Still, recycling alone is not enough. People should also reduce waste by using reusable bags, refusing single-use plastic, and buying only what they need. Repairing old clothes and electronics is another good choice. When everyone in a family takes part, recycling becomes part of daily life rather than a burden. A cleaner city begins with a single sorted bin at home. This experience also reminds readers that small daily choices can lead to large changes over time, and that understanding the topic well helps students make better decisions in their own lives and studies. With patience, practice, and support from teachers and friends, learners can turn new knowledge into real skills and use it with confidence in the future.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the main message of the passage?", "options": ["Sorting waste is impossible for families", "Only factories should be responsible for recycling", "Recycling should start at home with small daily actions", "Landfills are the best way to deal with rubbish"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，分类经过练习可养成习惯；B 错误，文章说家庭也有责任；C 正确，文章强调回收从家庭开始，小行动有大作用；D 错误，填埋不是最好的处理方式。"}, {"prompt": "Where should batteries and old medicines go?", "options": ["In the recycling bin with paper", "In the harmful waste bin", "In the kitchen waste bin", "In any bin that is empty"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，电池和过期药品属于有害垃圾；A 错误，不应与纸张混放；C 错误，不属于厨余垃圾；D 错误，不能随便放进空桶。"}, {"prompt": "Why do children often learn sorting faster than adults?", "options": ["Because they never need to sort rubbish", "Because they do not produce any waste", "Because schools teach them through games and activities", "Because adults refuse to learn anything"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，孩子同样需要分类；B 错误，孩子也会产生垃圾；C 正确，学校通过游戏活动教孩子分类；D 错误，成年人并非拒绝学习。"}, {"prompt": "What is one benefit of recycling aluminum cans?", "options": ["It uses more electricity than making new aluminum", "It saves a great deal of electricity", "It increases air pollution", "It wastes more raw materials"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，回收铝罐比生产新铝节省大量电力；A 错误，回收用电更少；C 错误，回收可减少污染；D 错误，回收节约原材料。"}, {"prompt": "What else does the author suggest besides recycling?", "options": ["Buy new electronics every month", "Throw away more single-use plastic", "Use reusable bags and buy only what is needed", "Never repair old clothes"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，不应每月买新电器；B 错误，应拒绝一次性塑料；C 正确，作者建议用可重复使用的袋子、按需购买；D 错误，修补旧衣是好选择。"}]}]}, {"no": 29, "passages": [{"title": "Social Media and Real Friendship", "text": "Social media has changed how young people keep in touch with friends. With one message, they can talk to someone far away, share photos, and join group chats at any time. For students who move to a new city for college, this is very helpful. They can stay close to old friends while making new ones. Many students say social media makes them feel less lonely, especially during the first weeks at university. However, online friendship is not the same as face-to-face friendship. A person may have five hundred friends online but feel alone in real life. Liking a photo takes only one second, while a real conversation takes time and attention. Some students spend hours scrolling through posts every day. They compare their own lives with the perfect photos of others and begin to feel unhappy. Research shows that heavy use of social media is linked to stress and poor sleep. It can also reduce time for study, exercise, and real conversations. This does not mean social media is bad. It is a tool, and how people use it matters. Experts suggest setting clear limits, such as turning off notifications during study time and not using phones during meals. Meeting friends in person, even for a short walk or a cup of tea, can build stronger trust than hundreds of online messages. Some students have started a simple habit: they put their phones away on Friday evenings and spend time talking with roommates. At first, it felt strange, but after a few weeks, they found they knew each other much better. True friendship needs time, honesty, and shared experiences. Social media can help people start a friendship, but only real contact can keep it strong. Used wisely, it connects people; used without control, it can leave them feeling more distant than before.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage on 'Social Media and Real Friendship'?", "options": ["Social media is completely harmful to students", "Social media can help friendship but cannot replace real contact", "Students should delete all social media accounts", "Online friends are always more honest than real friends"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，文章并未说社交媒体完全有害；B 错误，文章认为社交媒体有帮助但不能取代面对面交往；C 正确，文章主旨正是社交媒体可辅助友谊但不能替代真实接触；D 错误，未说网友总比现实朋友诚实。"}, {"prompt": "Why is social media helpful for new college students?", "options": ["It helps them stay in touch with old friends in a new city", "It guarantees they will pass all examinations", "It makes them sleep better at night", "It stops them from feeling hungry"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，文章说社交媒体帮助新生与老朋友保持联系；B 错误，未说能保证通过考试；C 错误，过度使用反而影响睡眠；D 正确，新生到新城市时社交媒体有助于保持旧友联系。"}, {"prompt": "What problem can heavy use of social media cause?", "options": ["Students will have more time for exercise", "Students will always become more confident", "Students may feel stressed, sleep poorly, and study less", "Students will never feel lonely"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，刷屏会减少锻炼时间；B 错误，未说会总是更自信；C 正确，重度使用与压力、睡眠差、学习时间减少有关；D 错误，网上朋友多仍可能感到孤独。"}, {"prompt": "What do experts suggest students do?", "options": ["Use phones during every meal", "Set limits such as turning off notifications while studying", "Spend more hours scrolling through posts", "Avoid meeting friends in person"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，专家建议设限，如学习时关闭通知；A 错误，不建议吃饭时用手机；C 错误，不建议花更多时间刷帖；D 错误，文章鼓励面对面见朋友。"}, {"prompt": "What does the Friday evening habit show?", "options": ["Phones are necessary for all conversations", "Students should never talk to roommates", "Putting phones away can help roommates know each other better", "Friday evenings are the worst time to meet friends"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，谈话不需要手机；B 错误，文章鼓励与室友交谈；C 正确，周五晚上放下手机后室友彼此更了解；D 错误，未说周五晚上最不适合见朋友。"}]}, {"title": "A Part-Time Job Teaches More Than Money", "text": "Many college students take part-time jobs during their studies. Some work in cafes, others deliver food, tutor younger students, or help in campus shops. The most obvious reason is money. A part-time job can help pay for books, meals, and daily expenses. For students from families with limited income, this support is especially important. But a part-time job teaches more than how to earn money. It also teaches time management. Students must plan their study time around work hours. If they work too many hours, their grades may suffer. If they plan well, they learn to use every hour wisely. A student who works ten hours a week often becomes better at organizing tasks and meeting deadlines. Work also builds communication skills. In a cafe, a student learns to talk politely to customers, solve small problems, and work with colleagues. These skills are useful in future jobs, even if the future job is completely different. Employers often value work experience because it shows responsibility and independence. However, part-time work has risks. Some jobs pay very little and demand long hours. Some students feel tired and lose interest in study. Teachers advise students to choose jobs related to their studies when possible, or jobs with flexible hours. Working on campus is often a good choice because managers understand class schedules. Parents also worry about safety. Students should avoid jobs that require working late at night alone or in unsafe places. In the end, a part-time job should support study, not replace it. When students balance work and study well, they gain money, skills, and confidence. The experience becomes part of their education, not just a way to earn pocket money. In the end, the lesson is clear: steady effort and thoughtful choices matter more than quick results, and students who keep learning from real examples will be better prepared for new challenges in school and in life. They can share what they learn with others and build good habits that last for many years.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the main purpose of the passage on 'A Part-Time Job Teaches More Than Money'?", "options": ["To explain how to open a cafe", "To advise students never to work while studying", "To show that part-time jobs teach skills beyond earning money", "To compare salaries in different cities"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，未教如何开咖啡馆；B 错误，文章并未劝学生绝不兼职；C 正确，文章主旨是兼职除赚钱外还教时间管理与沟通等技能；D 错误，未比较不同城市薪资。"}, {"prompt": "How can a part-time job help students manage time?", "options": ["By removing all deadlines", "By giving them unlimited free time", "By forcing them to plan study time around work hours", "By allowing them to skip classes"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，工作反而有截止期限；B 错误，兼职不会给无限空闲；C 正确，兼职迫使学生围绕工作时间规划学习；D 错误，未说可以逃课。"}, {"prompt": "Why do employers value part-time work experience?", "options": ["Because it shows responsibility and independence", "Because it proves students are rich", "Because it means students never study", "Because it guarantees high salaries"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，雇主看重兼职是因为它体现责任感与独立性；B 错误，兼职不证明富有；C 正确，工作经历显示责任感和独立性；D 错误，未说能保证高薪。"}, {"prompt": "What risk does the passage mention about part-time jobs?", "options": ["Some jobs pay little, demand long hours, and harm study", "All part-time jobs are completely safe", "Part-time jobs always improve grades", "Students never feel tired from work"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，文中提到有些工作报酬低、时间长、影响学习；B 错误，并非所有兼职都安全；C 错误，兼职不一定总能提高成绩；D 正确，低薪长工时是文中提到的风险。"}, {"prompt": "What advice is given about choosing a part-time job?", "options": ["Work as many hours as possible", "Work late at night alone in unsafe places", "Choose jobs with flexible hours or related to study when possible", "Ignore class schedules completely"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，工作过多会影响成绩；B 错误，应避免夜间独自在不安全处工作；C 正确，建议选与专业相关或时间灵活的工作；D 错误，校内工作应兼顾课程表。"}]}]}, {"no": 30, "passages": [{"title": "A Day at the Museum", "text": "On a rainy Saturday, Wang Li visited the city museum with her classmates. She had passed the building many times but had never gone inside. She expected it to be boring, with only old objects behind glass. To her surprise, she found the visit both interesting and relaxing. The museum has several floors. The first floor shows the history of the city, from a small fishing village to a modern port. Visitors can see old maps, photographs, and models of ships. The second floor is about daily life in the past. There are traditional clothes, kitchen tools, and furniture from one hundred years ago. Wang Li was surprised to see how different life was without electricity or running water. The third floor is for temporary exhibitions. This month, it shows photographs of mountains and rivers taken by young artists. Many visitors stop there to take pictures and discuss the works. Museums are not only for looking. Many offer guided tours, workshops, and activities for children. Visitors can listen to stories about each object and learn why it is important. Some museums now use technology to make visits more lively. Visitors can scan a code with a phone and watch a short video about an object. Others can try virtual reality and feel as if they are standing in an ancient street. Wang Li thinks museums help people understand their culture and history. A city without a museum may forget its past. She also believes museums should be more welcoming to young people. Lower ticket prices, more interactive activities, and evening openings could attract more visitors. After the visit, Wang Li and her friends talked about what they had seen over lunch. They agreed that the museum was not boring at all. It was a place where history came alive, and they planned to return for the next exhibition.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What did Wang Li expect before visiting the museum?", "options": ["That it would be closed on Saturdays", "That it would be full of modern cinemas", "That it would be boring with only old objects behind glass", "That it would have no history at all"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，博物馆周六是开放的；B 错误，未提现代影院；C 正确，她原以为博物馆只有玻璃后的旧物、很无聊；D 错误，一楼正是展示城市历史。"}, {"prompt": "What is shown on the second floor of the museum?", "options": ["Daily life in the past, such as clothes and kitchen tools", "Models of modern airplanes", "Photographs by young artists", "The history of other countries"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，二楼展示过去日常生活，包括传统服饰和厨具；A 错误，飞机模型未提及；C 错误，年轻艺术家摄影展在三楼；D 错误，未展示他国历史。"}, {"prompt": "How do some museums use technology now?", "options": ["By asking visitors to leave phones at home", "By letting visitors scan codes to watch videos or try virtual reality", "By removing all exhibitions", "By closing guided tours"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，博物馆鼓励用手机扫码；B 错误，未说要移除展览；C 正确，扫码看视频、体验虚拟现实是文中提到的技术应用；D 错误，导览仍在提供。"}, {"prompt": "Why does Wang Li think museums are important?", "options": ["They help people understand culture and history", "They make cities forget the past", "They only show photographs", "They are useful only for children"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，她认为博物馆帮助人们理解文化与历史；B 错误，博物馆是防止遗忘过去；C 错误，博物馆不只展示照片；D 正确，博物馆有助于理解文化历史。"}, {"prompt": "What does Wang Li suggest to attract more young visitors?", "options": ["Closing the museum on weekends", "Lower prices, more interactive activities, and evening openings", "Higher ticket prices and fewer activities", "Removing all temporary exhibitions"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，周末关闭适得其反；B 错误，提高票价会减少访客；C 正确，她建议降价、增加互动活动、晚间开放；D 错误，临时展览是吸引人的部分。"}]}, {"title": "The Problem of Food Waste", "text": "Every year, a huge amount of food is wasted around the world. In restaurants, people order more than they can eat. In supermarkets, vegetables that do not look perfect are thrown away. At home, families buy too much food and forget what is in the fridge. In China, the problem is also serious. Studies show that a large share of food served in restaurants is left uneaten. This waste is not only about money. Producing food uses land, water, and energy. When food is thrown away, all these resources are wasted too. Food waste in landfills also produces harmful gases that damage the environment. There are many reasons for food waste. Some people order extra dishes to show hospitality when they invite guests. Others do not plan meals carefully and buy food without a list. In shops, food near its expiry date is often removed, even if it is still safe to eat. Young people are also used to throwing away leftovers instead of saving them for the next meal. However, change is possible. Many restaurants now offer smaller portions and encourage customers to take leftovers home. The Clean Plate Campaign in China calls on people to finish their meals and avoid ordering too much. Schools teach students to value food and to understand how hard farmers work to grow it. At home, families can make a shopping list, store food correctly, and use leftovers to cook new dishes. Supermarkets can sell imperfect vegetables at lower prices instead of throwing them away. Food banks can collect safe, extra food and give it to people in need. Reducing food waste does not require great effort. It starts with a simple choice: order only what you can eat, buy only what you need, and respect every grain of food.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the main topic of the passage on 'The Problem of Food Waste'?", "options": ["How to open a new restaurant", "The serious problem of food waste and how to reduce it", "Why supermarkets should raise prices", "How farmers grow vegetables"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，文章讲食物浪费的严重性及减少方法；A 错误，未教开餐厅；C 错误，未说超市应涨价；D 错误，未讲农民如何种菜。"}, {"prompt": "Why is food waste more than a waste of money?", "options": ["Because producing food uses land, water, and energy", "Because wasted food always tastes better", "Because restaurants earn more from waste", "Because landfills produce fresh air"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，生产食物消耗土地、水和能源，浪费即浪费资源；B 错误，浪费的食物并非更美味；C 正确，生产食物耗费资源，丢弃即浪费这些资源；D 错误，垃圾填埋会产生有害气体。"}, {"prompt": "What is one reason people waste food in restaurants?", "options": ["They order extra dishes to show hospitality", "They are forced to eat everything", "They never invite guests", "They always cook at home"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，人们为表示好客常多点菜；B 错误，无人强迫吃完；C 错误，浪费常发生在请客时；D 正确，多点菜以示好客是文中原因之一。"}, {"prompt": "What does the Clean Plate Campaign ask people to do?", "options": ["Throw away all leftovers", "Order as much food as possible", "Finish meals and avoid ordering too much", "Eat only in expensive restaurants"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，应打包剩菜而非丢弃；B 错误，与行动相反；C 正确，光盘行动号召吃完、不多点；D 错误，未要求只去高档餐厅。"}, {"prompt": "What can supermarkets do to reduce waste?", "options": ["Stop selling fresh food", "Throw away all vegetables near expiry", "Raise prices for all food", "Sell imperfect vegetables at lower prices"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，不应停止售卖新鲜食品；B 错误，不应全部丢弃；C 错误，涨价不是减废方法；D 正确，超市可低价出售品相不佳的蔬菜。"}]}]}, {"no": 31, "passages": [{"title": "Working from Home: Freedom and Challenges", "text": "In recent years, working from home has become common in many companies. Employees can save time on travel, wear comfortable clothes, and arrange their own schedules. For parents, it is easier to look after children. For companies, it can reduce office costs. Many workers say they feel more relaxed and can focus better without office noise. However, working from home also brings challenges. The line between work and rest becomes unclear. Some people work longer hours because they cannot stop checking emails. Others feel lonely because they miss talking with colleagues face to face. A worker may sit at the same desk all day, eat lunch alone, and forget to take breaks. This can harm both health and mood. Good habits can help. Experts suggest creating a separate workspace, even if it is only a small corner of a room. Workers should keep regular hours, take short breaks, and go outside for a walk. Video meetings can help teams stay connected, but they should not fill the whole day. Companies also need to support remote workers. They can provide clear tasks, regular feedback, and chances for team members to meet in person. Trust is important. Managers should judge workers by results, not by how many hours they sit in front of a computer. Not every job can be done from home. Doctors, teachers, factory workers, and drivers must be present in person. For office jobs that mainly use computers, a mix of home and office work may be the best choice. Many companies now allow employees to work from home two or three days a week and come to the office on other days. This balance gives workers freedom while keeping teamwork alive. Working from home is not perfect, but with clear rules and good habits, it can work well for both employees and companies.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is one advantage of working from home?", "options": ["Workers can save travel time and arrange their own schedules", "Workers must wear formal clothes every day", "Workers have to pay higher office costs", "Workers cannot look after their children"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，在家工作无需穿正装；B 错误，公司可减少办公成本而非增加；C 正确，在家工作可节省通勤时间、自主安排日程；D 错误，在家工作更便于照顾孩子。"}, {"prompt": "What problem does working from home cause for some people?", "options": ["They always finish work too early", "The line between work and rest becomes unclear", "They talk too much with colleagues", "They get too much exercise"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，在家工作者常工作更久而非过早完成；B 错误，他们缺少与同事面对面交流；C 错误，久坐少动是问题之一；D 正确，工作与休息界限模糊是文中提到的问题。"}, {"prompt": "What do experts suggest for remote workers?", "options": ["Never go outside for a walk", "Create a separate workspace and keep regular hours", "Check emails all night", "Work in bed all day without breaks"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，应外出散步休息；B 错误，不应在床上全天工作不休息；C 错误，不应整夜查邮件；D 正确，专家建议设立独立工作区、保持规律作息。"}, {"prompt": "How should managers judge remote workers?", "options": ["By how many hours they sit at the computer", "By results rather than sitting time", "By how often they send messages at midnight", "By whether they wear formal clothes at home"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，管理者应按成果而非久坐时长评判；A 错误，久坐时长不是评判标准；C 错误，午夜发消息频率不重要；D 错误，着装不是评判标准。"}, {"prompt": "What working pattern do many companies now use?", "options": ["Everyone must work from home every day", "A mix of home work and office work", "No one is allowed to work from home", "All employees must work at night"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，并非要求每天在家工作；B 错误，并非禁止在家工作；C 错误，并非要求夜间工作；D 正确，许多公司采用居家与到岗相结合的混合模式。"}]}, {"title": "Understanding Stress in College Life", "text": "College life is exciting, but it can also be stressful. Students face examinations, deadlines, new friendships, and life away from home for the first time. A little stress can be helpful. It can push students to study harder and finish tasks on time. Too much stress, however, can cause problems. Students may feel anxious, sleep poorly, lose their appetite, or find it hard to concentrate. Some may even feel sad for a long time. Common sources of stress include heavy coursework, financial pressure, and worries about the future. Many students compare themselves with classmates and feel they are not good enough. Social media can make this worse, as people often show only their best moments online. Coping with stress starts with noticing it. Students should pay attention to changes in sleep, mood, and energy. Talking with friends, family, or teachers can help. Sharing worries often makes them feel smaller. Physical activity is also useful. A walk, a run, or a game of badminton can clear the mind. Regular sleep and healthy meals give the body energy to deal with pressure. Time management is another key skill. Breaking a large task into small steps makes it less frightening. Making a simple plan for the week helps students see what is urgent and what can wait. Universities also offer support. Counseling centers provide free, private help. Many students feel shy about asking for help, but counselors say seeking support is a sign of strength, not weakness. Stress is a normal part of college life. It does not mean a student has failed. Learning to manage stress is an important life skill. With support, good habits, and realistic goals, students can face challenges and grow stronger through them.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage on 'Understanding Stress in College Life'?", "options": ["College life has no stress at all", "Stress in college is normal and can be managed with support and good habits", "Students should avoid all examinations", "Stress is always harmful and cannot be helped"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，文章讲大学压力正常，可通过支持与好习惯管理；A 错误，大学生活确有压力；C 错误，未说要回避考试；D 错误，压力并非总是无法应对。"}, {"prompt": "What can a little stress do?", "options": ["It can push students to study harder and finish tasks on time", "It always causes serious illness", "It makes students sleep all day", "It stops students from making friends"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，适度压力能推动学习而非总致病；B 错误，适度压力不会让人整天睡觉；C 正确，少量压力可促使学生更努力、按时完成任务；D 错误，适度压力不会阻止交友。"}, {"prompt": "What is a common source of stress mentioned?", "options": ["Living too close to home", "Heavy coursework, financial pressure, and worries about the future", "Having too many holidays", "Too little homework"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，离家太近不是压力源；B 错误，作业太少不是压力源；C 错误，假期太多不是压力源；D 正确，课业重、经济压力和对未来的担忧是常见压力源。"}, {"prompt": "What does the passage suggest about counseling centers?", "options": ["They are only for weak students", "They provide free, private help and seeking help is a sign of strength", "They should be avoided by all students", "They charge high fees for every visit"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，寻求帮助不是软弱的表现；B 错误，不应回避咨询中心；C 错误，咨询是免费的；D 正确，咨询中心提供免费保密帮助，求助是坚强的表现。"}, {"prompt": "How can time management help reduce stress?", "options": ["By taking on more tasks than possible", "By doing everything at the last minute", "By ignoring all deadlines", "By breaking large tasks into small steps and making a weekly plan"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，承担过多任务会加重压力；B 错误，拖到最后会增加压力；C 错误，忽视截止日期无益；D 正确，把大任务拆小、制定周计划可减轻压力。"}]}]}, {"no": 32, "passages": [{"title": "Public Transport in a Growing City", "text": "As cities grow, more people need to travel every day. Public transport, such as buses and subways, plays a key role in keeping a city moving. A good bus or subway system can carry thousands of people at once, reduce traffic jams, and cut air pollution. In many Chinese cities, subways are fast, clean, and cheap. Passengers can travel across the city in less than an hour, even during rush hour. Buses reach places where subways do not go, and they are important for older people and students. However, public transport still faces problems. During morning and evening rush hours, buses and subways are very crowded. Passengers may have to wait in long lines and stand close together. In some areas, bus routes are limited, and people must walk far to reach a station. Delays can also happen when there is bad weather or road work. To improve public transport, cities are taking several steps. They are building new subway lines, adding more buses, and using smart cards and phone apps so passengers can pay quickly and check arrival times. Some cities have created bus-only lanes to make buses faster. Others encourage people to ride bicycles to stations by building bike parking areas. Choosing public transport also depends on personal habits. Some people prefer driving because it feels more private and comfortable. Others choose buses or subways because they are cheaper and better for the environment. When public transport is reliable and comfortable, more people will use it. A city with good public transport is not only easier to live in but also cleaner and quieter. Investing in buses and subways is an investment in the future of the city. Overall, this topic shows how everyday life and larger social questions are closely connected, and how careful thinking helps people choose wisely. When students pay attention, ask questions, and practise what they have learned, they can make progress step by step and enjoy the process of learning.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the main topic of the passage on 'Public Transport in a Growing City'?", "options": ["How to drive a private car in a city", "The role and improvement of public transport in growing cities", "The history of bicycles", "Why subways should be closed"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，文章不是教如何开私家车；B 错误，未讲自行车历史；C 正确，文章讲公共交通的作用与改进；D 错误，未说应关闭地铁。"}, {"prompt": "What advantage do subways have in many Chinese cities?", "options": ["They are fast, clean, and cheap", "They reach every small village", "They never have passengers", "They are more expensive than taxis"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，地铁快捷干净便宜；B 错误，地铁并非通达每个小村；C 错误，地铁乘客很多；D 正确，地铁快速、干净、便宜。"}, {"prompt": "What problem occurs during rush hours?", "options": ["Tickets become free for everyone", "Public transport stops completely", "No one uses public transport", "Buses and subways are very crowded"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，票价并未免费；B 错误，公共交通并未完全停运；C 错误，高峰时使用者很多；D 正确，高峰时段公交地铁非常拥挤。"}, {"prompt": "What are cities doing to improve public transport?", "options": ["Banning bicycles near stations", "Closing all bus stations", "Removing phone payment systems", "Building new subway lines and adding more buses"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，正在建自行车停放区；B 错误，未关闭公交站；C 错误，正在推广手机支付；D 正确，城市在建新地铁线、增加公交车。"}, {"prompt": "What does the author think about investing in public transport?", "options": ["It is a waste of money", "It is an investment in the future of the city", "It will make cities dirtier", "It should only help drivers"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，作者认为投资公交地铁就是投资城市未来；A 错误，作者肯定其价值；C 错误，公共交通可使城市更清洁；D 错误，公共交通服务所有市民。"}]}, {"title": "Gardens on Rooftops", "text": "In crowded cities, green space is limited. Parks are few, and land is expensive. To solve this problem, some cities have started building gardens on rooftops. A rooftop garden is a garden created on the top of a building. It can include grass, flowers, vegetables, and even small trees. These gardens bring many benefits. First, they make cities greener and more beautiful. A building covered with plants looks pleasant and gives people a place to relax. Workers can take a break in a rooftop garden during lunch, and residents can enjoy fresh air without leaving home. Second, rooftop gardens help the environment. Plants absorb rainwater, which reduces flooding after heavy rain. They also lower the temperature of buildings in summer, so less air conditioning is needed. This saves energy and reduces electricity bills. Third, rooftop gardens can produce food. Some schools and communities grow vegetables on rooftops and use them in canteens or share them with neighbors. Children learn where food comes from and how to care for plants. However, building a rooftop garden is not easy. The roof must be strong enough to hold soil and water. Waterproof layers are needed to prevent leaks. Plants must be chosen carefully because rooftops are windy and sunny. Maintenance also takes time and money. Despite these difficulties, more cities are trying rooftop gardens. In Singapore, many public housing buildings have gardens on their roofs. In China, some schools and offices have started similar projects. As cities continue to grow upward, rooftops may become the new parks of the future. A small garden on a roof can make a big difference to city life. This experience also reminds readers that small daily choices can lead to large changes over time, and that understanding the topic well helps students make better decisions in their own lives and studies. With patience, practice, and support from teachers and friends, learners can turn new knowledge into real skills and use it with confidence in the future.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is a rooftop garden?", "options": ["A flower shop on the street", "A park built underground", "A garden only for farmers in villages", "A garden built on the top of a building"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，不是街边花店；B 错误，不是地下公园；C 错误，不只供乡村农民使用；D 正确，屋顶花园是建在建筑顶部的花园。"}, {"prompt": "How do rooftop gardens help the environment?", "options": ["They absorb rainwater and lower building temperatures", "They increase flooding after rain", "They use more air conditioning", "They produce more pollution"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，植物吸收雨水、降低温度；B 错误，它们减少洪水而非增加；C 正确，吸收雨水、降温省电是环保益处；D 错误，它们减少污染而非增加。"}, {"prompt": "What can children learn from rooftop vegetable gardens?", "options": ["How to use air conditioning", "How to build tall buildings", "How to drive cars", "Where food comes from and how to care for plants"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，未教使用空调；B 错误，未教建高楼；C 错误，未教开车；D 正确，孩子可了解食物来源、学习照料植物。"}, {"prompt": "What is a challenge in building a rooftop garden?", "options": ["The roof must be strong enough and waterproof", "Rooftops always have too much soil", "Plants never need any care", "There is no wind or sun on rooftops"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，屋顶须承重且防水；B 错误，屋顶土壤并非过多；C 错误，植物需要维护；D 正确，屋顶须足够坚固并防水是挑战之一。"}, {"prompt": "What does the author predict about rooftops?", "options": ["They should be used only for parking", "They will disappear in all cities", "They are useless for city life", "They may become the new parks of the future"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，未说只应用于停车；B 错误，屋顶花园正在增多；C 错误，屋顶花园对城市生活有益；D 正确，作者认为屋顶可能成为未来的新公园。"}]}]}, {"no": 33, "passages": [{"title": "How AI Is Changing the Classroom", "text": "Artificial intelligence, or AI, is entering classrooms around the world. Teachers now use AI tools to prepare lessons, check homework, and give students quick feedback. For example, an AI program can correct grammar mistakes in an essay within seconds and suggest better sentences. It can also create practice questions based on what a student finds difficult. This saves teachers time and allows them to focus more on guiding students. For students, AI can be a helpful study partner. A student who does not understand a math problem can ask an AI tutor for a step-by-step explanation. A language learner can practice conversations with a chatbot at any time. AI can also adjust the difficulty of exercises. If a student answers correctly, the next question becomes harder; if the student struggles, the program offers easier practice. However, AI in education also raises concerns. Some students may depend too much on AI and stop thinking for themselves. If AI writes an essay for a student, the student learns nothing. Teachers worry about cheating and about whether AI answers are always correct. AI can make mistakes, especially on complex questions. Privacy is another issue. AI systems collect a great deal of information about students, and schools must protect this data carefully. Experts believe AI should support teachers, not replace them. A good teacher understands students' feelings, encourages them, and creates a warm classroom. AI cannot do these things. The best approach is to combine AI tools with human teaching. Students should learn to use AI wisely: check its answers, think critically, and use it as a tool for learning, not as a shortcut. When used properly, AI can make education more personal and more effective for every student.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What can AI do for teachers according to the passage?", "options": ["Prepare lessons, check homework, and give quick feedback", "Replace all teachers in schools", "Make students stop studying", "Collect no information at all"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，AI可备课、批改作业、快速反馈；A 错误，AI应辅助而非取代教师；C 错误，AI不是让学生停止学习；D 错误，AI系统会收集大量信息。"}, {"prompt": "How can AI help language learners?", "options": ["By removing all practice questions", "By forbidding them to speak", "By writing all essays for them", "By letting them practice conversations with a chatbot anytime"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，AI会生成练习题而非删除；B 错误，未禁止说话；C 错误，代写论文是应避免的依赖；D 正确，语言学习者可随时与聊天机器人练对话。"}, {"prompt": "What concern do teachers have about AI?", "options": ["Students may depend too much on AI and cheat", "AI always gives perfect answers", "AI makes classrooms too warm", "AI never collects student data"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，学生可能过度依赖AI并作弊；B 错误，AI并非总能给出正确答案；C 错误，未提教室太暖；D 正确，过度依赖与作弊是教师的担忧。"}, {"prompt": "What does the author believe about AI and teachers?", "options": ["AI can understand students' feelings better than teachers", "AI should replace all human teachers", "Teachers are no longer needed", "AI should support teachers, not replace them"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，AI无法理解学生情感；B 错误，不应取代教师；C 错误，教师仍然需要；D 正确，作者认为AI应辅助教师而非取代。"}, {"prompt": "What should students do when using AI?", "options": ["Accept every AI answer without thinking", "Check answers, think critically, and use AI as a learning tool", "Use AI only to cheat on exams", "Never ask AI any questions"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，不应不加思考接受所有答案；B 错误，不应用AI作弊；C 正确，应核对答案、批判思考、把AI当学习工具；D 错误，学生可以向AI提问。"}]}, {"title": "Volunteering Changes Lives", "text": "Every weekend, university student Zhao Lei travels to a nearby village to teach children English. He is a volunteer, which means he works without pay. He says the experience has changed his life. At first, he thought volunteering was only about helping others. Later, he realized it also helped him grow. He became more patient, more confident, and better at speaking in front of a group. Volunteering takes many forms. Some people help in hospitals, others clean parks, plant trees, or collect clothes for people in need. During disasters, volunteers deliver food and supplies to affected areas. In cities, volunteers often work in libraries, museums, and community centers. They give their time because they want to make their community better. For students, volunteering offers real-life experience that cannot be learned from textbooks. It teaches teamwork, responsibility, and problem-solving. A student who organizes a charity event learns how to plan, communicate, and lead a team. These skills are valuable for future jobs. Volunteering also helps students understand society better. They meet people from different backgrounds and learn about their lives and difficulties. This can change how they see the world and make them more caring. Of course, volunteering requires time and energy. Students must balance it with study. Experts suggest starting with a few hours a week and choosing an activity that matches personal interests. Even small actions matter. Helping an elderly neighbor carry shopping bags or teaching a child to read can make a real difference. Zhao Lei plans to continue volunteering after graduation. He believes that when people give their time to help others, they receive something valuable in return: friendship, experience, and a sense of purpose. In the end, the lesson is clear: steady effort and thoughtful choices matter more than quick results, and students who keep learning from real examples will be better prepared for new challenges in school and in life. They can share what they learn with others and build good habits that last for many years.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What does Zhao Lei do as a volunteer?", "options": ["He drives a taxi on weekends", "He works in a bank for high pay", "He sells clothes in a shop", "He teaches children English in a nearby village"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，未提开出租车；B 错误，志愿工作无报酬；C 错误，未提卖衣服；D 正确，赵磊周末去附近村庄教孩子英语。"}, {"prompt": "What did Zhao Lei learn from volunteering?", "options": ["He became more patient, confident, and better at speaking", "He learned to avoid talking to people", "He decided never to help others again", "He lost all interest in study"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，他变得更有耐心、自信、善于演讲；B 错误，他并未学会回避交流；C 正确，志愿服务让他更有耐心、自信和表达能力；D 错误，未说失去学习兴趣。"}, {"prompt": "What skill can a student learn by organizing a charity event?", "options": ["How to plan, communicate, and lead a team", "How to cheat on examinations", "How to avoid all responsibility", "How to work alone without talking"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，组织慈善活动可学规划、沟通和带队；A 错误，未教作弊；C 错误，志愿服务培养责任感；D 错误，活动需要团队合作。"}, {"prompt": "What advice is given to student volunteers?", "options": ["Start with a few hours a week and choose activities matching interests", "Spend all time volunteering and ignore study", "Choose activities they dislike", "Volunteer only during disasters"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，建议从每周几小时开始、选感兴趣的活动；B 错误，应平衡志愿与学习；C 错误，应选感兴趣的活动；D 正确，从少量时间起步、选合兴趣的活动是文中建议。"}, {"prompt": "What does Zhao Lei believe people receive from volunteering?", "options": ["Punishment from society", "Only money and fame", "Nothing at all", "Friendship, experience, and a sense of purpose"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，志愿服务不会受到惩罚；B 错误，志愿工作无报酬、无名利；C 错误，志愿者确有收获；D 正确，付出时间可收获友谊、经验与使命感。"}]}]}, {"no": 34, "passages": [{"title": "The Habit of Reading Every Day", "text": "Reading is one of the best habits a student can develop. A person who reads every day learns new words, new ideas, and new ways of thinking. Reading also improves writing, because good writers are usually good readers. They learn how sentences are built and how ideas are connected. However, many students say they have no time to read. They spend hours on phones, watching videos, or playing games. The truth is that reading does not need a lot of time. Fifteen minutes a day is enough to start. A student can read on the bus, before lunch, or before going to bed. The key is to choose books that are interesting. Some students enjoy novels, others prefer history, science, or biographies of famous people. When the book is interesting, reading feels like pleasure, not homework. Libraries and e-books make reading easier than ever. A student can borrow books for free or download them on a phone. Reading groups also help. When students discuss a book together, they understand it better and remember more. They can share different views and learn from each other. Reading also helps students relax. After a long day of study, a good story can take the mind to another world. Research shows that people who read regularly have better concentration and a larger vocabulary. They also do better in examinations that require understanding long texts. To build the habit, experts suggest setting a fixed time for reading and keeping a book nearby. Start with short books or articles. Do not worry about finishing quickly. The goal is to enjoy reading and to learn a little every day. Over months and years, those small daily minutes add up to a great deal of knowledge.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage on 'The Habit of Reading Every Day'?", "options": ["Reading every day is a valuable habit that brings many benefits", "Students should never read novels", "Reading is only useful for examinations", "Phones are better than books for learning"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，文章肯定每天阅读的价值；B 错误，文章鼓励读小说等感兴趣的书；C 正确，每天阅读是好习惯、益处很多是主旨；D 错误，未说手机比书更好。"}, {"prompt": "How much time does the passage suggest to start reading?", "options": ["At least five hours every day", "Fifteen minutes a day is enough to start", "Only one minute a week", "A whole weekend without breaks"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，不需要每天五小时；B 错误，并非每周一分钟；C 错误，不需要整个周末不停读；D 正确，每天十五分钟就足以开始。"}, {"prompt": "Why do reading groups help students?", "options": ["They make reading more boring", "They force students to read faster", "They stop students from choosing books", "They allow students to discuss books and learn from each other"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，讨论使阅读更有趣；B 错误，未说强迫读得更快；C 错误，小组不阻止选书；D 正确，读书小组通过讨论加深理解、互相学习。"}, {"prompt": "What does research show about regular readers?", "options": ["They forget everything they read", "They always fail examinations", "They cannot write good sentences", "They have better concentration and a larger vocabulary"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，阅读有助于记忆；B 错误，阅读者考试表现更好；C 错误，阅读有助于写作；D 正确，常阅读者专注力更好、词汇量更大。"}, {"prompt": "What advice is given to build a reading habit?", "options": ["Set a fixed time, keep a book nearby, and start with short books", "Read only very long books at once", "Worry about finishing as quickly as possible", "Read only when there is an examination"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，建议固定时间、随身带书、从短书开始；A 错误，不应只读很长的书；C 错误，不必担心读得快慢；D 错误，不应只在考试前阅读。"}]}, {"title": "Climate Change and Our Daily Choices", "text": "Climate change is one of the biggest challenges facing the world today. The earth is getting warmer because human activities produce too much carbon dioxide and other gases. Burning coal, oil, and gas for energy is the main cause. Cutting down forests also makes the problem worse, because trees absorb carbon dioxide. The effects are already visible. Summers are hotter, storms are stronger, and sea levels are rising. In some places, droughts make farming difficult. In others, floods damage homes and roads. Scientists warn that if people do not act, these problems will become more serious. Many people think climate change is only a problem for governments and scientists. In fact, daily choices matter too. Using public transport, riding a bicycle, or walking instead of driving can reduce emissions. Saving electricity at home helps as well. Turning off lights when leaving a room, using energy-saving bulbs, and unplugging chargers are small but useful actions. Eating less meat can also help, because raising animals for meat produces a large amount of greenhouse gases. Choosing local food reduces the energy used to transport food over long distances. Governments and companies also have a role to play. They can invest in solar and wind power, build better public transport, and make laws to reduce pollution. Many countries have set goals to reach carbon neutrality in the coming decades. This means balancing the amount of carbon produced with the amount removed from the air. Young people are playing an important part. Students organize activities to plant trees, save water, and raise awareness about climate change. Every small action counts. When millions of people make greener choices, the total effect is large. Protecting the climate is not only about the future; it is about the world we live in today.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the main cause of climate change mentioned?", "options": ["Burning coal, oil, and gas produces too much carbon dioxide", "Planting too many trees", "Using too little electricity", "Too much rain in winter"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，植树有助于吸收二氧化碳；B 错误，用电太少不是原因；C 正确，燃烧化石燃料产生过多二氧化碳是主因；D 错误，冬季多雨不是主因。"}, {"prompt": "What effect of climate change is mentioned?", "options": ["Summers are hotter and storms are stronger", "Winters are always warmer in every place", "Sea levels are falling quickly", "Farming becomes easier everywhere"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，夏天更热、风暴更强是文中提到的影响；B 错误，未说各地冬季都变暖；C 错误，海平面在上升而非下降；D 正确，夏天更热、风暴更强是已可见的影响。"}, {"prompt": "What daily choice can help reduce emissions?", "options": ["Eating more meat every day", "Using public transport, cycling, or walking", "Leaving lights on all day", "Driving a car for every short trip"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，多吃肉会增加温室气体；B 错误，短途开车增加排放；C 错误，开灯整天浪费电；D 正确，乘公交、骑车或步行可减少排放。"}, {"prompt": "What does carbon neutrality mean?", "options": ["Balancing carbon produced with carbon removed from the air", "Producing no food at all", "Stopping all use of electricity", "Banning all cars immediately"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，碳中和指碳排放与碳清除相平衡；A 错误，不是不生产食物；C 错误，不是停止一切用电；D 错误，不是立即禁车。"}, {"prompt": "What role do young people play?", "options": ["They refuse to save water", "They ignore climate change completely", "They increase pollution on purpose", "They organize tree planting and raise awareness"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，他们提倡节约用水；B 错误，他们积极参与；C 错误，他们不是故意增加污染；D 正确，年轻人组织植树、节水并提高环保意识。"}]}]}, {"no": 35, "passages": [{"title": "Mobile Payment Changes Daily Life", "text": "In China, mobile payment has changed daily life in a remarkable way. Ten years ago, people carried cash and bank cards everywhere. Today, many people leave home with only a phone. They use it to pay for breakfast, take a bus, buy clothes, and order food online. A quick scan of a code completes the payment in seconds. This is convenient for both customers and shop owners. Small shops do not need to prepare a lot of change. They can also keep clear records of every sale. For customers, mobile payment is fast and safe in many ways. They do not need to worry about losing cash or receiving fake notes. If a phone is lost, the payment account can be locked quickly. However, mobile payment also has problems. Older people who are not familiar with smartphones may find it difficult. Some small shops in rural areas still prefer cash. When the phone runs out of battery or there is no internet, payment becomes impossible. There are also worries about safety. If people click on unknown links or share passwords, they may lose money. Experts advise users to set strong passwords, check their bills regularly, and never share verification codes with strangers. Mobile payment has also changed how people manage money. With a phone app, users can see exactly how much they spend each month. This helps some people save money, but others find it too easy to spend. When payment takes only one second, people may buy things without thinking. In the future, mobile payment will likely become even more common. Face recognition and other new technologies may make payment even faster. Still, cash will probably remain useful for some people and some situations. The key is to use new technology wisely and to make sure no one is left behind.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the main topic of the passage on 'Mobile Payment Changes Daily Life'?", "options": ["Why people should stop using phones", "How to make a smartphone", "The history of paper money", "How mobile payment has changed daily life in China"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，未说应停用手机；B 错误，未教制造手机；C 错误，未讲纸币历史；D 正确，文章讲移动支付如何改变中国日常生活。"}, {"prompt": "What is one advantage of mobile payment for shop owners?", "options": ["They do not need to prepare much change and can keep clear records", "They must carry more cash every day", "They cannot see their sales", "They have to close their shops earlier"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，小店无需准备大量零钱且可记录销售；B 错误，移动支付减少现金需求；C 正确，不用备大量零钱、可清晰记账是店主的便利；D 错误，未说要提前关店。"}, {"prompt": "Who may find mobile payment difficult?", "options": ["Young people who use phones every day", "Older people unfamiliar with smartphones", "Shop owners in big cities", "Students who order food online"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，年轻人用手机很熟练；B 错误，大城市店主常用移动支付；C 错误，网购学生常用移动支付；D 正确，不熟悉智能手机的老年人可能觉得困难。"}, {"prompt": "What safety advice is given?", "options": ["Use the same simple password everywhere", "Set strong passwords and never share verification codes", "Click on all unknown links", "Share verification codes with strangers"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，不应到处用同一简单密码；B 错误，不应分享验证码；C 错误，不应点击未知链接；D 正确，应设强密码、绝不与陌生人分享验证码。"}, {"prompt": "What does the author think about the future of cash?", "options": ["Cash will probably remain useful for some people and situations", "Cash will disappear tomorrow for everyone", "Cash is already useless everywhere", "No one will ever need a phone again"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，作者认为现金在某些人群和场合仍有用；A 错误，现金不会明天就消失；C 错误，现金并非到处无用；D 错误，手机仍会被需要。"}]}, {"title": "Keeping Traditional Crafts Alive", "text": "In many parts of China, traditional crafts such as paper cutting, embroidery, and pottery have a long history. These skills were passed down from parents to children for hundreds of years. Each piece is made by hand, so no two are exactly the same. However, many traditional crafts are now in danger. Young people prefer modern jobs in cities, and handmade products take a long time to make. Machine-made goods are cheaper and faster to produce. As a result, fewer young people want to learn these skills, and some old craftsmen have no one to teach. To protect traditional crafts, many places are taking action. Schools invite craftsmen to teach students. Museums hold exhibitions and workshops where visitors can try making a simple piece themselves. On the internet, young artists share videos of their work and sell handmade products to customers around the country. This helps more people discover the beauty of traditional crafts. The government also supports craftsmen by giving them awards and helping them open studios. Some crafts have been listed as intangible cultural heritage, which brings more attention and protection. Traditional crafts are more than beautiful objects. They carry history, local culture, and the wisdom of past generations. A piece of embroidery may show the flowers of a region, while a pottery design may tell a story from long ago. When people buy and use these products, they help keep the craft alive. Young people can play a key role. They can learn a craft, use modern design to make it more attractive, and share it on social media. By combining tradition with new ideas, old crafts can find new life in the modern world and continue to be enjoyed by future generations.", "questions": [{"prompt": "Why are traditional crafts in danger?", "options": ["Young people prefer modern city jobs and machine goods are cheaper", "Handmade products are too fast to make", "Old craftsmen refuse to teach anyone", "No one likes beautiful objects"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，手工品制作耗时而非太快；B 错误，老工匠并非拒绝教学；C 正确，年轻人倾向现代工作、机制品更便宜是主因；D 错误，人们喜欢精美物品。"}, {"prompt": "How are schools helping protect crafts?", "options": ["By inviting craftsmen to teach students", "By banning all craft lessons", "By closing museums", "By asking students to forget tradition"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，学校请工匠来教学；B 错误，未禁止手工课；C 错误，未关闭博物馆；D 正确，学校邀请工匠授课是保护措施之一。"}, {"prompt": "What do young artists do on the internet?", "options": ["Share videos of their work and sell handmade products", "Hide all traditional crafts", "Stop making anything by hand", "Refuse to use social media"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，年轻手艺人在网上分享视频、售卖手工艺品；B 错误，他们在推广而非隐藏；C 错误，他们仍在手工制作；D 错误，他们积极使用社交媒体。"}, {"prompt": "Why are traditional crafts valuable?", "options": ["They carry history, local culture, and wisdom of past generations", "They are all made by machines", "They have no connection to culture", "They are only useful as decorations"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，传统工艺承载历史、地方文化与先辈智慧；A 错误，手工品非机制；C 错误，它们与文化密切相关；D 错误，其价值不只在于装饰。"}, {"prompt": "What can young people do to keep crafts alive?", "options": ["Learn a craft, use modern design, and share it online", "Ignore all traditional skills", "Buy only machine-made goods", "Keep crafts secret from others"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，年轻人可学习手艺、结合现代设计并网上分享；B 错误，不应忽视传统技能；C 错误，不应只买机制品；D 错误，不应把手艺藏起来。"}]}]}, {"no": 36, "passages": [{"title": "When Exams Cause Too Much Pressure", "text": "Examinations are a normal part of school life, but for many students they cause a great deal of pressure. A week before an important test, some students cannot sleep well. Others feel their hearts beat faster when they enter the examination room. A small amount of nervousness is normal, but too much pressure can harm performance. Students may forget what they studied or make simple mistakes. There are several reasons for examination pressure. Some students set very high goals for themselves and fear failure. Others worry about disappointing their parents or teachers. Competition among classmates can also add pressure, especially when everyone wants to be at the top of the class. Social media makes it worse when students compare scores online. To reduce pressure, students can prepare early. Starting revision weeks before the examination is better than studying all night before the test. Making a study plan, reviewing notes regularly, and doing practice tests can build confidence. On the day of the examination, arriving early, taking deep breaths, and reading questions carefully can help students stay calm. Parents and teachers also play a role. They should encourage effort rather than only results. A student who works hard but does not get the highest score still deserves praise. Schools can help by offering counseling and by teaching stress management skills. It is also important to keep a balanced life during examination season. Exercise, sleep, and time with friends should not be forgotten. A short walk or a talk with a friend can refresh the mind. Examinations measure what a student knows at one moment. They do not measure a person's whole value. Learning to handle pressure is a skill that will help students not only in school but also in future work and life.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage on 'When Exams Cause Too Much Pressure'?", "options": ["Examination pressure is common and can be reduced with preparation and support", "All students enjoy examinations very much", "Examinations should be cancelled in all schools", "Pressure always improves test scores"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，并非所有学生都喜欢考试；B 错误，未说应取消所有考试；C 正确，考试压力常见，可通过准备与支持缓解是主旨；D 错误，过大压力会损害表现。"}, {"prompt": "What can too much pressure cause during an exam?", "options": ["Students may forget what they studied or make simple mistakes", "Students always remember everything perfectly", "Students feel completely relaxed", "Students finish tests in half the time"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，过大压力可能导致遗忘或粗心错误；B 错误，并非总能完美记忆；C 错误，过大压力不会让人完全放松；D 正确，压力过大可能遗忘所学或犯简单错误。"}, {"prompt": "What is one reason for examination pressure?", "options": ["Students fear disappointing parents or teachers", "Students have too much free time", "Examinations are too easy for everyone", "Teachers never give any homework"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，害怕让父母老师失望是压力来源之一；B 错误，空闲太多不是原因；C 错误，考试并非对所有人都太容易；D 错误，未说老师从不留作业。"}, {"prompt": "What does the passage suggest students do before exams?", "options": ["Study all night before the test", "Prepare early, make a plan, and do practice tests", "Ignore all notes and textbooks", "Compare scores online as much as possible"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，建议提前准备、制定计划、做模拟题；A 错误，考前通宵不是好方法；C 错误，不应忽视笔记课本；D 错误，网上比分会加重压力。"}, {"prompt": "What should parents and teachers encourage?", "options": ["Effort rather than only results", "Only the highest scores", "Students to skip sleep", "Students to avoid all friends"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，应鼓励努力而非只看结果；B 错误，不应只看最高分；C 错误，不应让学生熬夜；D 错误，不应让学生回避朋友。"}]}, {"title": "Clean Energy for a Greener Future", "text": "For many years, most electricity came from burning coal and oil. These fuels produce a large amount of carbon dioxide and pollute the air. Today, clean energy sources such as solar power, wind power, and hydropower are becoming more important. They produce electricity without burning fuel, so they cause far less pollution. Solar panels can be placed on rooftops, in deserts, and even on water. In sunny areas, they can produce a great deal of electricity during the day. Wind turbines work best in open areas and along the coast, where wind is strong and steady. Hydropower uses flowing water to turn turbines and has been used for many years in China. Clean energy has many advantages. It reduces air pollution and helps fight climate change. It also uses resources that will not run out. The sun and wind are free and available in most places. As technology improves, the cost of solar panels and wind turbines has fallen greatly. In some places, clean energy is now cheaper than coal power. However, clean energy also faces challenges. Solar power depends on sunshine, so it produces less electricity on cloudy days or at night. Wind power depends on wind speed, which can change quickly. Storing electricity in large batteries is still expensive. Power lines must also be built to carry electricity from remote deserts or mountains to cities. China has made great progress in clean energy. It has built the largest solar and wind power capacity in the world. Many villages now use solar panels to provide electricity for homes and schools. In the future, clean energy will likely provide more and more of the world's electricity. With better batteries and smarter power systems, people can enjoy clean air and a stable supply of power at the same time.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the main topic of the passage on 'Clean Energy for a Greener Future'?", "options": ["The advantages and challenges of clean energy", "How to burn coal more efficiently", "Why people should stop using electricity", "The history of oil production"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，文章讲清洁能源的优点与挑战；B 错误，未教如何更高效烧煤；C 错误，未说应停止用电；D 错误，未讲石油生产史。"}, {"prompt": "Where do solar panels work well?", "options": ["In sunny areas where they can produce much electricity", "Only in dark underground rooms", "Only on rainy days", "In places with no sunlight at all"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，阳光充足地区适合太阳能；B 错误，地下暗室不适合；C 正确，阳光充足地区可产生大量电力；D 错误，无阳光处无法发电。"}, {"prompt": "What is a challenge for solar power?", "options": ["It produces less electricity on cloudy days or at night", "It always works the same at night", "It needs no sunshine at all", "It produces too much pollution"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，阴天或夜间发电较少是挑战；B 错误，夜间发电不同；C 错误，太阳能需要阳光；D 正确，阴天夜间发电少是太阳能的挑战。"}, {"prompt": "What has China achieved in clean energy?", "options": ["It has built the largest solar and wind power capacity in the world", "It has stopped using all clean energy", "It uses only coal power now", "It has no solar panels in villages"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，中国建成世界最大的太阳能与风电装机容量；B 错误，中国大力发展清洁能源；C 错误，中国正在减少煤电依赖；D 错误，许多村庄已使用太阳能板。"}, {"prompt": "What does the author predict about the future?", "options": ["Clean energy will provide more of the world's electricity", "Clean energy will disappear soon", "Coal will become the only energy source", "People will stop needing electricity"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，作者预测清洁能源将提供越来越多电力；A 错误，清洁能源不会很快消失；C 错误，煤炭不会成为唯一能源；D 错误，人们仍需要电力。"}]}]}, {"no": 37, "passages": [{"title": "Festivals Bring People Together", "text": "Festivals are an important part of culture in every country. In China, the Spring Festival is the most important celebration of the year. Families travel long distances to be together, share a big dinner, and watch fireworks. Children receive red envelopes with money, and people wish each other good luck for the new year. Other festivals also have special meanings. The Mid-Autumn Festival is a time to enjoy the full moon and eat mooncakes with family. The Dragon Boat Festival remembers a famous poet, and people race dragon boats and eat zongzi. Each festival has its own food, stories, and customs. Festivals are not only about fun. They help people remember history and pass traditions to younger generations. When grandparents tell stories about a festival, children learn about their culture. Festivals also strengthen family bonds. In busy modern life, family members often live in different cities. A festival gives them a reason to return home and spend time together. In recent years, festivals have changed in some ways. Many people now send greetings by phone instead of visiting in person. Online shopping makes it easy to buy festival gifts. Some traditional activities are less common than before, but new forms of celebration have appeared. For example, people watch festival shows online or join community activities. Festivals in other countries are also interesting. Christmas in Western countries is a time for family and giving. People decorate trees and exchange gifts. In India, Diwali is a festival of lights that celebrates hope and new beginnings. Learning about festivals in other cultures helps people understand each other better. Whether in China or abroad, festivals remind us of what matters most: family, friendship, and hope for the future. Overall, this topic shows how everyday life and larger social questions are closely connected, and how careful thinking helps people choose wisely. When students pay attention, ask questions, and practise what they have learned, they can make progress step by step and enjoy the process of learning.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage on 'Festivals Bring People Together'?", "options": ["Festivals help people remember culture and bring families together", "Festivals are only about eating food", "Spring Festival is unimportant in China", "People should stop celebrating festivals"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，节日不只是吃东西；B 错误，春节是中国最重要的节日；C 正确，节日帮助人们记住文化、团聚家人是主旨；D 错误，未说应停止庆祝节日。"}, {"prompt": "What do people do during the Mid-Autumn Festival?", "options": ["Enjoy the full moon and eat mooncakes with family", "Race dragon boats only", "Watch fireworks all night", "Exchange Christmas trees"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，中秋节赏满月、吃月饼；B 错误，赛龙舟是端午节；C 错误，看烟花主要是春节；D 正确，中秋赏月吃月饼是文中内容。"}, {"prompt": "How have festivals changed in recent years?", "options": ["People send greetings by phone and buy gifts online", "No one celebrates festivals anymore", "All traditional activities have disappeared", "Families never return home"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，近年人们用手机拜年、网购礼品；B 错误，人们仍在庆祝节日；C 错误，传统活动只是减少而非全部消失；D 错误，节日仍是家人回家的理由。"}, {"prompt": "What is Diwali?", "options": ["A festival of lights in India celebrating hope", "A Chinese food festival", "A Western Christmas tree", "A dragon boat race"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，排灯节是印度庆祝希望与新开始的光明节；A 错误，不是中国美食节；C 错误，不是西方圣诞树；D 错误，不是龙舟赛。"}, {"prompt": "What do festivals remind us of?", "options": ["Family, friendship, and hope for the future", "Only money and gifts", "Work and deadlines", "Competition and winning"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，节日提醒人们重视家庭、友谊与未来希望；B 错误，节日不只是钱与礼物；C 错误，未提工作与截止日期；D 错误，未提竞争与获胜。"}]}, {"title": "E-Books or Paper Books?", "text": "With the growth of smartphones and tablets, e-books have become popular among young readers. An e-book is a book in digital form that can be read on a screen. A single device can hold thousands of books, so readers do not need to carry heavy bags. E-books are often cheaper than paper books, and some are free. Readers can also change the font size, search for a word quickly, and read at night without a lamp. For students, e-books are convenient for study. They can highlight important sentences and take notes directly on the screen. However, many people still prefer paper books. They enjoy the feeling of turning pages and the smell of new paper. Reading a paper book can help people focus better because there are no messages or notifications to distract them. Research shows that people often remember more from paper books than from screens, especially when reading long texts. Paper books also do not need batteries or internet. They can be read anywhere, even when the power is off. They can also be shared easily. A reader can lend a paper book to a friend or keep it on a shelf for many years. The choice between e-books and paper books depends on personal needs. For travel, e-books are light and convenient. For deep reading at home, many people choose paper books. Some readers use both. They read e-books on the bus and paper books before bed. Libraries now offer both forms, so readers can choose what suits them best. In the future, both e-books and paper books will likely continue to exist. Technology changes how we read, but the pleasure of a good story remains the same, whether it is on a screen or on paper.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is one advantage of e-books?", "options": ["A device can hold thousands of books", "E-books are always more expensive", "E-books need heavy bags", "E-books cannot change font size"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，电子书通常更便宜；B 错误，电子书无需沉重的书包；C 正确，一台设备可存数千本书是电子书优点；D 错误，电子书可调整字体大小。"}, {"prompt": "Why do some people prefer paper books?", "options": ["They enjoy turning pages and can focus without notifications", "Paper books need batteries", "Paper books are harder to share", "Paper books always cost nothing"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，纸书无需电池；B 错误，纸书易于分享；C 错误，纸书并非免费；D 正确，喜欢翻页感觉、无通知干扰是偏爱纸书的原因。"}, {"prompt": "What does research show about remembering?", "options": ["People often remember more from paper books for long texts", "People remember nothing from paper books", "E-books always improve memory", "Screens never distract readers"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，研究表明长文阅读时纸书记忆效果常更好；B 错误，纸书并非让人什么都记不住；C 错误，电子书并非总能提高记忆；D 错误，屏幕会分散注意力。"}, {"prompt": "When are e-books especially convenient?", "options": ["For travel, because they are light", "Only when there is no electricity", "When readers want to lend books to friends", "When the power is off"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，旅行时电子书轻便；A 错误，断电时纸书更方便；C 错误，借书给朋友时纸书更方便；D 错误，断电时纸书可用。"}, {"prompt": "What is the author's conclusion?", "options": ["Both e-books and paper books will likely continue to exist", "Paper books will disappear tomorrow", "E-books are the only choice for everyone", "Reading is no longer important"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，作者认为两种书都将继续存在；B 错误，纸书不会明天消失；C 错误，电子书不是唯一选择；D 错误，阅读仍然重要。"}]}]}, {"no": 38, "passages": [{"title": "Eating Well on a Student Budget", "text": "Many students think healthy eating is expensive and difficult. In fact, it is possible to eat well even on a small budget. The key is to plan meals, choose simple foods, and cook more often. Vegetables, eggs, beans, and rice are cheap and nutritious. An egg provides protein, while beans provide fiber and vitamins. Seasonal vegetables are usually cheaper and fresher than out-of-season ones. Fast food may seem convenient, but it is often high in salt, sugar, and fat. Eating it every day can lead to weight gain and poor health. Cooking at home is usually healthier and cheaper. A student can cook a simple meal in twenty minutes: rice, a vegetable dish, and an egg or some tofu. Learning a few basic recipes is enough to start. Drinking water is also important. Many students drink sweet drinks instead of water. These drinks contain a lot of sugar and can harm teeth. Water is free in most places and is the best choice for thirst. Breakfast deserves special attention. Some students skip breakfast to save time or money. This is a bad habit. Without breakfast, students may feel tired and find it hard to concentrate in morning classes. A simple breakfast of bread, an egg, and milk can provide energy for the whole morning. Eating well also means eating regularly and not overeating. Students should avoid eating late at night, especially heavy snacks before bed. A regular eating schedule helps the body stay healthy. Healthy eating does not mean giving up all favorite foods. A student can still enjoy ice cream or fried chicken sometimes. The key is balance and moderation. With a little planning, students can eat well, stay healthy, and save money at the same time.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage on 'Eating Well on a Student Budget'?", "options": ["Healthy eating is possible on a student budget with planning", "Healthy food is always too expensive for students", "Students should eat fast food every day", "Cooking at home is always unhealthy"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，健康饮食并非学生负担不起；B 错误，不应每天吃快餐；C 正确，合理规划下学生也能吃得健康是主旨；D 错误，在家做饭通常更健康便宜。"}, {"prompt": "Which foods are described as cheap and nutritious?", "options": ["Vegetables, eggs, beans, and rice", "Only expensive imported fruit", "Sweet drinks and ice cream", "Fried chicken every day"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，蔬菜鸡蛋豆类米饭便宜有营养；B 错误，进口水果并非便宜选择；C 错误，甜饮料冰淇淋不健康；D 正确，蔬菜、鸡蛋、豆类和米饭便宜且营养。"}, {"prompt": "Why is fast food a problem if eaten daily?", "options": ["It is high in salt, sugar, and fat and can harm health", "It is always the cheapest choice", "It helps students concentrate better", "It contains no calories at all"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，快餐高盐高糖高脂、常吃有害健康；B 错误，快餐并非总是最便宜；C 错误，快餐无助集中注意力；D 错误，快餐热量很高。"}, {"prompt": "What does the passage say about breakfast?", "options": ["Skipping breakfast is a good way to save time", "Breakfast provides energy and helps concentration", "Breakfast is unnecessary for students", "Students should eat only sweet drinks for breakfast"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，早餐提供能量、有助集中注意力；A 错误，不吃早餐是坏习惯；C 错误，早餐对学生很重要；D 错误，不应只喝甜饮料当早餐。"}, {"prompt": "What does the author mean by balance and moderation?", "options": ["Students can enjoy favorite foods sometimes but not too much", "Students must never eat ice cream", "Students should overeat every night", "Students should eat only vegetables"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，平衡节制指偶尔享受喜爱食物但不过量；B 错误，并非绝不能吃冰淇淋；C 错误，不应每晚暴食；D 错误，不应只吃蔬菜。"}]}, {"title": "Protecting the Oceans", "text": "The oceans cover more than seventy percent of the earth's surface. They provide food, oxygen, and jobs for millions of people. They also help control the climate. However, the oceans are facing serious problems. Plastic pollution is one of the worst. Every year, millions of tons of plastic enter the sea. Plastic bags, bottles, and fishing nets can harm sea animals. Turtles may mistake plastic bags for food, and fish may become trapped in old nets. Tiny pieces of plastic can enter the food chain and affect human health. Overfishing is another problem. When too many fish are caught, fish populations fall quickly. Some species may disappear completely. This affects not only fishermen but also the whole ocean ecosystem. Climate change also harms the oceans. Warmer water damages coral reefs, which are home to many sea animals. Rising sea levels threaten coastal cities and islands. To protect the oceans, action is needed at many levels. Governments can make laws to reduce plastic use and control fishing. They can create marine protected areas where fishing is limited. Companies can reduce plastic packaging and develop better ways to recycle. Ordinary people can also help. Using reusable bags, refusing single-use plastic straws, and choosing seafood from sustainable sources are simple steps. Beach clean-up activities allow volunteers to remove rubbish and learn about ocean protection. Schools can teach students about the sea and why it matters. Protecting the oceans is not only about saving animals. It is about protecting our food, our climate, and our future. The ocean gives us so much; it is time for us to give something back. This experience also reminds readers that small daily choices can lead to large changes over time, and that understanding the topic well helps students make better decisions in their own lives and studies. With patience, practice, and support from teachers and friends, learners can turn new knowledge into real skills and use it with confidence in the future.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the main topic of the passage on 'Protecting the Oceans'?", "options": ["How to swim in the ocean", "The problems facing oceans and how to protect them", "Why people should stop eating fish", "The history of fishing boats"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，文章不是教游泳；B 错误，未说应停止吃鱼；C 正确，文章讲海洋面临的问题及保护方法；D 错误，未讲渔船历史。"}, {"prompt": "How does plastic harm sea animals?", "options": ["Turtles may eat plastic bags and fish may be trapped in nets", "Plastic makes sea water cleaner", "Plastic helps fish grow faster", "Plastic never enters the sea"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，海龟可能误食塑料袋、鱼会被旧网缠住；B 错误，塑料污染海水；C 错误，塑料无助鱼类生长；D 正确，塑料对海洋动物有害是文中内容。"}, {"prompt": "What is overfishing?", "options": ["Catching too many fish so populations fall quickly", "Catching only one fish a year", "Protecting all fish species", "Fishing only in rivers"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，过度捕捞指捕捞过多致鱼群锐减；B 错误，并非每年只捕一条；C 错误，过度捕捞不是保护鱼类；D 错误，不只在河流捕捞。"}, {"prompt": "What can governments do to protect oceans?", "options": ["Make laws to reduce plastic and create marine protected areas", "Encourage more plastic use", "Allow unlimited fishing everywhere", "Ignore climate change"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，政府可立法减塑、设立海洋保护区；A 错误，不应鼓励更多塑料使用；C 错误，不应允许无限捕捞；D 错误，不应忽视气候变化。"}, {"prompt": "What can ordinary people do?", "options": ["Use reusable bags and choose sustainable seafood", "Throw plastic into the sea", "Buy only single-use plastic", "Never join beach clean-ups"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，普通人可用可重复用袋、选可持续海产品；B 错误，不应向海里扔塑料；C 错误，不应只买一次性塑料；D 错误，可参加海滩清洁活动。"}]}]}, {"no": 39, "passages": [{"title": "Smartphones: Helpful or Harmful?", "text": "Smartphones have become part of almost every student's life. They are used for study, communication, entertainment, and payment. A student can look up a word, watch a lecture, or talk to a friend in another country with one small device. Smartphones make life easier in many ways. However, they also cause problems when used too much. One problem is distraction. Many students check their phones during class or while doing homework. A single message can break concentration, and it may take a long time to focus again. Some students spend hours on short videos or games every night and then feel tired the next day. Another problem is health. Looking at a small screen for a long time can cause eye strain and neck pain. Using a phone late at night can also affect sleep because the bright light keeps the brain awake. Social problems can appear too. Some students spend more time talking online than talking face to face. They may feel lonely even when they have many online friends. The key is not to give up smartphones completely but to use them wisely. Students can turn off notifications during study time and keep phones away from the bed at night. Setting a daily limit for entertainment apps can help. Parents and teachers can also guide students to use phones for learning, such as reading e-books or practicing English. Smartphones are tools. Like any tool, they can help or harm depending on how they are used. When students control their phones instead of being controlled by them, smartphones become a powerful aid for study and life. In the end, the lesson is clear: steady effort and thoughtful choices matter more than quick results, and students who keep learning from real examples will be better prepared for new challenges in school and in life. They can share what they learn with others and build good habits that last for many years.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage on 'Smartphones: Helpful or Harmful?'?", "options": ["Smartphones are useful tools but should be used wisely", "Smartphones should be banned for all students", "Smartphones have no benefits at all", "Students should use phones all night"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，文章未说应全面禁用手机；B 错误，手机有许多益处；C 正确，手机是有用工具、应明智使用是主旨；D 错误，不应在夜间一直用手机。"}, {"prompt": "How can a phone message affect study?", "options": ["It can break concentration and take time to refocus", "It always improves grades", "It makes homework disappear", "It helps students sleep better"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，一条消息会打断专注、需时间重新集中；B 错误，消息不会总能提高成绩；C 错误，消息不会让作业消失；D 正确，消息打断注意力是文中提到的问题。"}, {"prompt": "What health problem is mentioned?", "options": ["Eye strain and neck pain from long screen use", "Stronger eyesight from phones", "Better sleep from bright light", "No health effects at all"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，长时间看屏幕可致眼疲劳、颈痛；B 错误，手机不会增强视力；C 错误，亮光会影响睡眠；D 错误，手机确有健康影响。"}, {"prompt": "What suggestion is given for study time?", "options": ["Turn off notifications and keep phones away", "Check phones every minute", "Play games during class", "Use phones late at night in bed"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，建议学习时关闭通知、睡前远离手机；A 错误，不应每分钟查看手机；C 错误，不应在课堂玩游戏；D 错误，不应在床上深夜用手机。"}, {"prompt": "What does the author believe about smartphones?", "options": ["They are tools that can help or harm depending on use", "They control students completely and cannot be managed", "They are only for entertainment", "They should never be used for learning"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，手机是工具，用法决定利弊；B 错误，学生可以控制手机；C 错误，手机不只用于娱乐；D 错误，手机可用于学习。"}]}, {"title": "Community Service on Campus", "text": "Community service is volunteer work that helps the local community. At many universities, students take part in service activities outside the classroom. They may teach children, visit elderly people, clean public parks, or help at local events. These activities benefit both the community and the students themselves. For the community, student volunteers bring energy and fresh ideas. Children in poor areas can learn English or computer skills from university students. Elderly people who live alone feel happier when someone visits and talks with them. Parks become cleaner and safer when volunteers help maintain them. For students, community service is a chance to learn outside textbooks. They meet people from different backgrounds and understand real social problems. They learn to work in a team, solve problems, and take responsibility. A student who organizes a book donation learns how to plan and communicate with others. These experiences can also help students choose future careers. Some students discover they enjoy teaching or social work after volunteering. Many universities now encourage community service by giving credits or certificates. Some make it part of the curriculum. However, service should come from a sincere wish to help, not only from a desire for credits. Experts suggest that students choose service activities they truly care about. Even a few hours a month can make a difference. When students serve their community, they learn that learning is not only about personal success. It is also about using knowledge to help others and to make society better. This is one of the most valuable lessons of university life. Overall, this topic shows how everyday life and larger social questions are closely connected, and how careful thinking helps people choose wisely. When students pay attention, ask questions, and practise what they have learned, they can make progress step by step and enjoy the process of learning.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is community service?", "options": ["Volunteer work that helps the local community", "Paid work in a company", "Homework assigned by teachers", "A sports competition on campus"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，社区服务不是公司有偿工作；B 错误，不是教师布置的作业；C 正确，社区服务是帮助当地社区的志愿工作；D 错误，不是校园体育比赛。"}, {"prompt": "How do elderly people benefit from volunteers?", "options": ["They feel happier when someone visits and talks with them", "They are forced to work in parks", "They must teach English to students", "They lose all their friends"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，独居老人有人探访交谈会更开心；B 错误，老人不被迫在公园劳动；C 错误，老人不必教学生英语；D 正确，探访交谈让独居老人更开心。"}, {"prompt": "What can students learn from service?", "options": ["Teamwork, problem-solving, and responsibility", "How to avoid all social contact", "How to cheat on examinations", "How to ignore community problems"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，服务可学团队合作、解决问题与责任感；B 错误，服务增加社会接触；C 错误，服务不教作弊；D 错误，服务让人了解社会问题。"}, {"prompt": "Why do some universities give credits for service?", "options": ["To encourage students to take part in service", "To punish students who volunteer", "To stop students from studying", "To make service more expensive"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，大学给学分以鼓励学生参与服务；A 错误，给学分不是惩罚；C 错误，服务不阻止学习；D 错误，服务不会因此更贵。"}, {"prompt": "What does the author think service should come from?", "options": ["A sincere wish to help, not only a desire for credits", "Only a desire for certificates", "A wish to avoid classes", "A wish to earn money"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，服务应出于真心助人而非只为学分；B 错误，不应只为证书；C 错误，不应为逃课；D 错误，志愿服务无报酬。"}]}]}, {"no": 40, "passages": [{"title": "Preparing for a Job Interview", "text": "For many graduates, the first job interview is both exciting and stressful. Good preparation can make a big difference. The first step is to learn about the company. Candidates should visit the company website, read about its products and values, and understand what the job requires. This helps them answer questions clearly and show real interest. The second step is to prepare answers to common questions. Interviewers often ask candidates to introduce themselves, talk about their strengths and weaknesses, and explain why they want the job. Candidates should practice speaking clearly and giving examples from their study or part-time work. For example, instead of simply saying I am hardworking, a candidate can describe a project they finished on time. Appearance and manners also matter. Candidates should dress neatly, arrive ten minutes early, and greet the interviewer politely. During the interview, they should listen carefully, make eye contact, and avoid interrupting. It is also good to prepare a few questions to ask the interviewer, such as what training the company offers or what a typical day looks like. This shows curiosity and interest. After the interview, candidates can send a short thank-you message. This is polite and helps the interviewer remember them. If a candidate does not get the job, they should not feel hopeless. They can ask for feedback, learn from the experience, and prepare better next time. Every interview is practice for the next one. With preparation, confidence, and a positive attitude, graduates can turn a stressful interview into a chance to show their abilities and start their careers. This experience also reminds readers that small daily choices can lead to large changes over time, and that understanding the topic well helps students make better decisions in their own lives and studies. With patience, practice, and support from teachers and friends, learners can turn new knowledge into real skills and use it with confidence in the future.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the first step in preparing for an interview?", "options": ["Learn about the company and the job requirements", "Buy expensive clothes only", "Arrive late to show confidence", "Refuse to answer any questions"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，准备应先了解公司与岗位要求；B 错误，买贵衣服不是第一步；C 正确，了解公司及其要求是第一步；D 错误，不应拒绝回答问题。"}, {"prompt": "How should candidates talk about strengths?", "options": ["Give examples from study or part-time work", "Simply say a single word without explanation", "Talk only about weaknesses", "Never mention any experience"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，应结合学习或兼职经历举例说明；B 错误，只说一个词不够；C 错误，不应只谈缺点；D 正确，用实例说明优点是文中建议。"}, {"prompt": "What should candidates do during the interview?", "options": ["Listen carefully, make eye contact, and avoid interrupting", "Interrupt the interviewer often", "Look at the floor the whole time", "Answer questions without listening"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，面试时应认真倾听、眼神交流、不打断；B 错误，不应频繁打断；C 错误，不应一直低头；D 错误，应先倾听再回答。"}, {"prompt": "Why should candidates prepare questions to ask?", "options": ["To show curiosity and interest in the job", "To waste the interviewer's time", "To avoid talking about themselves", "To prove they know everything"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，准备提问可显示好奇心与兴趣；A 错误，提问不是浪费时间；C 错误，提问不是为回避自我介绍；D 错误，提问不是证明无所不知。"}, {"prompt": "What should candidates do if they do not get the job?", "options": ["Ask for feedback and prepare better next time", "Give up looking for any job", "Blame the interviewer publicly", "Never attend another interview"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，未被录用可请求反馈、下次准备更好；B 错误，不应放弃求职；C 错误，不应公开指责面试官；D 错误，不应再也不参加面试。"}]}, {"title": "Saving Water in Daily Life", "text": "Water is essential for life, yet many people waste it without thinking. In some parts of the world, clean water is already scarce. Even in places where water seems plentiful, saving water is important for the environment and for future generations. Small changes in daily life can save a large amount of water over time. In the bathroom, people can take shorter showers instead of long baths. Turning off the tap while brushing teeth can save several liters each time. Fixing a leaking tap quickly is also important, because a small drip can waste a great deal of water in a month. In the kitchen, water used to wash vegetables can be reused to water plants. Washing dishes in a basin rather than under running water also saves water. People should only run washing machines and dishwashers when they are full. In the garden, watering plants in the early morning or evening reduces evaporation. Choosing plants that need less water is another good idea. Schools and companies can help by installing water-saving taps and toilets and by teaching people about water conservation. Farmers also play a key role. Modern irrigation methods, such as drip irrigation, deliver water directly to plant roots and waste far less than traditional methods. Saving water does not mean living uncomfortably. It means using water wisely and avoiding waste. When everyone saves a little, the total amount is huge. A city where millions of people each save a few liters a day can save millions of liters in a year. Water is a shared resource. Protecting it today means our children will still have clean water tomorrow. In the end, the lesson is clear: steady effort and thoughtful choices matter more than quick results, and students who keep learning from real examples will be better prepared for new challenges in school and in life. They can share what they learn with others and build good habits that last for many years.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage on 'Saving Water in Daily Life'?", "options": ["Saving water in daily life is important and easy with small changes", "Water is unlimited and never needs saving", "Only farmers should save water", "Taking long baths saves water"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，水并非无限、需要节约；B 错误，不只农民应节水；C 正确，日常节水重要、小改变即可做到是主旨；D 错误，长时间泡澡浪费水。"}, {"prompt": "What can people do while brushing teeth?", "options": ["Turn off the tap to save several liters", "Leave the tap running all the time", "Use as much water as possible", "Brush teeth only once a month"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，刷牙时关掉水龙头可省数升水；B 错误，不应一直开着水龙头；C 错误，不应尽可能多用水；D 正确，刷牙关水是文中提到的节水方法。"}, {"prompt": "How can kitchen water be reused?", "options": ["Water used to wash vegetables can water plants", "Kitchen water should always be thrown away", "Vegetable water can never be reused", "Kitchen water is harmful to all plants"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，洗菜水可用来浇花；B 错误，不应总是倒掉；C 错误，洗菜水可以再利用；D 错误，洗菜水对植物并非有害。"}, {"prompt": "What is drip irrigation?", "options": ["A modern method delivering water directly to plant roots", "A way to waste more water", "A method that floods whole fields", "A type of shower for farmers"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，滴灌是把水直接送到植物根部的现代方法；A 错误，滴灌节约用水；C 错误，滴灌不是漫灌整片田；D 错误，滴灌不是淋浴。"}, {"prompt": "What does the author believe about saving water?", "options": ["When everyone saves a little, the total is huge", "Saving water makes life uncomfortable", "Only cities need to save water", "Saving water has no effect on the future"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，人人节水一点、总量巨大；B 错误，节水不意味着生活不适；C 错误，人人都应节水；D 错误，节水关系到未来。"}]}]}, {"no": 41, "passages": [{"title": "Learning Never Stops", "text": "Many people think learning ends when they leave school. In fact, learning continues throughout life. The world changes quickly. New technologies appear, jobs change, and new skills become necessary. People who keep learning can adapt to these changes and enjoy more opportunities. Lifelong learning takes many forms. Some people take evening courses, others learn online. A worker may learn a new computer program, a parent may learn a new language, and a retired person may learn painting or music. The internet makes learning easier than ever. Free courses, videos, and books are available to anyone with a phone or computer. Lifelong learning also keeps the mind active. Studies show that learning new skills can improve memory and delay age-related decline. It also gives people confidence and a sense of achievement. When a person learns to play an instrument or speak a new language, they feel proud of their progress. Of course, lifelong learning requires time and effort. Busy workers may find it hard to study after work. The key is to start small. Fifteen minutes a day is enough to make progress over time. Setting clear goals helps too. Instead of saying I want to learn English, a person can say I will learn ten new words a week. Employers also value lifelong learners. They prefer workers who are willing to learn new skills and adapt to change. In a fast-changing job market, the ability to learn may be more important than what a person already knows. Learning never stops because life never stops changing. People who keep learning stay curious, flexible, and ready for the future. Overall, this topic shows how everyday life and larger social questions are closely connected, and how careful thinking helps people choose wisely. When students pay attention, ask questions, and practise what they have learned, they can make progress step by step and enjoy the process of learning.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage on 'Learning Never Stops'?", "options": ["Learning continues throughout life and helps people adapt", "Learning ends when school ends", "Only young people need to learn", "Lifelong learning is useless for workers"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，学习并非毕业即结束；B 错误，不只年轻人需要学习；C 正确，终身学习帮助人们适应变化是主旨；D 错误，终身学习对工作者有用。"}, {"prompt": "What does the internet offer learners?", "options": ["Free courses, videos, and books for anyone", "Only expensive courses", "No learning materials at all", "Only games and entertainment"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，互联网提供免费课程、视频和书籍；B 错误，并非只有昂贵课程；C 错误，网上有大量学习资料；D 正确，互联网让免费课程视频书籍人人可得。"}, {"prompt": "How can lifelong learning help older people?", "options": ["It can improve memory and delay age-related decline", "It makes memory worse", "It stops people from feeling proud", "It prevents all learning"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，学习新技能可改善记忆、延缓衰退；B 错误，学习不会使记忆变差；C 错误，学习带来成就感；D 错误，学习不会阻止学习。"}, {"prompt": "What advice is given to busy workers?", "options": ["Start small with fifteen minutes a day and set clear goals", "Study ten hours every night", "Never set any goals", "Give up learning completely"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，建议从每天十五分钟开始、设定明确目标；A 错误，不必每晚学十小时；C 错误，应设定目标；D 错误，不应放弃学习。"}, {"prompt": "Why do employers value lifelong learners?", "options": ["They are willing to learn new skills and adapt to change", "They refuse to learn anything new", "They always want higher pay only", "They never change their jobs"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，雇主看重愿学新技能、适应变化的员工；B 错误，终身学习者乐于学习；C 错误，未说只想要高薪；D 错误，未说从不换工作。"}]}, {"title": "Protecting Wild Animals", "text": "Wild animals are an important part of nature. They keep ecosystems in balance. For example, birds eat insects that harm crops, and wolves control the number of deer. When a species disappears, the whole ecosystem can be affected. However, many wild animals are in danger. Habitat loss is the main reason. As cities and farms expand, forests are cut down and wetlands are filled. Animals lose their homes and food sources. Illegal hunting is another serious problem. Some animals are killed for their fur, horns, or meat. Pollution also harms wildlife. Chemicals in rivers can poison fish, and plastic in the sea can kill turtles and birds. To protect wild animals, many countries have created nature reserves. In these areas, hunting is banned and habitats are protected. China has built reserves for giant pandas, crested ibises, and other rare animals. These efforts have helped some species recover. Laws against illegal hunting have also become stricter. Ordinary people can help too. They should not buy products made from endangered animals. They can support wildlife organizations, visit nature reserves responsibly, and teach children to respect animals. Zoos can also play a role by protecting rare species and teaching visitors about wildlife. Protecting wild animals is not only about saving cute animals. It is about protecting the natural world that humans depend on for food, water, and clean air. When we protect animals, we protect ourselves and the future of the planet. This experience also reminds readers that small daily choices can lead to large changes over time, and that understanding the topic well helps students make better decisions in their own lives and studies. With patience, practice, and support from teachers and friends, learners can turn new knowledge into real skills and use it with confidence in the future.", "questions": [{"prompt": "Why are wild animals important?", "options": ["They keep ecosystems in balance", "They have no role in nature", "They only harm crops", "They are useless to humans"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，野生动物在自然中作用重要；B 错误，它们不只危害庄稼；C 正确，野生动物维持生态平衡；D 错误，它们对人类有用。"}, {"prompt": "What is the main reason wild animals are in danger?", "options": ["Habitat loss as cities and farms expand", "Too many nature reserves", "Animals have too much food", "People protect animals too much"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，栖息地丧失是主因；B 错误，自然保护区不是威胁；C 错误，动物并非食物过多；D 正确，城市农田扩张致栖息地丧失是主因。"}, {"prompt": "What have nature reserves done in China?", "options": ["They have helped some species like pandas recover", "They have increased illegal hunting", "They have destroyed all forests", "They have banned all visitors forever"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，保护区帮助大熊猫等物种恢复；B 错误，保护区减少非法狩猎；C 错误，保护区保护森林；D 错误，保护区可负责任参观。"}, {"prompt": "What can ordinary people do in connection with protecting wild animals?", "options": ["Buy products made from endangered animals", "Not buy such products and support wildlife organizations", "Hunt wild animals for fun", "Pollute rivers with chemicals"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，普通人应不买濒危动物制品、支持野生动物组织；A 错误，不应购买濒危制品；C 错误，不应为娱乐猎杀；D 错误，不应向河流排污。"}, {"prompt": "What is the author's view on protecting animals?", "options": ["Protecting animals also protects humans and the planet", "Protecting animals is only about cute animals", "Protecting animals harms humans", "Animals do not need any protection"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，保护动物也是保护人类与地球；B 错误，不只是为了可爱的动物；C 错误，保护动物不损害人类；D 错误，动物需要保护。"}]}]}, {"no": 42, "passages": [{"title": "Online Shopping: Convenience and Risks", "text": "Online shopping has become part of daily life for millions of people. With a phone, shoppers can buy clothes, books, food, and electronics without leaving home. They can compare prices from different shops, read reviews from other customers, and choose the best deal. During festivals, online shops offer large discounts, and parcels arrive at the door within a few days. For people who live far from big cities, online shopping is especially helpful because it brings more choices. However, online shopping also has risks. The biggest problem is that shoppers cannot touch or try products before buying. A dress may look beautiful in a photo but fit poorly in real life. The color may be different, or the quality may be lower than expected. Some sellers use false photos or fake reviews to attract buyers. Returning goods can also be troublesome and may cost extra money. Another risk is spending too much. Online shops are open all day and night, and it is easy to buy things on impulse. A shopper may buy clothes they do not need simply because they are on sale. Payment safety is also important. Shoppers should use trusted websites, check seller ratings, and never share bank passwords. To shop wisely, experts suggest making a list before shopping, comparing prices, reading reviews carefully, and checking return policies. It is also wise to wait a day before buying something expensive. Online shopping is convenient, but it works best when shoppers are careful and thoughtful. Used wisely, it saves time and money; used without control, it can lead to waste and disappointment. In the end, the lesson is clear: steady effort and thoughtful choices matter more than quick results, and students who keep learning from real examples will be better prepared for new challenges in school and in life. They can share what they learn with others and build good habits that last for many years.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is one advantage of online shopping?", "options": ["Shoppers can compare prices and read reviews", "Shoppers must travel to many shops", "Shoppers cannot find any choices", "Shoppers always pay higher prices"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，网购无需跑多家实体店；B 错误，网购选择更多；C 正确，可比价、看评价是网购优点；D 错误，网购常能找到更优惠价格。"}, {"prompt": "What is the biggest problem mentioned?", "options": ["Shoppers cannot touch or try products before buying", "Online shops close too early", "Parcels never arrive", "Online shops have no products"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，网购最大问题是无法触摸试用商品；B 错误，网店全天开放；C 错误，包裹通常几天内送达；D 正确，无法提前触摸试用是文中最大问题。"}, {"prompt": "Why do some shoppers spend too much online?", "options": ["It is easy to buy on impulse because shops are open all day", "Online shops never offer discounts", "Shoppers always make lists", "Online shopping is very slow"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，网店全天开放、易冲动消费；B 错误，网店常有折扣；C 错误，冲动购物者常不列清单；D 错误，网购很快捷。"}, {"prompt": "What advice is given about payment safety?", "options": ["Use trusted websites and never share bank passwords", "Share passwords with all sellers", "Click on every unknown link", "Use untrusted websites only"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，应使用可信网站、绝不分享银行密码；A 错误，不应与卖家分享密码；C 错误，不应点击未知链接；D 错误，不应只用不可信网站。"}, {"prompt": "What does the author suggest before buying something expensive?", "options": ["Wait a day before buying it", "Buy it immediately without thinking", "Never compare prices", "Ignore return policies"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，买贵重物品前建议等一天再决定；B 错误，不应不假思索立即购买；C 错误，应比较价格；D 错误，应查看退货政策。"}]}, {"title": "Managing Time in College", "text": "Time management is one of the most important skills for college students. Unlike high school, college gives students much more free time. They can choose when to study, when to rest, and when to join activities. Without good planning, time can disappear quickly. Many students find themselves busy all day but with little to show for it. They may spend hours on phones, chat with friends, or watch videos, and then rush to finish homework at the last minute. Good time management starts with clear goals. Students should know what they want to achieve each week. Making a to-do list each morning helps. The list should include study tasks, deadlines, and personal activities. Important and urgent tasks should come first. A popular method is to break large tasks into small steps. Writing a long essay seems difficult, but writing one paragraph a day is manageable. Short breaks are also important. Studying for fifty minutes and resting for ten minutes can help students stay focused. It is also important to say no sometimes. Joining too many clubs or activities can leave no time for study. Students should choose activities they truly enjoy and can manage. Technology can help or harm time management. Calendar apps and reminders can help students remember deadlines. At the same time, phones can be a major distraction. Turning off notifications during study time is a simple but effective step. Time management is not about filling every minute with work. It is about using time wisely so that study, rest, and fun all have their place. Students who manage time well feel less stressed and achieve more. They also have more time to enjoy college life. Overall, this topic shows how everyday life and larger social questions are closely connected, and how careful thinking helps people choose wisely. When students pay attention, ask questions, and practise what they have learned, they can make progress step by step and enjoy the process of learning.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage on 'Managing Time in College'?", "options": ["Time management helps college students use free time wisely", "College students have no free time at all", "Time management means working every minute", "Students should join as many clubs as possible"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，大学生有较多自由时间；B 错误，时间管理不是每分钟都工作；C 正确，时间管理帮助大学生明智利用自由时间是主旨；D 错误，不应加入过多社团。"}, {"prompt": "What problem do many students face?", "options": ["They are busy all day but achieve little", "They have too little to do", "They finish all homework too early", "They never use phones"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，许多学生整天忙却收效甚微；B 错误，他们并非无事可做；C 错误，他们常拖到最后赶作业；D 正确，忙碌却少有成果是文中问题。"}, {"prompt": "What method is suggested for large tasks?", "options": ["Break them into small steps", "Do everything in one night", "Ignore the task completely", "Wait until the deadline passes"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，建议把大任务拆成小步骤；B 错误，不应一夜完成所有；C 错误，不应完全忽视任务；D 错误，不应等截止日期过后。"}, {"prompt": "What study pattern is recommended?", "options": ["Study fifty minutes and rest ten minutes", "Study without any breaks", "Rest all day and study at night", "Study only five minutes a day"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，建议学五十分钟、休息十分钟；A 错误，不间断学习效果差；C 错误，不应整天休息夜间学习；D 错误，每天只学五分钟不够。"}, {"prompt": "What does the author think time management is about?", "options": ["Using time wisely so study, rest, and fun all have a place", "Filling every minute with work", "Avoiding all rest and fun", "Working without any plan"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，时间管理是明智用时、兼顾学习休息娱乐；B 错误，不是每分钟都工作；C 错误，不应回避休息娱乐；D 错误，时间管理需要计划。"}]}]}, {"no": 43, "passages": [{"title": "Public Libraries for Everyone", "text": "Public libraries are free places where anyone can read, study, and learn. They have books, newspapers, computers, and quiet rooms. In many cities, libraries also offer courses, lectures, and activities for children. For students who cannot afford many books, libraries are especially valuable. They can borrow books for free and use computers to do homework. Libraries also help people who are looking for jobs. They provide information about job openings and help with writing resumes. For older people, libraries are places to read newspapers, meet friends, and learn new skills. In recent years, libraries have changed. Many now offer e-books, online courses, and digital resources. Visitors can borrow an e-book without visiting the library in person. Some libraries have makerspaces where people can use 3D printers and learn about technology. Others have children's corners with toys and story time. However, libraries face challenges. Some people think libraries are old-fashioned because everything is online. Funding is also a problem in some places. Libraries need money to buy new books, maintain buildings, and pay staff. When budgets are cut, opening hours may be reduced. Despite these problems, libraries remain important. They are one of the few public places where people can stay for free without buying anything. They promote reading, learning, and equality. A child from a poor family can read the same books as a child from a rich family. To keep libraries alive, communities should use them, support them, and tell others about their value. A city with a good library is a city that values knowledge and its people. This experience also reminds readers that small daily choices can lead to large changes over time, and that understanding the topic well helps students make better decisions in their own lives and studies. With patience, practice, and support from teachers and friends, learners can turn new knowledge into real skills and use it with confidence in the future.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the main topic of the passage on 'Public Libraries for Everyone'?", "options": ["The value and changes of public libraries", "How to build a new shopping mall", "Why libraries should be closed", "The history of newspapers only"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，文章不是讲建购物中心；B 错误，未说应关闭图书馆；C 正确，文章讲公共图书馆的价值与变化；D 错误，不只讲报纸历史。"}, {"prompt": "How do libraries help job seekers?", "options": ["They provide job information and help with resumes", "They offer jobs to everyone immediately", "They stop people from working", "They charge high fees for help"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，图书馆提供招聘信息、帮助写简历；B 错误，并非立即给所有人工作；C 错误，图书馆不阻止工作；D 正确，提供求职信息与简历帮助是文中内容。"}, {"prompt": "What new service do some libraries offer?", "options": ["Makerspaces with 3D printers", "Only old newspapers", "No computers at all", "Paid entry for all visitors"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，一些图书馆设有创客空间、提供3D打印机；B 错误，图书馆不只提供旧报纸；C 错误，图书馆提供电脑；D 错误，图书馆免费开放。"}, {"prompt": "What challenge do libraries face?", "options": ["Funding problems and reduced opening hours", "Too many visitors every day", "Too many new books", "Everyone loves libraries too much"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，资金问题可导致开放时间缩短；A 错误，访客多不是主要挑战；C 错误，新书多不是问题；D 错误，人们喜爱图书馆不是挑战。"}, {"prompt": "Why are libraries important for equality?", "options": ["A poor child can read the same books as a rich child", "Only rich people can use libraries", "Libraries charge different prices", "Libraries refuse poor children"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，穷孩子与富孩子可读同样的书；B 错误，图书馆对所有人免费；C 错误，图书馆不按身份收费；D 错误，图书馆不拒绝穷孩子。"}]}, {"title": "Green Buildings for a Better City", "text": "Buildings use a large amount of energy for heating, cooling, and lighting. They also produce a great deal of waste. Green buildings are designed to reduce this impact on the environment. They use less energy, less water, and fewer harmful materials. A green building may have solar panels on its roof, good insulation to keep heat in winter and out in summer, and windows that let in natural light. This reduces the need for air conditioning and electric lights. Water-saving taps and toilets are also common. Some green buildings collect rainwater and reuse it for watering plants or flushing toilets. Green buildings also use materials that are safe and can be recycled. Wood from sustainable forests, recycled steel, and low-pollution paints are popular choices. Indoor air quality is another focus. Plants inside the building and good ventilation systems help people breathe more easily and feel more comfortable. Studies show that people who work or study in green buildings often feel healthier and more productive. They have fewer headaches and can concentrate better. Green buildings may cost more to build at first, but they save money over time through lower energy and water bills. Governments in many countries now encourage green buildings by giving rewards or setting standards. In China, many new schools, offices, and homes are built to green standards. As cities grow, green buildings will play a bigger role. They show that it is possible to live comfortably while protecting the environment. A green building is not just a building; it is a step toward a greener future. In the end, the lesson is clear: steady effort and thoughtful choices matter more than quick results, and students who keep learning from real examples will be better prepared for new challenges in school and in life. They can share what they learn with others and build good habits that last for many years.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is a green building?", "options": ["A building designed to use less energy and water", "A building painted green on the outside", "A building with no windows", "A building that uses more electricity"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，绿色建筑不只是外墙涂绿；B 错误，绿色建筑有窗户；C 正确，绿色建筑旨在少用能源与水；D 错误，绿色建筑用电更少。"}, {"prompt": "How do green buildings save energy?", "options": ["Through solar panels, insulation, and natural light", "By keeping all lights on all day", "By using more air conditioning", "By removing all windows"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，太阳能板、隔热与自然采光可节能；B 错误，不应整天开灯；C 错误，不应多用空调；D 正确，太阳能、隔热、自然光是节能方式。"}, {"prompt": "What water feature is mentioned?", "options": ["Rainwater is collected and reused", "Water is wasted on purpose", "No water is used at all", "Toilets use more water than before"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，一些绿色建筑收集雨水再利用；B 错误，不应故意浪费水；C 错误，建筑仍需用水；D 错误，节水马桶用水更少。"}, {"prompt": "What benefit do people in green buildings experience?", "options": ["They often feel healthier and more productive", "They always feel sick", "They cannot concentrate at all", "They have more headaches"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，绿色建筑内的人常更健康、更高效；A 错误，他们并非总感不适；C 错误，他们专注力更好；D 错误，他们头痛更少。"}, {"prompt": "Why may green buildings cost more at first?", "options": ["Because of green design and materials, but they save money over time", "Because they use cheap materials", "Because they have no solar panels", "Because they waste more energy"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，绿色设计材料初期成本高、长期省钱；B 错误，绿色建筑不用廉价有害材料；C 错误，绿色建筑常有太阳能板；D 错误，绿色建筑节能而非浪费。"}]}]}, {"no": 44, "passages": [{"title": "Learning a Foreign Language", "text": "Learning a foreign language opens a door to a new world. It allows people to talk with others, understand different cultures, and enjoy books and films in their original language. For students, a foreign language can also bring better job opportunities. Many companies prefer employees who can speak English or another language. However, learning a language takes time and patience. Many students give up because they find it difficult. They memorize words for a test and then forget them quickly. They are afraid of making mistakes when speaking, so they stay silent in class. To learn well, students need regular practice. Listening to songs, watching films, and reading short articles can help. Speaking is especially important. Even if a student makes mistakes, speaking helps build confidence. Language partners are useful. A student can talk with a native speaker online and learn natural expressions. Writing a short diary in the foreign language every day is another good habit. It helps students use new words in real sentences. Grammar is important, but it should not be the only focus. Communication is the real goal. A student who can express ideas clearly, even with small grammar mistakes, is doing well. Technology offers many tools for language learning. Apps can remind students to review words, correct pronunciation, and provide practice tests. However, apps alone are not enough. Real conversations and real reading matter most. Learning a language is like learning to swim. Reading about swimming will not help; only practice in the water works. With daily practice, patience, and courage to speak, anyone can make steady progress and enjoy the journey. Overall, this topic shows how everyday life and larger social questions are closely connected, and how careful thinking helps people choose wisely. When students pay attention, ask questions, and practise what they have learned, they can make progress step by step and enjoy the process of learning.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage on 'Learning a Foreign Language'?", "options": ["Learning a foreign language takes regular practice and brings many benefits", "Learning a language is impossible for students", "Only grammar matters in language learning", "Students should never speak a foreign language"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，学习语言并非不可能；B 错误，不只语法重要；C 正确，学外语需坚持练习、益处很多是主旨；D 错误，应勇于开口说外语。"}, {"prompt": "Why do many students give up learning a language?", "options": ["They find it difficult and are afraid of mistakes", "They learn too quickly", "They have too much free time", "They speak perfectly from the start"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，觉得困难、怕犯错是放弃原因；B 错误，并非学得太快；C 错误，空闲太多不是原因；D 正确，怕犯错、觉得难是放弃的原因。"}, {"prompt": "What does the passage suggest about speaking?", "options": ["Speaking builds confidence even with mistakes", "Students should stay silent in class", "Speaking is unimportant", "Mistakes mean failure"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，开口说即使犯错也有助建立信心；B 错误，不应课堂沉默；C 错误，口语很重要；D 错误，犯错不等于失败。"}, {"prompt": "What is a good daily habit suggested?", "options": ["Writing a short diary in the foreign language", "Memorizing words only for tests", "Never reading any articles", "Avoiding all native speakers"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，建议每天用外语写简短日记；A 错误，只为考试背单词易忘；C 错误，应多读短文；D 错误，应与母语者交流。"}, {"prompt": "What does the swimming comparison mean?", "options": ["Language learning needs real practice, not just reading about it", "Swimming is the only useful sport", "Language learning is exactly like swimming", "Students should learn to swim first"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，游泳比喻指语言学习需要真实练习；B 错误，游泳不是唯一有用运动；C 错误，比喻不是说两者完全相同；D 错误，不必先学游泳。"}]}, {"title": "Exploring Space: Why It Matters", "text": "Humans have always looked up at the sky and wondered what is beyond the earth. Space exploration began in the twentieth century, and it has brought many benefits. Satellites help us predict weather, navigate with GPS, and communicate across the world. Without satellites, modern life would be very different. Space research has also led to new technologies. Materials developed for spacecraft are now used in daily life, from water filters to medical equipment. Studying other planets helps scientists understand the earth better. By learning about Mars, for example, scientists can learn more about climate change on earth. Space exploration also inspires young people. When children watch a rocket launch or see photos of distant planets, they become interested in science and engineering. Many future scientists and engineers start with this curiosity. However, space exploration is expensive. Some people argue that the money should be spent on solving problems on earth, such as poverty, disease, and pollution. They ask whether exploring Mars is more important than helping people who are hungry today. This is a fair question. Supporters of space exploration say it is not a choice between space and earth. Many space technologies help solve earth problems, such as monitoring forests, oceans, and crops. International cooperation in space can also bring countries together. China has made great progress in space. Its space station, lunar missions, and Mars rover show what careful planning and hard work can achieve. In the future, space exploration will likely continue. Humans may return to the Moon and travel farther. Whether we go to space or stay on earth, the spirit of exploration pushes us to learn, to dream, and to improve our lives. This experience also reminds readers that small daily choices can lead to large changes over time, and that understanding the topic well helps students make better decisions in their own lives and studies. With patience, practice, and support from teachers and friends, learners can turn new knowledge into real skills and use it with confidence in the future.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the main topic of the passage on 'Exploring Space: Why It Matters'?", "options": ["Why space exploration matters and its benefits and debates", "How to build a rocket at home", "Why satellites should be banned", "The history of the Moon only"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，文章不是教在家造火箭；B 错误，未说应禁用卫星；C 正确，文章讲太空探索的意义、益处与争议；D 错误，不只讲月球历史。"}, {"prompt": "How do satellites help modern life?", "options": ["They help predict weather, navigate, and communicate", "They make weather worse", "They stop all communication", "They are useless for navigation"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，卫星帮助预报天气、导航与通信；B 错误，卫星不使天气变差；C 错误，卫星促进通信；D 正确，卫星预报天气、导航、通信是文中益处。"}, {"prompt": "What criticism of space exploration is mentioned?", "options": ["It is expensive and money could help earth problems", "It is too cheap to matter", "It has no effect on technology", "It never inspires young people"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，有人认为太空探索昂贵、钱应用于解决地球问题；B 错误，太空探索并不便宜；C 错误，太空技术带来许多新技术；D 错误，太空探索激励年轻人。"}, {"prompt": "What do supporters say about space technology?", "options": ["Many space technologies help solve earth problems", "Space technology harms all earth problems", "Space technology is useless on earth", "Space exploration should stop all cooperation"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，支持者说许多太空技术有助于解决地球问题；A 错误，太空技术并非损害地球问题；C 错误，太空技术在地球上有用；D 错误，太空合作可增进国家间合作。"}, {"prompt": "What has China achieved in space?", "options": ["A space station, lunar missions, and a Mars rover", "No space missions at all", "Only weather satellites", "A rocket that never launched"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，中国建成空间站、探月任务与火星车；B 错误，中国有许多太空任务；C 错误，成就不限于气象卫星；D 错误，中国火箭已成功发射。"}]}]}, {"no": 45, "passages": [{"title": "Bike Sharing in the City", "text": "Bike sharing has become a popular way to travel in many Chinese cities. With a phone app, people can find a shared bicycle nearby, unlock it by scanning a code, and ride to their destination. When they arrive, they lock the bike and pay a small fee. It is cheap, convenient, and good for health. For short trips, bike sharing is often faster than driving because riders can avoid traffic jams. It is also better for the environment because bicycles produce no pollution. Many students use shared bikes to go to class, visit friends, or explore the city. Bike sharing also helps solve the last mile problem. People can ride a bike from a subway station to their home or office, a distance that is too far to walk but too short to drive. However, bike sharing has problems. In some cities, too many bikes are placed in the same place, blocking sidewalks and entrances. Some bikes are damaged or left in rivers and parks. Companies must spend money to repair bikes and move them to places where they are needed. Users also need to follow rules. They should park bikes in proper places, ride safely, and not ride on sidewalks where it is forbidden. Cities have started to manage bike sharing better. They limit the number of bikes, set parking areas, and fine companies that do not maintain their bikes. Technology also helps. GPS shows where bikes are, and apps remind users to park correctly. Bike sharing shows how technology can change daily travel. When used responsibly, it makes cities cleaner, healthier, and more convenient. A small bicycle can make a big difference to urban life. In the end, the lesson is clear: steady effort and thoughtful choices matter more than quick results, and students who keep learning from real examples will be better prepared for new challenges in school and in life. They can share what they learn with others and build good habits that last for many years.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage on 'Bike Sharing in the City'?", "options": ["Bike sharing is convenient and green but needs good management", "Bike sharing should be banned in all cities", "Bikes are slower than cars for all trips", "Bike sharing has no problems at all"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，文章未说应全面禁止共享单车；B 错误，短途骑行常比开车快；C 正确，共享单车方便环保、但需良好管理是主旨；D 错误，共享单车确有问题。"}, {"prompt": "How do people use a shared bike?", "options": ["Find it by app, scan to unlock, ride, then lock and pay", "Buy the bike and take it home", "Ride without any payment", "Call a driver to ride for them"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，用App找车、扫码解锁、骑行后锁车付费是使用方法；B 错误，无需买下单车；C 错误，骑行需付少量费用；D 正确，App找车扫码解锁骑行锁车付费是流程。"}, {"prompt": "What is the last mile problem?", "options": ["The distance from a station to home too far to walk but too short to drive", "The longest bike race in the world", "A problem with bike tires only", "The distance between two cities"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，最后一公里指车站到家步行太远、开车太近的距离；B 错误，不是自行车比赛；C 错误，不只是轮胎问题；D 错误，不是两城市间距离。"}, {"prompt": "What problem does bike sharing cause in some cities?", "options": ["Too many bikes block sidewalks and entrances", "There are never enough bikes", "Bikes never get damaged", "No one uses shared bikes"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，过多单车会堵塞人行道与入口；A 错误，有些城市单车过多；C 错误，单车会被损坏；D 错误，许多人使用共享单车。"}, {"prompt": "What are cities doing to manage bike sharing?", "options": ["Limiting bike numbers and setting parking areas", "Banning all phone apps", "Encouraging users to park anywhere", "Ignoring damaged bikes"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，城市限制单车数量、设置停放区；B 错误，未禁止手机应用；C 错误，不鼓励随意停放；D 错误，城市要求公司维护单车。"}]}, {"title": "Caring for Older People", "text": "As people live longer, many countries face an aging population. In China, the number of older people is growing quickly. Caring for them has become an important social issue. Older people need both physical care and emotional support. Many live alone because their children work in other cities. They may feel lonely and need someone to talk with. Simple visits, phone calls, and help with shopping can make a big difference. Health care is another key need. Older people often have chronic diseases such as high blood pressure or diabetes. They need regular check-ups and medicine. Communities can help by building health centers nearby and organizing free check-ups. Exercise programs for older people, such as tai chi and walking groups, help them stay healthy and meet friends. Technology can also help older people. Smartphones allow them to talk with family by video, order food, and call for help in an emergency. However, many older people find technology difficult. Young people can help by teaching them patiently. Society should also respect older people and value their experience. They have knowledge, stories, and skills that younger generations can learn from. Some older people volunteer to teach children, share traditional crafts, or help in community activities. Caring for older people is not only the duty of families. It is the duty of the whole society. When we care for older people today, we are also building a society that will care for us when we grow old. Respect and care for the elderly are signs of a caring and civilized society. Overall, this topic shows how everyday life and larger social questions are closely connected, and how careful thinking helps people choose wisely. When students pay attention, ask questions, and practise what they have learned, they can make progress step by step and enjoy the process of learning.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the main topic of the passage on 'Caring for Older People'?", "options": ["Caring for older people in an aging society", "How to make young people rich", "Why older people should live alone", "The history of tai chi only"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，文章不是教年轻人致富；B 错误，未说老人应独居；C 正确，文章讲老龄化社会中照顾老年人；D 错误，不只讲太极历史。"}, {"prompt": "Why do many older people feel lonely?", "options": ["They live alone because children work in other cities", "They have too many visitors", "They never want to talk to anyone", "They live with all their children"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，子女在外地工作、老人独居易感孤独；B 错误，访客太多不是原因；C 错误，老人需要有人交谈；D 正确，子女异地工作、老人独居是孤独原因。"}, {"prompt": "What health need is mentioned?", "options": ["Regular check-ups and medicine for chronic diseases", "No need for any medicine", "Only emergency care", "No need to exercise"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，老人需定期体检与慢性病药物；B 错误，老人需要药物；C 错误，不只应急 care；D 错误，锻炼对老人有益。"}, {"prompt": "How can young people help older people with technology?", "options": ["Teach them patiently to use smartphones", "Refuse to help them", "Take away all their phones", "Ignore their questions"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，年轻人可耐心教老人用智能手机；A 错误，不应拒绝帮助；C 错误，不应拿走手机；D 错误，不应忽视提问。"}, {"prompt": "What does the author believe about caring for the elderly?", "options": ["It is the duty of the whole society", "It is only the duty of hospitals", "It is unnecessary in modern society", "It should be done only by strangers"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，照顾老人是全社会的责任；B 错误，不只是医院的责任；C 错误，照顾老人很有必要；D 错误，不应只由陌生人照顾。"}]}]}, {"no": 46, "passages": [{"title": "Protecting Your Privacy Online", "text": "Every day, people share a great deal of information online. They post photos, share locations, and enter personal details on websites and apps. This information can be useful, but it can also be misused. Protecting online privacy has become an important skill for everyone, especially for students. One risk is that personal information can be stolen. If a person uses a weak password or the same password for many accounts, hackers may break into their accounts. They can steal money, send false messages, or use personal photos in harmful ways. Another risk is that companies collect data about users. They track what people search for, what they buy, and where they go. This data is often used for advertising, but users may not know how much is collected. To protect privacy, people should use strong, different passwords for each important account. They should turn on two-step verification when possible. This means that even if someone knows the password, they still need a code from the phone to log in. People should also think before sharing. They should not post their home address, phone number, or travel plans in public. Checking privacy settings on social media is important. Users can choose who can see their posts and photos. It is also wise to be careful with free Wi-Fi in public places. Such networks may not be safe, and hackers can steal information through them. Students should learn about privacy at school and at home. Teachers and parents can explain why it matters and how to stay safe. Privacy is not about hiding everything. It is about controlling who can see your information and how it is used. In a digital world, protecting privacy is protecting yourself.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage on 'Protecting Your Privacy Online'?", "options": ["Protecting online privacy is an important skill", "Sharing everything online is always safe", "Passwords are unnecessary for accounts", "Companies never collect user data"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，网上分享并非总是安全；B 错误，密码对账户很重要；C 正确，保护网上隐私是重要技能是主旨；D 错误，公司会收集用户数据。"}, {"prompt": "What risk comes from weak passwords?", "options": ["Hackers may break into accounts and steal money or photos", "Accounts become safer", "Hackers cannot access anything", "Passwords become stronger"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，弱密码可让黑客入侵账户、窃取钱财照片；B 错误，弱密码使账户更不安全；C 错误，黑客可进入账户；D 正确，弱密码或重复密码易被黑客破解。"}, {"prompt": "What is two-step verification?", "options": ["A method requiring a phone code in addition to a password", "A way to share passwords with friends", "A method to remove all passwords", "A way to post photos publicly"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，两步验证指除密码外还需手机验证码；B 错误，不是与朋友分享密码；C 错误，不是取消所有密码；D 错误，不是公开晒照片的方法。"}, {"prompt": "What should people avoid sharing in public?", "options": ["Home address, phone number, and travel plans", "Their favorite books", "General news articles", "Public holiday greetings"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，不应在公开场合分享家庭住址、电话与旅行计划；A 错误，分享喜欢的书无妨；C 错误，分享新闻无妨；D 错误，节日问候无妨。"}, {"prompt": "Why should people be careful with free public Wi-Fi?", "options": ["Such networks may not be safe and hackers can steal information", "Free Wi-Fi is always completely safe", "Free Wi-Fi never connects to the internet", "Public Wi-Fi always protects privacy"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，公共免费Wi-Fi可能不安全、黑客可窃取信息；B 错误，免费Wi-Fi并非绝对安全；C 错误，免费Wi-Fi可以上网；D 错误，公共Wi-Fi不一定保护隐私。"}]}, {"title": "Visiting a Local Market", "text": "In many Chinese cities, local markets are lively places full of color, sound, and smell. Early in the morning, vendors set up stalls with fresh vegetables, fruits, meat, and fish. Shoppers walk between stalls, choose what they need, and bargain for a better price. For many older people, going to the market is a daily habit and a social activity. They meet neighbors, talk with vendors, and learn about seasonal foods. Local markets offer food that is often fresher and cheaper than in supermarkets. Vegetables are picked in the morning and sold the same day. Shoppers can ask vendors how to cook a vegetable or which fruit is sweetest. This personal service is hard to find in a large supermarket. Markets also support local farmers and small businesses. When people buy from a market stall, their money goes directly to the farmer or vendor. This helps local families earn a living. However, markets face competition from supermarkets and online shopping. Supermarkets are cleaner, cooler in summer, and open longer hours. Online shopping delivers food to the door. Young people, who are busy with work and study, often prefer these convenient choices. Some markets have changed to attract younger customers. They have improved cleanliness, added payment by phone, and opened food stalls where people can eat local snacks. Some markets have become tourist attractions, where visitors can taste local food and buy souvenirs. Local markets are more than places to buy food. They are part of community life and local culture. Protecting them means protecting a way of life that has existed for hundreds of years and still brings people together today. This experience also reminds readers that small daily choices can lead to large changes over time, and that understanding the topic well helps students make better decisions in their own lives and studies. With patience, practice, and support from teachers and friends, learners can turn new knowledge into real skills and use it with confidence in the future.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the main topic of the passage on 'Visiting a Local Market'?", "options": ["Local markets and their value in community life", "How to open a supermarket", "Why markets should be closed", "The history of online shopping only"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，文章不是教开超市；B 错误，未说应关闭市场；C 正确，文章讲地方市场及其社区价值；D 错误，不只讲网购历史。"}, {"prompt": "Why do older people enjoy going to markets?", "options": ["It is a daily habit and a social activity", "They are forced to go every day", "They dislike all vendors", "They never buy any food"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，去市场是日常习惯与社交活动；B 错误，无人强迫他们每天去；C 错误，他们与摊主交谈愉快；D 正确，去市场是习惯与社交活动。"}, {"prompt": "What advantage do markets have over supermarkets?", "options": ["Food is often fresher and cheaper with personal service", "Markets are always cleaner", "Markets open longer hours", "Markets sell only imported food"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，市场食品更新鲜便宜、服务更贴心；B 错误，超市更干净；C 错误，超市营业时间更长；D 错误，市场多售本地食品。"}, {"prompt": "How have some markets changed?", "options": ["They improved cleanliness and added phone payment", "They stopped selling all food", "They banned all young customers", "They removed all food stalls"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，一些市场改善卫生、增加手机支付；A 错误，未停止售卖食品；C 错误，未禁止年轻顾客；D 错误，市场增加了小吃摊。"}, {"prompt": "What does the author think markets are?", "options": ["More than places to buy food; part of community life and culture", "Only places for old people", "Useless in modern life", "The same as supermarkets"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，市场不只是买菜处、更是社区生活与文化的一部分；B 错误，市场不只属于老人；C 错误，市场在现代仍有价值；D 错误，市场与超市不同。"}]}]}, {"no": 47, "passages": [{"title": "Studying Abroad: A New Experience", "text": "Every year, many Chinese students choose to study abroad. They go to the United States, the United Kingdom, Australia, and other countries. Studying abroad offers many benefits. Students can improve their foreign language skills by using the language every day. They can learn about different cultures, meet people from around the world, and become more independent. Living alone in a foreign country teaches students to cook, manage money, and solve problems by themselves. These experiences help them grow quickly. However, studying abroad also brings challenges. The first challenge is language. Even students with good test scores may find it hard to understand lectures or join discussions at first. Homesickness is another common problem. Students may miss their families, friends, and familiar food. The cost is also high. Tuition, housing, and living expenses can be a heavy burden for families. Cultural differences can cause misunderstandings. For example, classroom styles, food habits, and social rules may be different. Students need time to adapt. To prepare, students should improve their language skills before leaving, learn about the culture of the country, and plan their finances carefully. Once abroad, they should join clubs, make local friends, and ask for help when needed. Universities abroad often have support services for international students. Studying abroad is not right for everyone. Some students may prefer to study at home and travel later. For those who go abroad, the experience can be life-changing. It broadens their view of the world and gives them confidence to face new challenges in the future. In the end, the lesson is clear: steady effort and thoughtful choices matter more than quick results, and students who keep learning from real examples will be better prepared for new challenges in school and in life. They can share what they learn with others and build good habits that last for many years.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the main benefit of studying abroad?", "options": ["Students improve language skills and become more independent", "Students never need to study", "Students forget their own culture completely", "Students always spend less money"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，留学仍需学习；B 错误，留学不是忘却本国文化；C 正确，留学可提高语言能力、变得更独立；D 错误，留学花费很高。"}, {"prompt": "What is a common challenge for students abroad?", "options": ["Homesickness and missing family and familiar food", "Never feeling lonely", "Always understanding every lecture easily", "Having no expenses at all"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，思乡、想念家人与熟悉食物是常见挑战；B 错误，留学生常感孤独；C 错误，初到时听课可能困难；D 正确，思乡是文中提到的挑战。"}, {"prompt": "What cultural challenge is mentioned?", "options": ["Classroom styles, food habits, and social rules may differ", "All cultures are exactly the same", "Students never meet local people", "Culture has no effect on study"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，课堂风格、饮食习惯与社交规则可能不同；B 错误，各国文化并不相同；C 错误，留学生会结识当地人；D 错误，文化影响学习生活。"}, {"prompt": "What should students do before leaving?", "options": ["Improve language, learn about culture, and plan finances", "Ignore all preparation", "Spend all money at once", "Avoid learning the language"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，出国前应提高语言、了解文化、规划财务；A 错误，不应忽视准备；C 错误，不应一次花光钱；D 错误，应学习语言。"}, {"prompt": "What does the author think about studying abroad?", "options": ["It can be life-changing and broaden views", "It is right for every student", "It has no challenges at all", "It always makes students unhappy"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，留学可改变人生、开阔视野；B 错误，留学并非适合每个人；C 错误，留学确有挑战；D 错误，留学并非总让人不开心。"}]}, {"title": "Air Pollution and City Life", "text": "Air pollution is a serious problem in many large cities. Cars, factories, and coal burning produce smoke and harmful gases. On bad days, the sky turns grey, and people can smell the pollution in the air. Air pollution harms health. It can cause coughs, breathing problems, and eye irritation. Children and older people are especially vulnerable. Long-term exposure can increase the risk of serious diseases. It also harms the environment. Polluted air can damage plants and buildings. To reduce air pollution, cities have taken many steps. They have limited the number of cars on certain days, encouraged public transport, and closed or moved polluting factories. Many cities now use electric buses and promote clean energy. Factories must install filters to reduce emissions. Ordinary people can help too. They can walk, cycle, or take a bus instead of driving. They can save electricity and avoid burning rubbish. Planting trees also helps because trees absorb harmful gases and produce oxygen. On days with heavy pollution, people should wear masks, avoid outdoor exercise, and keep windows closed. Schools may cancel outdoor activities to protect children. Technology helps monitor air quality. Phone apps show the air quality index, so people can decide whether to go outside. The government publishes air quality data every day. In recent years, air quality in many Chinese cities has improved. Blue skies appear more often than before. This shows that action works. Clean air is not a luxury; it is a basic need for health and a good life. Protecting the air means protecting ourselves and future generations. Overall, this topic shows how everyday life and larger social questions are closely connected, and how careful thinking helps people choose wisely. When students pay attention, ask questions, and practise what they have learned, they can make progress step by step and enjoy the process of learning.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What causes air pollution in cities?", "options": ["Cars, factories, and coal burning", "Planting too many trees", "Using electric buses", "Walking and cycling"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，植树有助于净化空气；B 错误，电动公交减少污染；C 正确，汽车、工厂与燃煤是污染来源；D 错误，步行骑行减少污染。"}, {"prompt": "Who is especially vulnerable to air pollution?", "options": ["Children and older people", "Only factory workers", "Only drivers", "Healthy young adults only"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，儿童与老人尤其易受影响；B 错误，不只工厂工人易受影响；C 错误，不只司机易受影响；D 正确，儿童与老人尤其脆弱。"}, {"prompt": "What have cities done to reduce pollution?", "options": ["Limited cars, encouraged public transport, and moved factories", "Increased coal burning", "Removed all buses", "Banned all trees"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，城市限行、鼓励公交、搬迁污染工厂；B 错误，不应增加燃煤；C 错误，不应取消公交；D 错误，不应禁止植树。"}, {"prompt": "What can ordinary people do in connection with air pollution and city life?", "options": ["Walk, cycle, or take a bus instead of driving", "Burn rubbish in the open air", "Use more electricity wastefully", "Cut down all trees"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，普通人可步行骑行乘公交代替开车；A 错误，不应露天焚烧垃圾；C 错误，不应浪费电力；D 错误，不应砍伐树木。"}, {"prompt": "What does the author conclude about clean air?", "options": ["It is a basic need for health and a good life", "It is a luxury only for rich people", "It is unimportant for cities", "It will never improve"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，清洁空气是健康与美好生活的基本需要；B 错误，清洁空气不是奢侈品；C 错误，清洁空气对城市重要；D 错误，空气质量已在改善。"}]}]}, {"no": 48, "passages": [{"title": "Creative Thinking in Daily Life", "text": "Creative thinking is often thought to belong only to artists and inventors. In fact, everyone can be creative in daily life. Creative thinking means looking at problems in new ways and finding different solutions. It is not about being strange; it is about being open-minded and curious. A student who finds a new way to memorize words is being creative. A worker who improves a work process is also being creative. Creative thinking can be developed. One way is to ask questions. Instead of accepting the first answer, people can ask why and what if. Another way is to connect different ideas. A good idea often comes from combining two old ideas in a new way. For example, a phone combined with a camera created the smartphone camera that we use every day. Reading widely, traveling, and talking with different people can provide new ideas. Taking breaks also helps. When the mind rests, it can make new connections. Many people get their best ideas while walking or taking a shower. Schools should encourage creative thinking, not only memorization. Teachers can give open questions with more than one correct answer. Students can work in groups, discuss ideas, and try different methods. Mistakes should be seen as part of learning, not as failure. Creative thinking is important for the future. Many jobs will change because of technology. Workers who can think creatively will adapt better and find new opportunities. Creativity also makes life more interesting. It helps people solve problems, enjoy learning, and express themselves. Everyone has creative ability. The key is to practice it every day, stay curious, and not be afraid to try new things. This experience also reminds readers that small daily choices can lead to large changes over time, and that understanding the topic well helps students make better decisions in their own lives and studies. With patience, practice, and support from teachers and friends, learners can turn new knowledge into real skills and use it with confidence in the future.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is creative thinking according to the passage?", "options": ["Looking at problems in new ways and finding different solutions", "Only painting beautiful pictures", "Memorizing facts without thinking", "Copying others exactly"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，创造性思维不只属于画家；B 错误，不是死记硬背；C 正确，创造性思维指换角度看问题、找不同解法；D 错误，不是照抄他人。"}, {"prompt": "How can people develop creative thinking?", "options": ["By asking questions and connecting different ideas", "By never asking why", "By avoiding all new experiences", "By accepting the first answer always"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，提问与联结不同想法可培养创造力；B 错误，不应回避新体验；C 错误，不应总接受第一个答案；D 正确，提问并联结想法是文中方法。"}, {"prompt": "What example is given of combining ideas?", "options": ["A phone combined with a camera", "A book combined with a chair", "A car combined with a house", "A shoe combined with a hat"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，手机与相机结合产生智能手机相机；B 错误，未提书与椅子结合；C 错误，未提车与房子结合；D 错误，未提鞋与帽子结合。"}, {"prompt": "Why should schools encourage creative thinking?", "options": ["Because many jobs will change and creative workers adapt better", "Because memorization is the only skill needed", "Because students should never make mistakes", "Because creativity is useless for work"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，未来许多工作会变化、具创造力者更易适应；A 错误，记忆不是唯一技能；C 错误，犯错是学习的一部分；D 错误，创造力对工作有用。"}, {"prompt": "What does the author suggest about mistakes?", "options": ["They should be seen as part of learning, not failure", "They should be punished severely", "They mean a person is not creative", "They should never happen"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，错误应视为学习的一部分而非失败；B 错误，不应严厉惩罚错误；C 错误，犯错不代表没有创造力；D 错误，错误难免会发生。"}]}, {"title": "Forests: The Lungs of the Earth", "text": "Forests are often called the lungs of the earth because they produce oxygen and absorb carbon dioxide. They are home to more than half of the world's land animals and plants. Forests provide wood, food, medicine, and clean water. They also protect soil from erosion and help control floods. However, forests are disappearing quickly. Every year, large areas of forest are cut down for farming, housing, and timber. In some places, forests are burned to clear land. This destroys habitats and releases a large amount of carbon dioxide into the air, which worsens climate change. When forests disappear, animals lose their homes. Some species may become extinct. Soil becomes loose and is easily washed away by rain. Rivers may become dirty, and floods may become more frequent. Protecting forests is essential. Governments can create national parks and nature reserves where logging is banned. They can make laws to punish illegal logging. Planting trees is another important action. Many countries have started large tree-planting programs. In China, millions of trees are planted every year to stop deserts from expanding and to improve the environment. Ordinary people can help by using less paper, recycling, and buying wood products from sustainable sources. They can also support organizations that protect forests. Schools can teach children about the value of forests and organize tree-planting activities. Forests are not only important for nature; they are important for human life. Without forests, the air would be dirtier, the climate would be hotter, and many animals would disappear. Protecting forests today means protecting the future of the earth and of ourselves. In the end, the lesson is clear: steady effort and thoughtful choices matter more than quick results, and students who keep learning from real examples will be better prepared for new challenges in school and in life. They can share what they learn with others and build good habits that last for many years.", "questions": [{"prompt": "Why are forests called the lungs of the earth?", "options": ["Because they produce oxygen and absorb carbon dioxide", "Because they look like human lungs", "Because they need air to breathe", "Because they are only found in hospitals"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，森林被称为地球之肺因其产氧吸碳；B 错误，并非因外形像肺；C 正确，森林产氧并吸收二氧化碳是得名原因；D 错误，森林不只存在于医院。"}, {"prompt": "What happens when forests are cut down?", "options": ["Animals lose homes and carbon dioxide is released", "Animals gain more homes", "Climate change improves immediately", "Soil becomes stronger"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，砍伐使动物失去家园、释放二氧化碳；B 错误，动物不会获得更多家园；C 错误，砍伐加剧气候变化；D 正确，砍伐致动物失栖、释放二氧化碳。"}, {"prompt": "What can governments do to protect forests?", "options": ["Create parks and reserves and punish illegal logging", "Encourage more logging everywhere", "Burn more forests to clear land", "Remove all nature reserves"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，政府可建公园保护区、惩处非法采伐；B 错误，不应鼓励更多采伐；C 错误，不应焚烧森林开荒；D 错误，不应取消保护区。"}, {"prompt": "What tree-planting effort is mentioned in China?", "options": ["Millions of trees are planted every year", "No trees are planted at all", "Only one tree is planted a year", "Trees are planted only in cities"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，中国每年种植数百万棵树；A 错误，并非完全不种树；C 错误，不只种一棵树；D 错误，植树不限于城市。"}, {"prompt": "What can ordinary people do in connection with forests: the lungs of the earth?", "options": ["Use less paper, recycle, and buy sustainable wood", "Use more paper wastefully", "Buy wood from illegal sources", "Never plant any trees"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，普通人可少用纸、回收、买可持续木材；B 错误，不应浪费纸张；C 错误，不应买非法木材；D 错误，可参与植树活动。"}]}]}, {"no": 49, "passages": [{"title": "Healthy Aging: Growing Old Well", "text": "People are living longer than ever before. Healthy aging means staying healthy, active, and happy as we grow older. It is not only about living a long life but also about living a good life. Healthy aging starts early. Habits formed in youth affect health in old age. Eating well, exercising regularly, and not smoking are important at every age. Exercise is especially helpful for older people. Walking, tai chi, and swimming can keep muscles strong and joints flexible. Exercise also improves balance, which reduces the risk of falls. Even light exercise every day is better than no exercise. Mental health is also important. Older people should stay socially active. Talking with family and friends, joining clubs, and learning new skills can prevent loneliness and keep the mind sharp. Learning to use a smartphone or playing chess are good ways to stay mentally active. Regular health check-ups help detect problems early. High blood pressure, diabetes, and other diseases can be managed better if found early. Older people should follow doctors' advice and take medicine correctly. Society also has a role. Communities should provide safe parks, accessible transport, and activities for older people. Families should spend time with older relatives and listen to their needs. Respect for older people is part of healthy aging. Growing old is a natural part of life. It should not be feared. With good habits, social support, and a positive attitude, people can enjoy their later years. Healthy aging is not only for older people; it is a goal for everyone, at every stage of life. Overall, this topic shows how everyday life and larger social questions are closely connected, and how careful thinking helps people choose wisely. When students pay attention, ask questions, and practise what they have learned, they can make progress step by step and enjoy the process of learning.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What does healthy aging mean?", "options": ["Staying healthy, active, and happy as we grow older", "Living a long life without any happiness", "Only living as long as possible", "Avoiding all exercise"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，健康老龄化不只是长寿、也要生活得好；B 错误，不只是活得尽可能久；C 正确，健康老龄化指年老时保持健康、活跃与快乐；D 错误，应坚持锻炼。"}, {"prompt": "What exercise is helpful for older people?", "options": ["Walking, tai chi, and swimming", "Only running marathons", "Never moving at all", "Lifting very heavy weights daily"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，散步太极游泳对老人有益；B 错误，不必只跑马拉松；C 错误，不应完全不动；D 正确，散步、太极、游泳有助于老人健康。"}, {"prompt": "How can older people stay mentally active?", "options": ["By staying socially active and learning new skills", "By staying alone all the time", "By never talking to anyone", "By avoiding all learning"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，保持社交、学习新技能可保持思维活跃；B 错误，长期独处不利心理健康；C 错误，应与人交谈；D 错误，应持续学习。"}, {"prompt": "Why are regular check-ups important?", "options": ["They help detect problems early", "They make diseases worse", "They are useless for older people", "They replace all medicine"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，定期体检有助于及早发现问题；A 错误，体检不会加重疾病；C 错误，体检对老人有用；D 错误，体检不能替代药物。"}, {"prompt": "What role should society play?", "options": ["Provide safe parks, transport, and activities for older people", "Ignore older people's needs", "Remove all parks", "Stop families from visiting elders"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，社会应提供安全公园、便利交通与活动；B 错误，不应忽视老人需求；C 错误，不应拆除公园；D 错误，不应阻止家人探望老人。"}]}, {"title": "Electric Bikes on the Road", "text": "Electric bikes, or e-bikes, have become very popular in Chinese cities and towns. They are cheaper than cars, faster than ordinary bicycles, and easy to park. Many people use e-bikes to go to work, take children to school, or deliver food. For short trips, they are a convenient choice. E-bikes also produce no exhaust fumes, so they are better for the environment than petrol vehicles. However, e-bikes also cause safety problems. Some riders go too fast, run red lights, or ride on sidewalks. This is dangerous for riders and for pedestrians. Accidents involving e-bikes happen often, especially at busy crossings. Another problem is charging. Some people charge e-bike batteries inside their homes or in stairways. If a battery catches fire, it can cause serious damage. Fires caused by charging have happened in several cities. To improve safety, cities have made new rules. Riders must wear helmets, follow traffic lights, and ride in proper lanes. E-bikes must be registered, and their speed is limited. Charging stations have been built in many communities so people do not need to charge batteries at home. Riders also need to learn traffic rules. Many cities offer safety training for e-bike riders. Parents should teach children not to ride e-bikes until they are old enough and have learned the rules. E-bikes are a useful form of transport, but safety must come first. When riders follow rules, wear helmets, and charge safely, e-bikes can be both convenient and safe for everyone on the road. This experience also reminds readers that small daily choices can lead to large changes over time, and that understanding the topic well helps students make better decisions in their own lives and studies. With patience, practice, and support from teachers and friends, learners can turn new knowledge into real skills and use it with confidence in the future.", "questions": [{"prompt": "Why are e-bikes popular?", "options": ["They are cheaper than cars and faster than ordinary bikes", "They are more expensive than cars", "They are difficult to park", "They produce a lot of exhaust fumes"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，电动自行车比汽车便宜；B 错误，电动自行车易于停放；C 正确，比汽车便宜、比普通自行车快是受欢迎的原因；D 错误，电动自行车无尾气排放。"}, {"prompt": "What safety problem is mentioned?", "options": ["Some riders go too fast and run red lights", "All riders always follow rules", "E-bikes never cause accidents", "Pedestrians are never in danger"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，有些骑手超速闯红灯是安全问题；B 错误，并非所有骑手都守规；C 错误，电动自行车事故时有发生；D 正确，超速闯红灯是文中提到的安全问题。"}, {"prompt": "What is dangerous about charging e-bikes at home?", "options": ["A battery may catch fire and cause serious damage", "Charging at home is always safe", "Batteries never catch fire", "Charging at home saves all lives"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，在家充电电池可能起火、造成严重损害；B 错误，在家充电并非总是安全；C 错误，电池可能起火；D 错误，在家充电并不保证安全。"}, {"prompt": "What new rules have cities made?", "options": ["Riders must wear helmets and follow traffic lights", "Riders can ignore all traffic lights", "E-bikes need no registration", "E-bikes can go at any speed"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，城市要求骑手戴头盔、遵守红绿灯；A 错误，不应无视红绿灯；C 错误，电动自行车须登记；D 错误，电动自行车限速。"}, {"prompt": "What does the author believe about e-bikes?", "options": ["They can be convenient and safe if riders follow rules", "They should be banned completely", "Safety does not matter for e-bikes", "Children should ride e-bikes without learning rules"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，遵守规则时电动自行车既方便又安全；B 错误，未说应全面禁止；C 错误，安全至关重要；D 错误，儿童须到适龄并学好规则才能骑行。"}]}]}, {"no": 50, "passages": [{"title": "Campus Clubs and Student Life", "text": "Campus clubs are an important part of university life. There are clubs for sports, music, dance, photography, debate, and volunteering. Students join clubs to pursue interests, make friends, and learn new skills. A student who joins a photography club can learn to use a camera and take part in exhibitions. A student in a debate club can improve speaking and thinking skills. Clubs also help students relax after study. After a long day of classes, playing badminton or singing in a choir can refresh the mind. Clubs create a sense of belonging. New students who join a club quickly meet people with similar interests. They feel less lonely and more connected to campus life. Clubs also teach teamwork and leadership. Organizing an event requires planning, cooperation, and problem-solving. A club leader learns how to manage a team and communicate with different people. These skills are useful for future work. However, joining too many clubs can be a problem. Students may have no time for study if they spend every evening in club activities. Experts suggest joining one or two clubs that truly interest them. Quality is more important than quantity. Universities support clubs by providing rooms, funding, and guidance. Some clubs become famous for their performances or competitions. They bring honor to the university and attract new students. Campus clubs make university life richer and more colorful. They are places where students discover interests, build friendships, and grow into confident young people. For many graduates, memories of club activities are among the happiest memories of their university years. In the end, the lesson is clear: steady effort and thoughtful choices matter more than quick results, and students who keep learning from real examples will be better prepared for new challenges in school and in life. They can share what they learn with others and build good habits that last for many years.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What is the main idea of the passage on 'Campus Clubs and Student Life'?", "options": ["Campus clubs enrich student life and teach useful skills", "Clubs are useless for students", "Students should join as many clubs as possible", "Clubs only waste study time"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，社团对学生有用；B 错误，不应加入过多社团；C 正确，校园社团丰富学生生活、教授实用技能是主旨；D 错误，社团不只是浪费学习时间。"}, {"prompt": "What can a student learn in a debate club?", "options": ["Speaking and thinking skills", "How to play football only", "How to avoid all discussions", "How to sing in a choir"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，辩论社可提高演讲与思维能力；B 错误，辩论社不是只教踢足球；C 错误，辩论社鼓励讨论；D 正确，辩论社提高口才与思维是文中内容。"}, {"prompt": "How do clubs help new students?", "options": ["They meet people with similar interests and feel less lonely", "They make new students feel more isolated", "They prevent new students from studying", "They force new students to stay alone"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，社团帮助新生结识志同道合者、减少孤独；B 错误，社团不会使新生更孤立；C 错误，社团不阻止学习；D 错误，社团不强迫新生独处。"}, {"prompt": "What advice is given about joining clubs?", "options": ["Join one or two clubs that truly interest them", "Join every club on campus", "Join clubs they dislike", "Never join any club"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，建议加入一两个真正感兴趣的社团；A 错误，不应加入所有社团；C 错误，不应加入不喜欢的社团；D 错误，不应一个社团都不加入。"}, {"prompt": "What do clubs teach besides interests?", "options": ["Teamwork and leadership skills", "How to avoid all responsibility", "How to work alone always", "How to ignore other people"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，社团教授团队合作与领导力；B 错误，社团培养责任感；C 错误，社团强调合作；D 错误，社团鼓励与人沟通。"}]}, {"title": "Sustainable Fashion: Dressing with Care", "text": "Fashion is a big industry, but it also causes serious environmental problems. Making clothes uses a large amount of water, energy, and chemicals. Many clothes are worn only a few times and then thrown away. In landfills, they take a long time to break down and can release harmful substances. Fast fashion encourages people to buy new clothes often and cheaply. This leads to overproduction and waste. Sustainable fashion offers a different choice. It means buying fewer clothes, choosing better quality, and wearing them for a longer time. A well-made coat that lasts five years is better for the environment than five cheap coats that are thrown away quickly. Sustainable fashion also means choosing clothes made from natural or recycled materials. Organic cotton uses less water and fewer chemicals than ordinary cotton. Clothes made from recycled plastic bottles or old clothes reduce waste. Second-hand shopping is another part of sustainable fashion. Buying used clothes is cheaper and gives old clothes a new life. Many young people now enjoy visiting second-hand shops and finding unique items. Repairing clothes is also important. Sewing a button or fixing a tear can make a garment last much longer. Some brands now offer repair services or take back old clothes for recycling. Consumers can make a difference by thinking before buying. They can ask: Do I really need this? Will I wear it many times? Can I repair it if it breaks? Choosing sustainable fashion does not mean giving up style. It means dressing with care for the planet and for the people who make our clothes. Overall, this topic shows how everyday life and larger social questions are closely connected, and how careful thinking helps people choose wisely. When students pay attention, ask questions, and practise what they have learned, they can make progress step by step and enjoy the process of learning.", "questions": [{"prompt": "What problem does fast fashion cause?", "options": ["Overproduction and waste from frequent cheap buying", "Less waste and fewer clothes", "Cleaner water and air", "No environmental impact"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "正确答案为 C。A 错误，快时尚导致浪费而非减少浪费；B 错误，快时尚污染水与空气；C 正确，快时尚导致过度生产与浪费；D 错误，快时尚确有环境影响。"}, {"prompt": "What does sustainable fashion mean?", "options": ["Buying fewer, better-quality clothes and wearing them longer", "Buying as many cheap clothes as possible", "Throwing away clothes after one wear", "Never wearing the same coat twice"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "正确答案为 D。A 错误，可持续时尚指少买、买好、穿得久；B 错误，不应尽可能多买廉价衣服；C 错误，不应穿一次就扔；D 正确，少买优质衣物、穿更久是可持续时尚。"}, {"prompt": "What material choice is more sustainable?", "options": ["Organic cotton using less water and fewer chemicals", "Ordinary cotton using more chemicals", "Clothes that cannot be recycled", "Materials that harm the environment"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，有机棉用水少、化学品少更可持续；B 错误，普通棉用更多化学品；C 错误，不可回收衣物不可持续；D 错误，有害环境材料不可持续。"}, {"prompt": "What is second-hand shopping?", "options": ["Buying used clothes to give them a new life", "Buying only brand-new expensive clothes", "Throwing away all old clothes", "Refusing to wear any used clothes"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "正确答案为 B。B 正确，二手购物指购买旧衣、赋予新生命；A 错误，不是只买全新昂贵衣物；C 错误，不是丢弃所有旧衣；D 错误，不应拒绝穿二手衣。"}, {"prompt": "What should consumers ask before buying?", "options": ["Do I really need this and will I wear it many times?", "Should I buy ten of the same item?", "Should I throw it away tomorrow?", "Should I ignore quality completely?"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "正确答案为 A。A 正确，购买前应问是否真的需要、会穿多次；B 错误，不应一次买十件相同衣物；C 错误，不应明天就扔掉；D 错误，不应忽视质量。"}]}]}];
